const studyBankDataScience = [
    { q: "A model scores 99% accuracy on its training data but only 70% on new data. What is this gap most likely a sign of?", a: ["Overfitting to noise in the training data", "Underfitting due to an overly simple model", "High bias from excessive regularization", "Data leakage from the test set into training"], correct: "Overfitting to noise in the training data", explanation: "A model that memorizes training data, noise included, scores high on training but generalizes poorly. Underfitting or heavy regularization would hurt training accuracy too, and leakage would inflate test scores rather than lower them." },
    { q: "Why do practitioners hold out a test set that the model never sees during training?", a: ["To estimate performance on unseen data", "To shrink training time on large datasets", "To balance class proportions for training", "To let the model see more varied examples"], correct: "To estimate performance on unseen data", explanation: "The held-out test set gives an honest estimate of how well the model generalizes. It isn't there to speed up training or rebalance classes, and the model never trains on it." },
    { q: "SQL, the standard language for working with relational databases, stands for what?", a: ["Structured Query Language", "Sequential Query Language", "Standard Query Language", "Structured Question Language"], correct: "Structured Query Language", explanation: "SQL stands for Structured Query Language. 'Standard Query Language' is a common mix-up, probably because SQL is an ANSI/ISO standard." },
    { q: "What is the median of the dataset 3, 8, 6, 2, 10, 4?", a: ["5", "5.5", "4", "6"], correct: "5", explanation: "Sorted: 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 10. With an even count, the median is the average of the two middle values, (4 + 6) / 2 = 5. 5.5 is the mean, not the median." },
    { q: "A study reports p = 0.03 using a significance level of α = 0.05. Which interpretation is correct?", a: ["The result is significant at the 5% level", "There is a 3% chance the null is true", "There is a 97% chance the effect is real", "The observed effect is large and important"], correct: "The result is significant at the 5% level", explanation: "Since p < α, the result is statistically significant. A p-value is the probability of data at least this extreme if the null were true. It is not the probability that the null is true, and it says nothing about effect size." },
    { q: "Which chart is the standard choice for showing the shape of a single continuous variable's distribution?", a: ["Histogram", "Bar chart", "Pie chart", "Scatter plot"], correct: "Histogram", explanation: "A histogram bins a continuous variable and shows how many values fall in each bin, which reveals shape, center and spread. Bar charts compare categories, and scatter plots relate two variables." },
    { q: "Turning a raw timestamp column into new 'hour of day' and 'is weekend' columns before training is an example of what?", a: ["Feature engineering", "Hyperparameter tuning", "Data normalization", "Dimensionality reduction"], correct: "Feature engineering", explanation: "Feature engineering means creating or transforming input variables so a model can learn more easily. Normalization only rescales existing values, and dimensionality reduction removes features rather than deriving new ones." },
    { q: "If two variables have a Pearson correlation of −1, which statement is true?", a: ["All points lie exactly on a downward-sloping line", "The two variables are completely unrelated", "Y drops by exactly 1 unit whenever X rises by 1", "Points trend downward with moderate scatter around a line"], correct: "All points lie exactly on a downward-sloping line", explanation: "r = −1 is a perfect negative linear relationship: every point falls on a line with negative slope. The slope can be any negative number, not necessarily −1." },
    { q: "Which Python library provides the DataFrame, the main tool most data scientists use for tabular data wrangling?", a: ["NumPy", "Pandas", "SciPy", "Matplotlib"], correct: "Pandas", explanation: "Pandas provides the DataFrame and Series types for loading, cleaning and reshaping tabular data. NumPy supplies the arrays underneath but has no labeled DataFrame." },
    { q: "In gradient descent, which direction does each update move the model's parameters?", a: ["Opposite the gradient of the loss", "Along the gradient of the loss", "Toward the mean of the training data", "Perpendicular to the gradient of the loss"], correct: "Opposite the gradient of the loss", explanation: "The gradient points in the direction of steepest increase, so gradient descent steps the opposite way (the negative gradient) to lower the loss. Moving along the gradient would be gradient ascent." },
    { q: "What is the VC dimension of linear classifiers (hyperplanes with a bias term) in n-dimensional space?", a: ["n", "n + 1", "2n", "2ⁿ"], correct: "n + 1", explanation: "A hyperplane with a bias can shatter n + 1 points in general position but not n + 2. In 2D, for example, a line can shatter any 3 non-collinear points but no set of 4." },
    { q: "In Bayes' theorem, the posterior equals likelihood × prior divided by a normalizing term. What is that denominator called?", a: ["The prior probability", "The likelihood function", "The marginal likelihood", "The posterior probability"], correct: "The marginal likelihood", explanation: "The denominator is the marginal likelihood, also called the evidence: the probability of the data averaged over all hypotheses. It makes the posterior sum (or integrate) to 1." },
    { q: "Under Mercer's theorem, what property must a symmetric kernel have for it to equal an inner product in some feature space?", a: ["Positive semi-definiteness", "Differentiability everywhere", "Translation invariance", "Values bounded in [0, 1]"], correct: "Positive semi-definiteness", explanation: "Mercer's condition requires the kernel to be positive semi-definite. That is what lets SVMs use it as an implicit dot product. Many valid kernels, such as polynomial kernels, are neither translation-invariant nor bounded." },
    { q: "Which property does the Kullback-Leibler divergence D(P‖Q) actually have?", a: ["It is never negative", "It is symmetric in P and Q", "It obeys the triangle inequality", "It is bounded above by 1"], correct: "It is never negative", explanation: "KL divergence is ≥ 0, with equality only when P = Q (Gibbs' inequality). It is not symmetric, fails the triangle inequality, and can be arbitrarily large, so it is not a true distance metric." },
    { q: "At a point where the gradient is zero, which Hessian condition is sufficient to guarantee a strict local minimum?", a: ["Positive definite", "Negative definite", "Indefinite", "Positive semi-definite"], correct: "Positive definite", explanation: "A positive definite Hessian means the function curves upward in every direction, which confirms a strict local minimum. Semi-definite is inconclusive, negative definite gives a maximum, and indefinite gives a saddle point." },
    { q: "Why do variational autoencoders maximize the ELBO instead of the data log-likelihood directly?", a: ["The marginal likelihood is intractable to compute", "The decoder's log-likelihood is non-differentiable", "Maximizing the ELBO makes the KL term symmetric", "The ELBO stops the encoder from overfitting"], correct: "The marginal likelihood is intractable to compute", explanation: "Computing log p(x) requires integrating over all latent variables, which is intractable. The ELBO is a tractable lower bound on it, so pushing the ELBO up pushes the likelihood up too." },
    { q: "Which equation expresses a state's value as the immediate reward plus the discounted value of the next state?", a: ["Bellman equation", "Policy gradient theorem", "Fokker-Planck equation", "Wiener-Hopf equation"], correct: "Bellman equation", explanation: "The Bellman equation breaks value into reward now plus γ times the value of the next state. That recursion underlies value iteration and Q-learning. The policy gradient theorem gives gradients of expected return instead." },
    { q: "Compared with Ridge (L2), what distinctive effect does Lasso (L1) regularization have on a linear model's coefficients?", a: ["It drives some coefficients exactly to zero", "It shrinks all coefficients by the same factor", "It keeps every coefficient strictly nonzero", "It enlarges coefficients of correlated features"], correct: "It drives some coefficients exactly to zero", explanation: "Lasso produces sparse models: some coefficients become exactly zero, so it doubles as feature selection. Ridge shrinks coefficients toward zero but almost never makes them exactly zero." },
    { q: "From a Bayesian view, L2 (Ridge) regularization is equivalent to MAP estimation with what prior on the weights?", a: ["Uniform prior", "Laplace prior", "Gaussian prior", "Cauchy prior"], correct: "Gaussian prior", explanation: "The negative log of a zero-mean Gaussian prior is proportional to the squared weights, which is exactly the L2 penalty. A Laplace prior gives the L1 (Lasso) penalty instead." },
    { q: "As you keep adding trees to a Random Forest, what happens to its bias and variance?", a: ["Bias stays about the same; variance falls", "Bias falls; variance drops all the way to zero", "Bias rises; variance falls as trees are added", "Bias falls; variance stays about the same"], correct: "Bias stays about the same; variance falls", explanation: "Averaging more trees reduces variance, but the bias is that of the individual trees and does not change. Variance levels off above zero because the trees remain correlated." },
    { q: "A classifier has precision 0.5 and recall 1.0. What is its F1 score?", a: ["≈0.67", "0.75", "≈0.71", "0.50"], correct: "≈0.67", explanation: "F1 is the harmonic mean: 2 × 0.5 × 1.0 / (0.5 + 1.0) = 1/1.5 ≈ 0.67. 0.75 is the arithmetic mean and ≈0.71 is the geometric mean. The harmonic mean pulls F1 toward the lower of the two values." },
    { q: "On a heavily imbalanced dataset, why can ROC-AUC look much better than PR-AUC for the same model?", a: ["Many true negatives keep the FPR low", "It ignores the true positive rate", "It heavily overweights false negatives", "It requires calibrated probabilities"], correct: "Many true negatives keep the FPR low", explanation: "FPR = FP / (all negatives). With a huge negative class, even many false positives barely raise FPR, so the ROC curve looks good. Precision, which PR-AUC uses, exposes those false positives." },
    { q: "In encoder-decoder (seq2seq) RNN models, what problem was the attention mechanism introduced to fix?", a: ["The fixed-length context vector bottleneck", "Exploding gradients in the decoder's recurrent layers", "The lack of word-order information", "Out-of-vocabulary words in the input"], correct: "The fixed-length context vector bottleneck", explanation: "Without attention, the whole input sentence is squeezed into one fixed-size vector, which hurts on long sentences. Attention lets the decoder look back at every encoder state at each output step." },
    { q: "In Transformers, why are the query-key dot products divided by √dₖ before the softmax?", a: ["To keep large dot products from saturating softmax", "To reduce attention's quadratic memory cost", "To make the attention weights sum to one", "To add non-linearity between attention heads"], correct: "To keep large dot products from saturating softmax", explanation: "Dot products grow with dimension. Large values push softmax into near one-hot regions where gradients vanish. The softmax already makes the weights sum to 1, and the scaling does not change memory cost." },
    { q: "In a convolutional neural network, what is a unit's 'receptive field'?", a: ["The input region that can affect that unit", "The number of channels in its feature map", "The count of learnable weights in its filter", "The spatial size of its output feature map"], correct: "The input region that can affect that unit", explanation: "The receptive field is the patch of the original input that influences a given activation. It grows with depth, so deeper units 'see' larger areas of the image." },
    { q: "Which optimizer keeps per-parameter adaptive learning rates by combining momentum with RMSProp-style scaling?", a: ["Adam", "Adagrad", "RMSProp", "Nesterov momentum"], correct: "Adam", explanation: "Adam (Adaptive Moment Estimation) tracks both a running mean of gradients (momentum) and a running mean of squared gradients (RMSProp-style scaling). RMSProp alone has no momentum term." },
    { q: "Which standard modeling assumption does time series data most fundamentally violate?", a: ["Independence of observations", "Linearity of the relationships", "Normality of residuals", "Constant error variance"], correct: "Independence of observations", explanation: "Each value in a time series depends on earlier values (autocorrelation), so observations are not i.i.d. Linearity, normality and constant variance may or may not hold, but dependence is built into time series." },
    { q: "In an ARIMA(p, d, q) model, what does the 'd' parameter specify?", a: ["The number of times the series is differenced", "The number of lagged autoregressive terms", "The number of lagged forecast-error terms", "The length of the seasonal cycle"], correct: "The number of times the series is differenced", explanation: "'d' is the 'integrated' order: how many times the series is differenced to make it stationary. p counts autoregressive lags and q counts moving-average (forecast-error) lags." },
    { q: "What is the null hypothesis of the Augmented Dickey-Fuller (ADF) test?", a: ["The series has a unit root", "The series is stationary", "The series has no autocorrelation", "The residuals are normally distributed"], correct: "The series has a unit root", explanation: "The ADF test's null is a unit root (non-stationarity), so rejecting it is evidence of stationarity. The KPSS test does the reverse: its null is stationarity." },
    { q: "Which dimensionality-reduction method builds a fuzzy topological graph of the data and fits a low-dimensional layout by minimizing cross-entropy between the two graphs?", a: ["PCA", "t-SNE", "UMAP", "Isomap"], correct: "UMAP", explanation: "UMAP (Uniform Manifold Approximation and Projection) optimizes a fuzzy-set cross-entropy between high- and low-dimensional graphs. t-SNE instead minimizes a KL divergence between pairwise-similarity distributions." },
    { q: "The principal components found by PCA are the eigenvectors of which matrix?", a: ["The covariance matrix of the data", "The Hessian of the reconstruction loss", "The adjacency matrix of a k-NN graph", "The Cholesky factor of the data matrix"], correct: "The covariance matrix of the data", explanation: "PCA eigendecomposes the covariance matrix (in practice often via an SVD of the centered data). Eigenvectors of a k-NN graph's matrices are used in spectral embedding, not PCA." },
    { q: "What is the main difference between Word2Vec's CBOW and Skip-Gram training objectives?", a: ["CBOW predicts a word from its context; Skip-Gram predicts context from a word", "CBOW predicts context from a word; Skip-Gram predicts a word from its context", "CBOW keeps word order in the window; Skip-Gram ignores word order", "CBOW uses attention over context; Skip-Gram uses a fixed-size window"], correct: "CBOW predicts a word from its context; Skip-Gram predicts context from a word", explanation: "CBOW (Continuous Bag of Words) averages the context vectors to predict the center word. Skip-Gram uses the center word to predict each surrounding word. CBOW is a 'bag', so it discards word order." },
    { q: "When sampling text from a language model, what does raising the softmax temperature above 1 do?", a: ["Flattens the distribution, making output more random", "Sharpens the distribution, making output more predictable", "Removes tokens that fall below a probability cutoff", "Increases the maximum length of the generated text"], correct: "Flattens the distribution, making output more random", explanation: "Dividing the logits by T > 1 shrinks the differences between them, so the distribution flattens and less likely tokens get picked more often. T < 1 sharpens it. Cutting off low-probability tokens is top-k or top-p sampling." },
    { q: "Which loss function was designed for extreme class imbalance in object detection by down-weighting easy, well-classified examples?", a: ["Hinge loss", "Focal loss", "Huber loss", "Triplet loss"], correct: "Focal loss", explanation: "Focal loss multiplies cross-entropy by (1 − p)^γ, so easy examples (mostly background) count for little and training focuses on hard ones. It was introduced with the RetinaNet detector." },
    { q: "What property makes a sequential decision problem a Markov Decision Process?", a: ["Next state depends only on current state and action", "Rewards depend on the full history of past states", "Transitions are fully deterministic given the action", "The agent sees the reward of every possible action"], correct: "Next state depends only on current state and action", explanation: "The Markov property says the future depends only on the present state and action, not on the earlier history. MDP transitions are generally stochastic, not deterministic." },
    { q: "What is the key difference between Q-learning and SARSA in reinforcement learning?", a: ["Q-learning is off-policy; SARSA is on-policy", "Q-learning is on-policy; SARSA is off-policy", "Q-learning is model-based; SARSA is model-free", "Q-learning needs continuous actions; SARSA discrete"], correct: "Q-learning is off-policy; SARSA is on-policy", explanation: "Q-learning updates toward the best next action (max over Q), whatever the agent actually does next. SARSA updates toward the action the agent really takes. Both are model-free." },
    { q: "What does batch normalization do inside a deep neural network?", a: ["Standardizes activations with mini-batch statistics", "Splits each training batch into smaller sub-batches", "Rescales the final softmax outputs to sum to one", "Randomly zeros activations to reduce overfitting"], correct: "Standardizes activations with mini-batch statistics", explanation: "Batch norm subtracts the mini-batch mean, divides by the standard deviation, then applies a learned scale and shift. This stabilizes training and allows higher learning rates. Randomly zeroing activations is dropout." },
    { q: "When choosing tree splits, XGBoost approximates the loss with which mathematical tool?", a: ["First-order Taylor expansion", "Second-order Taylor expansion", "Fourier series expansion", "Lagrange interpolation"], correct: "Second-order Taylor expansion", explanation: "XGBoost uses both the gradient and the Hessian of the loss (a second-order expansion) to score splits and set leaf weights. Classic gradient boosting uses only the first-order gradient." },
    { q: "In natural language processing, what is Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) used for?", a: ["Discovering topics in a collection of documents", "Finding a projection that separates labeled classes", "Detecting anomalies in sensor time series", "Learning dense word embeddings from context"], correct: "Discovering topics in a collection of documents", explanation: "Latent Dirichlet Allocation is a generative topic model: each document is a mix of topics, and each topic is a distribution over words. Linear Discriminant Analysis, which shares the acronym, is the class-separating projection." },
    { q: "What characterizes the 'dying ReLU' problem in neural networks?", a: ["Units get stuck outputting zero and stop learning", "Weights grow until the activations overflow to NaN", "Gradients shrink as they pass through sigmoids", "Units saturate at a fixed large positive value"], correct: "Units get stuck outputting zero and stop learning", explanation: "If a ReLU unit's input becomes negative for every example, it outputs 0 and its gradient is 0, so its weights never update again. Leaky ReLU avoids this by keeping a small slope for negative inputs." },
    { q: "The BLEU score is mainly used to evaluate which kind of output, and how?", a: ["Machine translation, via n-gram precision against references", "Summarization, via n-gram recall against reference summaries", "Image generation, via feature statistics of real images", "Language models, via exponentiated average loss per token"], correct: "Machine translation, via n-gram precision against references", explanation: "BLEU measures n-gram precision of a candidate translation against reference translations, with a brevity penalty for short outputs. The recall-based summary metric is ROUGE, FID scores images, and perplexity is the exponentiated loss." },
    { q: "Which of these is a strictly proper scoring rule for evaluating predicted probabilities?", a: ["Accuracy", "Brier score", "F1 score", "ROC AUC"], correct: "Brier score", explanation: "The Brier score (mean squared error of predicted probabilities) is minimized in expectation only by reporting your true beliefs. Accuracy, F1 and AUC depend only on thresholds or rankings, so they don't reward calibration." },
    { q: "In the original GAN formulation, what loss does the discriminator minimize?", a: ["Binary cross-entropy for telling real from fake", "Reconstruction error of the generator's input", "KL divergence from the generator to a Gaussian", "Mean squared error between real and fake pixels"], correct: "Binary cross-entropy for telling real from fake", explanation: "The discriminator is a binary classifier trained with cross-entropy to label real samples 1 and generated samples 0. Reconstruction error belongs to autoencoders." },
    { q: "What key advantage does the Wasserstein GAN's Earth Mover's distance give over the original GAN objective?", a: ["Meaningful gradients when the distributions don't overlap", "No need for a separate discriminator or critic network", "Exact log-likelihoods for every generated sample", "Direct support for generating discrete tokens"], correct: "Meaningful gradients when the distributions don't overlap", explanation: "When real and generated distributions don't overlap, Jensen-Shannon divergence is constant and gives no gradient. Wasserstein distance still varies smoothly. WGAN still trains a critic network." },
    { q: "What does the forget gate in an LSTM cell do?", a: ["Discards parts of the previous cell state", "Decides which new information to write to the cell", "Controls how much cell state becomes hidden output", "Randomly drops units to prevent overfitting"], correct: "Discards parts of the previous cell state", explanation: "The forget gate outputs values from 0 to 1 that scale the old cell state: 0 means erase, 1 means keep. Writing new information is the input gate's job, and exposing the cell state is the output gate's." },
    { q: "Which regularization technique replaces hard one-hot targets like [1, 0, 0] with softened ones like [0.9, 0.05, 0.05]?", a: ["Dropout", "Label smoothing", "Weight decay", "Early stopping"], correct: "Label smoothing", explanation: "Label smoothing moves a little probability mass from the true class to the others, which discourages overconfident predictions. Dropout, weight decay and early stopping leave the targets unchanged." },
    { q: "How does SMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique) create new training examples?", a: ["Interpolates new points between minority neighbors", "Duplicates existing minority examples at random", "Removes majority examples lying near the class boundary", "Upweights the minority class in the loss function"], correct: "Interpolates new points between minority neighbors", explanation: "SMOTE picks a minority example and one of its k nearest minority neighbors, then creates a new point somewhere on the line between them. Plain random oversampling just duplicates existing examples." },
    { q: "In network analysis, what does eigenvector centrality measure for a node?", a: ["Importance weighted by how central its neighbors are", "The number of direct edges attached to the node", "How often it lies on shortest paths between other nodes", "The inverse of its average distance to all others"], correct: "Importance weighted by how central its neighbors are", explanation: "Eigenvector centrality counts a link to an important node for more than a link to a peripheral one, the same idea behind PageRank. The distractors describe degree, betweenness and closeness centrality." },
    { q: "Geometrically, why does an L1 penalty tend to produce sparse solutions in linear models?", a: ["Its constraint region has corners lying on the axes", "Its penalty grows exponentially with weight size", "Its constraint region is a smooth circle or hypersphere", "It is differentiable everywhere, including at zero"], correct: "Its constraint region has corners lying on the axes", explanation: "The L1 constraint region is a diamond (in 2D) with corners on the axes. Loss contours usually touch it first at a corner, where some weights are exactly zero. The smooth L2 ball has no such corners." },
    { q: "Which of these is a classic symptom of the 'curse of dimensionality'?", a: ["Distances between points become nearly uniform", "Model weights tend to explode toward infinity", "Gradients can't be computed beyond ~1,000 features", "Training data becomes denser as features are added"], correct: "Distances between points become nearly uniform", explanation: "As dimensions grow, data becomes sparse and the nearest and farthest neighbors end up almost equally far away, which weakens distance-based methods like k-NN. Data gets sparser with more features, not denser." },
    { q: "In optimization, what is a saddle point?", a: ["A min along some directions and a max along others", "A point where the gradient is nonzero but small", "The lowest point of a convex function", "A flat region where the loss is exactly constant"], correct: "A min along some directions and a max along others", explanation: "At a saddle point the gradient is zero but the Hessian has both positive and negative eigenvalues, so the surface curves up one way and down another. A flat region of constant loss is a plateau." },
    { q: "Which property defines a strictly convex function?", a: ["Any chord between two points lies above the graph", "Any chord between two points lies below the graph", "Its first derivative is negative everywhere", "Its Hessian is negative definite everywhere"], correct: "Any chord between two points lies above the graph", explanation: "For a strictly convex function, the line segment joining any two points on the graph lies strictly above it between those points. As a result, it has at most one minimum, and any local minimum is the global one. Chords below the graph describe a concave function." },
    { q: "Which diagnostic measure equals the true positive rate divided by the false positive rate?", a: ["Positive likelihood ratio", "Positive predictive value", "Diagnostic odds ratio", "Youden's J statistic"], correct: "Positive likelihood ratio", explanation: "LR+ = sensitivity / (1 − specificity) = TPR / FPR. Youden's J is TPR − FPR, and the diagnostic odds ratio is LR+ divided by LR−." },
    { q: "What does the 'kernel trick' let an SVM do?", a: ["Compute high-dimensional inner products without mapping points", "Project inputs to fewer dimensions before fitting the margin", "Choose the regularization constant C automatically from data", "Hash features into a fixed-size vector to save memory"], correct: "Compute high-dimensional inner products without mapping points", explanation: "A kernel k(x, y) returns the inner product of the mapped points directly, so the SVM works in a huge (even infinite) feature space without ever computing the mapping. Hashing features is the separate 'hashing trick'." },
    { q: "What is the 'reparameterization trick' in variational autoencoders?", a: ["Writing z = μ + σ·ε so gradients pass through sampling", "Replacing ReLU with a smooth approximation like softplus", "Converting categorical inputs into continuous embeddings", "Rescaling weights by their norm to stabilize training"], correct: "Writing z = μ + σ·ε so gradients pass through sampling", explanation: "Sampling z directly blocks backpropagation. Drawing ε from N(0, 1) and computing z = μ + σ·ε moves the randomness into ε, so gradients can flow to μ and σ." },
    { q: "In causal inference, what is a confounder?", a: ["A third variable that affects both treatment and outcome", "A variable lying on the causal path from treatment to outcome", "A variable that is caused by both treatment and outcome", "Random measurement error in the outcome variable"], correct: "A third variable that affects both treatment and outcome", explanation: "A confounder causes both the treatment and the outcome, which creates a spurious association between them. A variable on the causal path is a mediator, and one caused by both is a collider." },
    { q: "How does L-BFGS differ from plain gradient descent?", a: ["It approximates the inverse Hessian from recent gradients", "It computes the exact Hessian at every single iteration", "It uses random mini-batches to estimate the gradient", "It adds a momentum term built from previous updates"], correct: "It approximates the inverse Hessian from recent gradients", explanation: "L-BFGS is a quasi-Newton method. It builds a low-memory approximation of the inverse Hessian from the last few gradient and step pairs, which gives it second-order-like steps. Computing the exact Hessian is Newton's method." },
    { q: "In information theory, what does Shannon entropy quantify?", a: ["The average uncertainty, in bits, of a random variable", "The spread of a distribution around its mean value", "The divergence between two probability distributions", "The amount of information shared between two random variables"], correct: "The average uncertainty, in bits, of a random variable", explanation: "Entropy H(X) = −Σ p log₂ p is the average surprise of an outcome, equivalently the minimum average number of bits needed to encode it. A fair coin has 1 bit. Shared information between two variables is mutual information." },
    { q: "What happens during the E-step of the Expectation-Maximization (EM) algorithm?", a: ["Compute expected latent variables under current parameters", "Update parameters to maximize the expected log-likelihood", "Reduce the data's dimensionality with an SVD", "Draw new samples from the full joint distribution"], correct: "Compute expected latent variables under current parameters", explanation: "The E-step computes the posterior over the latent variables given the current parameters, for example each point's cluster responsibilities. The M-step then updates the parameters to maximize the expected log-likelihood." },
    { q: "In deep learning, what is a skip (residual) connection?", a: ["A path that adds a layer's input to its later output", "A rule that skips training examples already learned", "A link that drops random connections during training", "A gate that decides which time steps to forget"], correct: "A path that adds a layer's input to its later output", explanation: "Skip connections, as in ResNet, carry activations around one or more layers and add them back in. This gives gradients a direct path and makes very deep networks trainable. Randomly dropping connections is DropConnect." },
    { q: "In tf-idf weighting, why does a word like 'the' get a low weight even if it appears many times in a document?", a: ["It occurs in almost every document, so idf is low", "Its term frequency is capped at a fixed maximum value", "Very short words get a built-in length penalty", "Stemming merges it with other common words"], correct: "It occurs in almost every document, so idf is low", explanation: "idf = log(N / document frequency). A word found in nearly every document has idf near 0, which cancels its high term frequency. Words frequent in one document but rare elsewhere get the highest weights." },
    { q: "A churn model uses the feature 'account_closed_date', which is only filled in after a customer churns, and scores 99% in validation. What is the problem?", a: ["Target leakage", "Overfitting to noise", "Covariate shift", "Class imbalance"], correct: "Target leakage", explanation: "The feature carries information that only exists once the outcome is known, so the model is effectively reading the answer. It looks great in validation but fails in production. Plain overfitting would show poor validation scores." },
    { q: "Which distribution models the number of independent events in a fixed time interval at a constant average rate, such as emails per hour?", a: ["Binomial distribution", "Poisson distribution", "Exponential distribution", "Geometric distribution"], correct: "Poisson distribution", explanation: "The Poisson distribution counts events in a fixed interval at a constant rate λ. The exponential distribution models the waiting time between those events, and the binomial needs a fixed number of trials." },
    { q: "In survival analysis, what does the Kaplan-Meier estimator produce?", a: ["A non-parametric estimate of the survival function", "A hazard ratio adjusted for several covariates", "A parametric Weibull fit to observed event times", "A logistic model of whether the event occurs"], correct: "A non-parametric estimate of the survival function", explanation: "Kaplan-Meier builds a step-function estimate of survival probability over time and handles right-censored subjects. Covariate-adjusted hazard ratios come from Cox regression." },
    { q: "In linear regression, what does the homoscedasticity assumption require?", a: ["Residuals have constant variance across fitted values", "Residuals follow a normal distribution with mean zero", "Predictors are not strongly correlated with each other", "Residuals are uncorrelated from one point to the next"], correct: "Residuals have constant variance across fitted values", explanation: "Homoscedasticity means the spread of residuals is the same at all levels of the predictors. When it fails (heteroscedasticity), the usual standard errors are unreliable. Correlated predictors are a separate issue, multicollinearity." },
    { q: "What does the F-statistic in a one-way ANOVA represent?", a: ["Between-group variance divided by within-group variance", "Within-group variance divided by between-group variance", "The difference between the two largest group means", "The total variance divided by the number of groups"], correct: "Between-group variance divided by within-group variance", explanation: "F = (variance between group means) / (variance within groups). A large F means the group means differ by more than random noise inside the groups would explain." },
    { q: "What does a reliability diagram (calibration curve) show about a classifier?", a: ["Whether predicted probabilities match observed frequencies", "How precision and recall trade off across thresholds", "How training and validation loss change over epochs", "How the true positive rate varies with the false positive rate"], correct: "Whether predicted probabilities match observed frequencies", explanation: "It plots predicted probability against the actual fraction of positives. A well-calibrated model sits on the diagonal: events predicted at 80% happen about 80% of the time. The other options describe PR curves, learning curves and ROC curves." },
    { q: "What is temperature scaling, as used to calibrate neural network classifiers?", a: ["Dividing logits by one learned scalar after training", "Adjusting the learning rate smoothly as training progresses", "Fitting a separate isotonic curve to each class", "Adding Gaussian noise to inputs during training"], correct: "Dividing logits by one learned scalar after training", explanation: "After training, a single temperature T is fit on validation data and the logits are divided by it. This softens overconfident probabilities without changing which class ranks highest, so accuracy is unchanged." },
    { q: "Which anomaly-detection algorithm builds random trees and flags points that get separated from the rest after unusually few random splits?", a: ["DBSCAN", "Isolation Forest", "One-Class SVM", "Local Outlier Factor"], correct: "Isolation Forest", explanation: "Isolation Forest relies on anomalies being 'few and different', so random splits separate them quickly (short path lengths). Local Outlier Factor compares local densities instead, and DBSCAN is a density-based clustering method." },
    { q: "A point's mean distance to its own cluster is 1, and its mean distance to the nearest other cluster is 4. What is its silhouette value?", a: ["0.75", "0.25", "3", "−0.75"], correct: "0.75", explanation: "Silhouette s = (b − a) / max(a, b) = (4 − 1) / 4 = 0.75. Values near 1 mean the point fits its own cluster well, and negative values suggest it may be in the wrong cluster." },
    { q: "In Dirichlet process mixture models, what does the 'Chinese Restaurant Process' describe?", a: ["A prior where points join existing clusters or start new ones", "A sampler that assigns points to a fixed number K of clusters", "A schedule for annealing the temperature during MCMC", "A rule that assigns each point to its nearest centroid"], correct: "A prior where points join existing clusters or start new ones", explanation: "Each new customer (data point) joins a table (cluster) with probability proportional to how many people already sit there, or opens a new table. This lets the number of clusters grow with the data instead of fixing K in advance." },
    { q: "What does the Cramér-Rao lower bound state?", a: ["Lowest variance any unbiased estimator can achieve", "Lowest bias any minimum-variance estimator can achieve", "Minimum sample size needed for a consistent estimate", "Largest Fisher information a single sample can carry"], correct: "Lowest variance any unbiased estimator can achieve", explanation: "The variance of any unbiased estimator is at least 1 / (Fisher information). An estimator that reaches this bound is called efficient." },
    { q: "How does Gibbs sampling generate draws from a joint distribution?", a: ["Drawing each variable in turn from its full conditional", "Proposing random moves accepted with some probability", "Resampling the dataset with replacement many times", "Simulating Hamiltonian dynamics using gradients"], correct: "Drawing each variable in turn from its full conditional", explanation: "Gibbs sampling updates one variable at a time by drawing from its distribution given all the others. These draws are always accepted. Accept/reject proposals describe Metropolis-Hastings, and gradient-driven dynamics describe Hamiltonian Monte Carlo." },
    { q: "Which property defines an orthogonal matrix Q?", a: ["Its transpose equals its inverse", "Its determinant is exactly zero", "It equals its own transpose", "All of its eigenvalues are real numbers"], correct: "Its transpose equals its inverse", explanation: "QᵀQ = I, so Qᵀ = Q⁻¹. Orthogonal matrices preserve lengths and angles (rotations and reflections) and have determinant ±1. Equaling its own transpose defines a symmetric matrix." },
    { q: "In an epsilon-greedy reinforcement learning policy, what does ε control?", a: ["The probability of taking a random action", "The discount factor applied to future rewards", "The step size used for value updates", "The threshold for ending an episode early"], correct: "The probability of taking a random action", explanation: "With probability ε the agent explores by picking a random action. Otherwise it exploits the best-known action. The discount factor is γ and the step size is α." },
    { q: "What is zero-shot learning?", a: ["Recognizing classes never seen during training", "Learning a new class from a single labeled example", "Training without any labeled examples at all", "Adapting a model with a few labeled examples"], correct: "Recognizing classes never seen during training", explanation: "Zero-shot models classify unseen classes using side information such as text descriptions or shared embeddings. Learning from one example is one-shot learning, and learning from a handful is few-shot learning." },
    { q: "In self-supervised learning, what is a contrastive loss designed to do?", a: ["Pull similar pairs together and push dissimilar ones apart", "Reconstruct the input from a compressed latent code", "Predict masked tokens from their surrounding context", "Maximize the variance of each output dimension"], correct: "Pull similar pairs together and push dissimilar ones apart", explanation: "Contrastive methods such as SimCLR pull two augmented views of the same image together in embedding space and push other images away. Reconstruction is the autoencoder objective, and masked prediction is BERT-style." },
    { q: "Starting from an ImageNet-trained network and fine-tuning it on 2,000 chest X-rays is an example of what?", a: ["Transfer learning", "Ensemble learning", "Knowledge distillation", "Multi-task learning"], correct: "Transfer learning", explanation: "Transfer learning reuses features learned on a large general dataset to get a head start on a smaller, specific task. Distillation trains a small model to mimic a large one, which is a different goal." },
    { q: "What does the softmax function output for the input vector (0, 0, ln 2)?", a: ["(0.25, 0.25, 0.50)", "(0.00, 0.00, 1.00)", "(0.33, 0.33, 0.33)", "(0.20, 0.20, 0.60)"], correct: "(0.25, 0.25, 0.50)", explanation: "Exponentiating gives (1, 1, 2), which sums to 4. Dividing by 4 gives (0.25, 0.25, 0.5). Softmax always produces positive values that sum to 1, unlike a hard argmax (0, 0, 1)." },
    { q: "In the bias-variance decomposition, what is 'irreducible error'?", a: ["Noise in the data that no model can remove", "Error from poorly tuned hyperparameters", "Error from a model too simple for the pattern", "Error from sensitivity to the training sample"], correct: "Noise in the data that no model can remove", explanation: "Irreducible error comes from randomness or unmeasured factors in the outcome itself and sets a floor on achievable error. 'Too simple' describes bias and 'sensitive to the sample' describes variance, both of which can be reduced." },
    { q: "In MCMC, what is the Gelman-Rubin statistic (R-hat) used to assess?", a: ["Whether multiple chains agree, signaling convergence", "The optimal step size for the MCMC proposal distribution", "How many effectively independent draws a chain has", "The exact posterior mean of each model parameter"], correct: "Whether multiple chains agree, signaling convergence", explanation: "R-hat compares the variance between several independent chains with the variance within them. Values near 1 suggest they have converged to the same distribution. Counting effectively independent draws is the effective sample size." },
    { q: "What must be true of the source signals for Independent Component Analysis (ICA) to separate a mixture?", a: ["They are independent and non-Gaussian", "They are independent and Gaussian", "They are uncorrelated and orthogonal", "Their mixing matrix is symmetric"], correct: "They are independent and non-Gaussian", explanation: "Mixtures of independent signals look more Gaussian than the sources (central limit effect), so ICA recovers the sources by maximizing non-Gaussianity. Gaussian sources can't be separated this way; at most one source may be Gaussian." },
    { q: "What does the Neyman-Pearson lemma establish in hypothesis testing?", a: ["The likelihood ratio test is most powerful for simple hypotheses", "Maximum likelihood estimators are asymptotically normal", "p-values are uniformly distributed under the null hypothesis", "A confidence interval is equivalent to a two-sided test"], correct: "The likelihood ratio test is most powerful for simple hypotheses", explanation: "For a simple null versus a simple alternative at a fixed significance level, rejecting when the likelihood ratio is large gives the highest power of any test. The other statements are true results but come from other theorems." },
    { q: "In generalized linear models, what defines membership in the exponential family of distributions?", a: ["A density writable as h(x)·exp(η·T(x) − A(η))", "A density whose tails decay exponentially fast", "A distribution defined only on positive values", "A distribution with a memoryless waiting time"], correct: "A density writable as h(x)·exp(η·T(x) − A(η))", explanation: "Exponential-family distributions (Gaussian, Poisson, Bernoulli, gamma, and others) share this canonical form with natural parameter η and sufficient statistic T(x). The name has nothing to do with the exponential distribution's memorylessness or with tail decay." },
    { q: "When running hundreds of hypothesis tests, why is the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure usually more powerful than the Bonferroni correction?", a: ["It controls the expected fraction of false discoveries among rejections", "It controls the chance of even one false rejection across all the tests", "It assumes independent tests, so it divides α by √m instead of by m", "It pools all p-values into one combined test before rejecting any"], correct: "It controls the expected fraction of false discoveries among rejections", explanation: "Benjamini-Hochberg controls the false discovery rate (FDR), a looser target than Bonferroni's family-wise error rate (the chance of any false rejection), so it rejects more true effects." },
    { q: "By Sklar's theorem, what does a copula let you do when modeling a multivariate distribution?", a: ["Model the dependence structure separately from the marginals", "Reduce dimensionality by projecting onto uncorrelated components", "Replace the covariance matrix with a diagonal one", "Make non-stationary series stationary by differencing each margin"], correct: "Model the dependence structure separately from the marginals", explanation: "Sklar's theorem says any joint distribution can be written as a copula applied to its marginal CDFs, so you can pick any marginals and link them with a chosen dependence structure. Projecting onto uncorrelated components is PCA." },
    { q: "The Rao-Blackwell theorem conditions an unbiased estimator on a sufficient statistic. What is true of the resulting estimator?", a: ["It stays unbiased, with variance no larger than before", "It stays unbiased, with variance equal to the Cramér-Rao bound", "It loses some bias, but its variance may go up or down", "It becomes biased, but with strictly lower mean squared error"], correct: "It stays unbiased, with variance no larger than before", explanation: "Taking E[estimator | sufficient statistic] keeps the mean the same and can only reduce (or keep) the variance. It does not guarantee reaching the Cramér-Rao bound." },
    { q: "Which distance measures how far a point is from a distribution's mean while accounting for the covariance between variables?", a: ["Euclidean distance", "Chebyshev distance", "Mahalanobis distance", "Bhattacharyya distance"], correct: "Mahalanobis distance", explanation: "Mahalanobis distance scales by the inverse covariance matrix, so it counts distance in correlated 'standard deviation' units. Bhattacharyya distance compares two distributions, not a point to a distribution." },
    { q: "In survival analysis, what is the key assumption of the Cox proportional hazards model?", a: ["A Weibull-shaped baseline hazard for all subjects", "A constant hazard ratio between any two subjects", "A hazard for each subject that is constant over time", "Survival times that are normally distributed"], correct: "A constant hazard ratio between any two subjects", explanation: "Cox leaves the baseline hazard unspecified and only assumes covariates multiply it by a constant factor. A constant hazard for each subject is the exponential model, a different assumption." },
    { q: "In maximum likelihood estimation, the Fisher information equals which of these?", a: ["The variance of the score (gradient of the log-likelihood)", "The mean of the score (gradient of the log-likelihood)", "The large-sample variance of the MLE itself", "The expected Hessian of the log-likelihood, with no minus sign"], correct: "The variance of the score (gradient of the log-likelihood)", explanation: "Fisher information is the variance of the score, equivalently minus the expected Hessian. The MLE's large-sample variance is its inverse, and the score's mean is zero." },
    { q: "What property of the Metropolis-Hastings accept/reject rule ensures the Markov chain's stationary distribution is the target distribution?", a: ["Detailed balance (reversibility) with respect to the target", "Symmetry of the proposal distribution in both directions", "Independence between successive samples of the chain", "A burn-in period long enough to forget the starting point"], correct: "Detailed balance (reversibility) with respect to the target", explanation: "The acceptance ratio is chosen so that flow from A to B equals flow from B to A under the target, which makes the target stationary. Metropolis-Hastings explicitly allows asymmetric proposals; burn-in is a practical step, not the guarantee." },
    { q: "What allows a normalizing flow to compute exact likelihoods of data?", a: ["A chain of invertible maps with cheap Jacobian determinants", "An adversarial critic that estimates the density ratio to real data", "An evidence lower bound (ELBO) maximized with an encoder network", "A fixed noising schedule reversed by a learned denoising network"], correct: "A chain of invertible maps with cheap Jacobian determinants", explanation: "Invertibility plus the change-of-variables formula gives the exact density, as long as each Jacobian determinant is easy to compute. VAEs only maximize a lower bound, and GANs give no explicit density." },
    { q: "Why is Group Normalization often preferred over Batch Normalization when only 1–2 images fit on each GPU?", a: ["It computes statistics over channel groups within each sample", "It computes batch statistics but with a much larger momentum", "It normalizes each channel across the batch and spatial axes", "It normalizes all channels of a layer together across the batch"], correct: "It computes statistics over channel groups within each sample", explanation: "Group Norm's statistics come from one sample at a time, so they don't depend on batch size. Normalizing each channel across the batch is exactly what Batch Norm does, which becomes noisy with tiny batches." },
    { q: "Swish is defined as x · sigmoid(x). Which property sets its shape apart from ReLU?", a: ["It is non-monotonic, dipping slightly below zero for negative x", "It is bounded between 0 and 1, like the sigmoid it is built from", "It is exactly zero for all negative x but smooth at the origin", "It is linear for negative x with a small fixed slope of 0.01"], correct: "It is non-monotonic, dipping slightly below zero for negative x", explanation: "For negative x, Swish dips to a small negative minimum before returning toward zero, so it is smooth and non-monotonic. A fixed 0.01 slope describes Leaky ReLU, and Swish is unbounded above." },
    { q: "In a Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Model (DDPM), what does the forward process do?", a: ["Adds Gaussian noise step by step on a fixed variance schedule", "Removes noise step by step using a trained neural denoiser", "Encodes images into a discrete codebook of latent tokens", "Trains a discriminator to separate real images from noised ones"], correct: "Adds Gaussian noise step by step on a fixed variance schedule", explanation: "The forward process is a fixed Markov chain that gradually destroys the data with noise; it has nothing to learn. Removing noise with a neural network is the reverse (generative) process." },
    { q: "Which image-generation metric fits Gaussians to Inception-network features of real and generated images and measures the distance between them (lower is better)?", a: ["Inception Score (IS)", "Fréchet Inception Distance (FID)", "Kernel Inception Distance (KID)", "Structural Similarity Index (SSIM)"], correct: "Fréchet Inception Distance (FID)", explanation: "FID computes the Fréchet (Wasserstein-2) distance between two Gaussians fitted to Inception features. KID also uses Inception features but compares them with a kernel (MMD) instead of fitting Gaussians, and IS uses no real images." },
    { q: "What is 'mode collapse' in a Generative Adversarial Network?", a: ["The generator covers only a few modes, losing output variety", "The discriminator gets so strong that generator gradients vanish", "The generator reproduces exact training images instead of new ones", "The two networks' losses oscillate forever without converging"], correct: "The generator covers only a few modes, losing output variety", explanation: "In mode collapse the generator finds a few outputs that fool the discriminator and keeps producing them, ignoring the rest of the data distribution. Vanishing gradients from an overly strong discriminator are a separate GAN failure." },
    { q: "Why did the original Transformer use sine and cosine waves of different frequencies for positional encoding?", a: ["PE(p+k) is a linear function of PE(p), easing relative-position learning", "The encodings are mutually orthogonal, keeping token embeddings separable", "The encodings shrink with position, down-weighting distant tokens", "The encodings are learned, so they adapt to typical sequence lengths"], correct: "PE(p+k) is a linear function of PE(p), easing relative-position learning", explanation: "By the angle-addition identities, shifting by k is a fixed rotation of each sine/cosine pair, so relative offsets are easy to represent. The sinusoidal encodings are fixed, not learned, and they don't decay with position." },
    { q: "What does label smoothing (e.g., training toward 0.9 instead of 1.0 for the true class) mainly combat?", a: ["Overconfidence, as logits grow huge to chase 100% certainty", "Vanishing gradients in the early layers of very deep networks", "Class imbalance, by up-weighting rare classes in the loss", "Noisy inputs, by slightly blurring each training image"], correct: "Overconfidence, as logits grow huge to chase 100% certainty", explanation: "With hard one-hot targets, cross-entropy keeps pushing logits apart, giving overconfident, poorly calibrated models. Softened targets cap that pressure; they do nothing specific for class imbalance." },
    { q: "How does the CutMix data augmentation work?", a: ["Pastes a patch from another image and mixes labels by patch area", "Blends two whole images pixel-wise and mixes labels by blend weight", "Erases a random square of the image and keeps the original label", "Shuffles image tiles in a grid and asks the network to reorder them"], correct: "Pastes a patch from another image and mixes labels by patch area", explanation: "CutMix cuts a rectangle from one image into another and sets the label in proportion to area. Whole-image blending is Mixup, and erasing a square with no replacement is Cutout." },
    { q: "What is the core idea of knowledge distillation?", a: ["A small student model learns to match a large teacher's softened outputs", "Weights below a magnitude threshold are pruned from a large network", "32-bit float weights are converted to 8-bit integers for inference", "Several models' predictions are averaged into one ensemble output"], correct: "A small student model learns to match a large teacher's softened outputs", explanation: "The teacher's temperature-softened probabilities carry 'dark knowledge' about which classes resemble each other, helping the student generalize. Pruning and quantization are other compression methods." },
    { q: "How does natural gradient descent differ from ordinary gradient descent?", a: ["It preconditions the gradient with the inverse Fisher information matrix", "It preconditions the gradient with a running mean of squared gradients", "It adds a momentum term so updates keep moving along past directions", "It uses the exact inverse Hessian of the loss, as in Newton's method"], correct: "It preconditions the gradient with the inverse Fisher information matrix", explanation: "Using the inverse Fisher matrix measures step size by change in the model's output distribution (KL divergence), making updates invariant to parameterization. A running mean of squared gradients is RMSProp/Adam." },
    { q: "What change does AdamW make to the standard Adam optimizer?", a: ["It decouples weight decay from the adaptive gradient update", "It adds a linear learning-rate warmup at the start of training", "It uses the running maximum of squared gradients, not the average", "It removes bias correction from the first and second moments"], correct: "It decouples weight decay from the adaptive gradient update", explanation: "In Adam, L2 decay added to the gradient gets rescaled by the adaptive denominator, weakening it; AdamW subtracts decay directly from the weights. Keeping a running maximum of squared gradients is AMSGrad." },
    { q: "What distinguishes Nesterov accelerated gradient (NAG) from classical momentum?", a: ["It takes the gradient at the look-ahead point after the momentum step", "It takes the gradient at the current point, then adds the momentum", "It scales each parameter's step by its summed squared gradients", "It caps the momentum vector's norm to stop runaway velocity"], correct: "It takes the gradient at the look-ahead point after the momentum step", explanation: "NAG first moves along the accumulated velocity, then measures the gradient there, letting it correct overshoot sooner. Taking the gradient at the current point is classical momentum; summed squared gradients is Adagrad." },
    { q: "Why do MobileNet-style networks replace standard convolutions with depthwise separable ones?", a: ["Far fewer multiply-adds, by splitting spatial filtering from channel mixing", "A larger receptive field, by spacing the kernel taps apart with dilation", "Better adversarial robustness, by limiting each filter to one channel", "Support for any input size, by replacing dense layers with pooling"], correct: "Far fewer multiply-adds, by splitting spatial filtering from channel mixing", explanation: "A depthwise convolution filters each channel spatially, then a 1×1 pointwise convolution mixes channels, cutting compute roughly 8–9× for 3×3 kernels. Spacing kernel taps apart is dilated convolution." },
    { q: "In Inception-style networks, what is a 1×1 convolution mainly used for?", a: ["Cutting the channel count before costly 3×3 and 5×5 convolutions", "Shrinking the height and width of feature maps, like stride-2 pooling", "Adding spatial context by mixing each pixel with its eight neighbors", "Randomly dropping whole channels during training as a regularizer"], correct: "Cutting the channel count before costly 3×3 and 5×5 convolutions", explanation: "A 1×1 convolution mixes channels at each pixel without touching spatial size, so it works as a cheap bottleneck. It sees no neighbors and does not downsample (with stride 1)." },
    { q: "How does RoI Align (used in Mask R-CNN) improve on RoI Pooling?", a: ["It samples features bilinearly at exact, unrounded positions", "It rounds region borders to the nearest cell before pooling", "It applies non-maximum suppression to regions before pooling", "It pools the whole feature map globally instead of per region"], correct: "It samples features bilinearly at exact, unrounded positions", explanation: "RoI Pooling quantizes (rounds) coordinates, misaligning features by up to a cell, which hurts pixel-level masks. RoI Align skips rounding and interpolates values at exact positions." },
    { q: "In Apache Spark, which of these operations is most likely to trigger a shuffle?", a: ["A groupByKey collecting each key's values", "A filter that keeps rows matching a predicate", "A map that transforms each record independently", "A cache that keeps an RDD in executor memory"], correct: "A groupByKey collecting each key's values", explanation: "Grouping (like joins) needs all records with the same key on the same partition, so data moves across the network. Filter and map are narrow transformations that work within each partition." },
    { q: "What does the Catalyst optimizer do in Spark SQL?", a: ["Rewrites logical query plans into optimized physical plans", "Schedules executors and restarts them when worker nodes fail", "Manages JVM garbage collection and off-heap executor memory", "Translates DataFrame code into Hadoop MapReduce jobs"], correct: "Rewrites logical query plans into optimized physical plans", explanation: "Catalyst applies rule-based and cost-based optimizations, such as pushing filters down and reordering joins, then picks a physical plan. Restarting failed executors is the cluster manager's job." },
    { q: "How does 'salting' fix data skew in a distributed join?", a: ["Appending random suffixes to hot keys and replicating the other side's rows", "Dropping the most frequent keys and joining them separately in a later pass", "Broadcasting the larger table to every executor so no shuffle is needed", "Rehashing keys with a stronger hash function so collisions spread out"], correct: "Appending random suffixes to hot keys and replicating the other side's rows", explanation: "A salt splits one overloaded key into many sub-keys spread over partitions; the matching rows on the other side are duplicated per salt so the join still works. A better hash can't help, since every row with the same key still hashes to one partition." },
    { q: "A Bloom filter answers 'is x in the set?' using little memory. What kind of errors can it make?", a: ["False positives are possible; false negatives are not", "False negatives are possible; false positives are not", "Both false positives and false negatives are possible", "Neither error occurs, as long as the bit array is not full"], correct: "False positives are possible; false negatives are not", explanation: "An inserted element always sets its bits, so it is never missed; but other elements can set the same bits, so a non-member can look present. That is why 'no' answers can be trusted outright." },
    { q: "What does the HyperLogLog algorithm estimate using only a few kilobytes of memory?", a: ["The number of distinct elements in a huge stream", "The most frequent items (heavy hitters) in a stream", "Whether a given element has appeared in the stream", "Approximate quantiles, such as a stream's median"], correct: "The number of distinct elements in a huge stream", explanation: "HyperLogLog hashes items and tracks the longest run of leading zeros to estimate cardinality. Membership checks are a Bloom filter's job, and heavy hitters use sketches like Count-Min." },
    { q: "A fraud model's input features look statistically the same as last year, but fraudsters changed tactics so the same inputs now map to different labels. What is this?", a: ["Concept drift: P(Y | X) has changed", "Data drift: P(X) has changed", "Label shift: P(Y) changed, P(X | Y) fixed", "Train-serve skew from a pipeline bug"], correct: "Concept drift: P(Y | X) has changed", explanation: "When the relationship between inputs and the target changes, that is concept drift. Data drift would show up as a change in the input distribution itself, which the scenario rules out." },
    { q: "In a multi-armed bandit, how does Thompson sampling choose the next arm?", a: ["Draws from each arm's reward posterior and plays the best draw", "Plays the arm with the highest upper confidence bound on its mean", "Plays the best arm so far, but a random arm with probability ε", "Splits traffic evenly across arms until a fixed sample size"], correct: "Draws from each arm's reward posterior and plays the best draw", explanation: "Thompson sampling is Bayesian: arms are chosen in proportion to the probability they are best, so exploration fades as posteriors narrow. Highest upper bound is UCB; random with probability ε is ε-greedy." },
    { q: "For a single prediction, the SHAP values of all features add up to what?", a: ["The prediction minus the average (base) prediction", "Exactly 1, since SHAP values are normalized importances", "The prediction itself, with no base value subtracted", "The drop in accuracy when every feature is permuted"], correct: "The prediction minus the average (base) prediction", explanation: "SHAP's local accuracy (efficiency) property means base value + sum of SHAP values = the prediction. So the attributions explain the gap from the average prediction, not the raw output." },
    { q: "How does LIME produce an explanation for one prediction of a black-box model?", a: ["Fits a simple model to the black box's outputs on nearby perturbed samples", "Computes the gradient of the output with respect to each input feature", "Averages each feature's marginal contribution over all feature subsets", "Measures the accuracy lost when each feature's values are shuffled"], correct: "Fits a simple model to the black box's outputs on nearby perturbed samples", explanation: "LIME perturbs the instance, queries the model, and fits a locally weighted interpretable surrogate such as a sparse linear model. Averaging over feature subsets describes Shapley values (SHAP)." },
    { q: "What does Platt scaling do for a support vector machine or other classifier?", a: ["Fits a sigmoid to the classifier's scores to give calibrated probabilities", "Rescales input features to zero mean and unit variance before training", "Fits a non-decreasing step function to the scores to give probabilities", "Reweights classes in the hinge loss to correct for class imbalance"], correct: "Fits a sigmoid to the classifier's scores to give calibrated probabilities", explanation: "Platt scaling trains a two-parameter logistic regression on held-out scores, turning SVM margins into probabilities. The non-decreasing step-function approach is isotonic regression." },
    { q: "How does Byte Pair Encoding (BPE) build an LLM's subword vocabulary?", a: ["Repeatedly merging the most frequent adjacent pair of symbols", "Repeatedly splitting the longest words at their rarest letter", "Keeping only whole words above a minimum corpus frequency", "Pruning a large vocabulary by likelihood under a unigram model"], correct: "Repeatedly merging the most frequent adjacent pair of symbols", explanation: "BPE starts from characters (or bytes) and greedily merges the most common pair until reaching the target vocabulary size, so rare words split into known pieces. Pruning by unigram likelihood is the separate Unigram LM tokenizer." },
    { q: "How do the pre-training objectives of BERT and GPT differ?", a: ["BERT fills in masked tokens using both sides; GPT predicts the next token", "BERT predicts the next token; GPT fills in masked tokens using both sides", "BERT predicts the previous token; GPT predicts the next token", "BERT reconstructs whole sentences; GPT classifies sentence pairs"], correct: "BERT fills in masked tokens using both sides; GPT predicts the next token", explanation: "BERT uses masked language modeling with bidirectional attention; GPT uses causal (left-to-right) language modeling. Bidirectional context suits understanding tasks, while causal modeling suits generation." },
    { q: "What does ROUGE-L measure between a generated summary and a reference?", a: ["Longest common subsequence of words", "Overlap of consecutive word pairs", "N-gram precision with a brevity penalty", "Cosine similarity of word embeddings"], correct: "Longest common subsequence of words", explanation: "ROUGE-L finds the longest in-order word sequence shared by both texts, allowing gaps. Bigram overlap is ROUGE-2, and n-gram precision with a brevity penalty is BLEU." },
    { q: "In a pointer-generator network for summarization, what problem does the pointer (copy) mechanism solve?", a: ["Producing rare or out-of-vocabulary words found in the source", "Repeating the same phrase, by tracking where attention already went", "Exploding gradients when backpropagating through long documents", "Low diversity among the candidates returned by beam search"], correct: "Producing rare or out-of-vocabulary words found in the source", explanation: "A generation probability lets the model either generate from its vocabulary or copy a source token, so names and rare words can appear in the output. Tracking past attention to curb repetition is the separate coverage mechanism." },
    { q: "How does a Vision Transformer (ViT) turn an image into transformer input?", a: ["Splits it into fixed-size patches, embeds each, and treats them as tokens", "Treats every individual pixel as a token and attends over all of them", "Scans pixel rows with a recurrent network, then attends over row states", "Pools the image into one vector and feeds it through stacked MLP layers"], correct: "Splits it into fixed-size patches, embeds each, and treats them as tokens", explanation: "ViT cuts the image into patches (e.g., 16×16), linearly projects each, adds position embeddings, and feeds the sequence to a standard encoder. Pixel-level tokens would make attention far too expensive." },
    { q: "What defines the U-Net architecture used in image segmentation?", a: ["An encoder-decoder whose skip links concatenate encoder maps into the decoder", "An encoder-decoder with no skip links, squeezing all detail through a bottleneck", "A stack of residual blocks that add each block's input back to its output", "A backbone plus a top-down pathway that adds features for box detection"], correct: "An encoder-decoder whose skip links concatenate encoder maps into the decoder", explanation: "U-Net's skip connections pass high-resolution encoder features to matching decoder stages, recovering detail lost to pooling. Additive residual blocks define ResNet, and the top-down detection pathway is FPN." },
    { q: "In object detection, what does non-maximum suppression (NMS) do?", a: ["Removes overlapping boxes for one object, keeping the top-scoring one", "Merges all overlapping boxes into one averaged box per object", "Clips gradients whose magnitude exceeds a maximum threshold", "Suppresses easy background anchors in the classification loss"], correct: "Removes overlapping boxes for one object, keeping the top-scoring one", explanation: "NMS keeps the highest-confidence box and discards others whose IoU with it exceeds a threshold. Averaging overlapping boxes is a different technique (weighted box fusion)." },
    { q: "What distinguishes panoptic segmentation from semantic and instance segmentation?", a: ["Every pixel gets a class, plus an instance ID for countable objects", "Every pixel gets a class, but same-class objects are merged together", "Only countable objects get pixel masks; background stays unlabeled", "Every object and background region gets a bounding box, not a mask"], correct: "Every pixel gets a class, plus an instance ID for countable objects", explanation: "Panoptic segmentation unifies the two: background 'stuff' gets a class label and each 'thing' gets its own instance. Merging same-class objects is semantic segmentation; leaving background unlabeled is instance segmentation." },
    { q: "How does CLIP learn to align images and text during pretraining?", a: ["A contrastive loss pulling matched image-text embeddings together", "A captioning loss training the image encoder to write each caption", "A reconstruction loss in one autoencoder shared by both modalities", "A region-to-phrase matcher built on parsed syntax trees of captions"], correct: "A contrastive loss pulling matched image-text embeddings together", explanation: "CLIP encodes N images and N captions in a batch and trains so the N correct pairs have high cosine similarity while the mismatched pairs score low. It never generates captions." },
    { q: "In reinforcement learning, what is the clipped surrogate objective of Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) designed to prevent?", a: ["Overly large policy updates, by limiting the new/old probability ratio", "Overestimated Q-values, by taking the minimum of two critic networks", "Forgetting old skills, by replaying trajectories from past policies", "Sparse-reward stalls, by relabeling failed goals as achieved ones"], correct: "Overly large policy updates, by limiting the new/old probability ratio", explanation: "PPO clips the probability ratio (e.g., to 1 ± 0.2) so one update can't move the policy too far and collapse performance. Taking the minimum of two critics is TD3's trick." },
    { q: "Which description fits the Soft Actor-Critic (SAC) algorithm?", a: ["Off-policy; maximizes reward plus policy entropy", "On-policy; maximizes reward with a clipped ratio", "Off-policy; learns a deterministic policy plus noise", "On-policy; uses Monte Carlo returns with no critic"], correct: "Off-policy; maximizes reward plus policy entropy", explanation: "SAC learns from a replay buffer and adds an entropy bonus, so it keeps a stochastic, exploratory policy. A deterministic policy plus noise describes DDPG/TD3; the clipped ratio is PPO." },
    { q: "In TD(0) learning with reward r, discount γ, and value estimate V, what is the temporal-difference error for a step from s to s′?", a: ["r + γ·V(s′) − V(s)", "r + V(s′) − γ·V(s)", "G − V(s), G = full return", "γ·(r + V(s′)) − V(s)"], correct: "r + γ·V(s′) − V(s)", explanation: "The TD target is the immediate reward plus the discounted next-state value; the error is that target minus the current estimate. Using the full return G instead gives the Monte Carlo error." },
    { q: "In TD(λ), what do eligibility traces provide that lets learning sit between one-step TD and Monte Carlo?", a: ["A decaying memory of visited states, so one TD error updates many", "A learned model of transitions, so the agent can plan simulated steps", "A buffer of past transitions, sampled uniformly to break correlations", "A schedule that lowers the discount factor γ as training progresses"], correct: "A decaying memory of visited states, so one TD error updates many", explanation: "Each visited state keeps a trace that decays by γλ per step, so a new TD error credits recent states in proportion. λ = 0 gives one-step TD and λ = 1 approximates Monte Carlo." },
    { q: "Which problem does Hindsight Experience Replay (HER) mainly address, and how?", a: ["Sparse rewards, by relabeling failed episodes with goals actually reached", "Correlated samples, by replaying stored transitions in a random order", "Slow learning, by replaying transitions with large TD errors more often", "Overestimation, by using a separate target network for bootstrapping"], correct: "Sparse rewards, by relabeling failed episodes with goals actually reached", explanation: "If an agent misses its goal, HER pretends the place it did reach was the goal, turning a zero-reward episode into a useful lesson. Replaying high-TD-error transitions more often is prioritized experience replay." },
    { q: "For a graph with degree matrix D and weighted adjacency matrix W, what is the unnormalized graph Laplacian used in spectral clustering?", a: ["L = D − W", "L = W − D", "L = D⁻¹W", "L = D + W"], correct: "L = D − W", explanation: "L = D − W is positive semi-definite, and its smallest eigenvectors reveal connected groups, which k-means then clusters. D⁻¹W is the random-walk transition matrix, not the Laplacian." },
    { q: "In DBSCAN, what makes a point a 'core point'?", a: ["It has at least minPts neighbors within distance ε", "It is within ε of a core point but has too few neighbors", "It is the point nearest the mean of its cluster", "It is farther than ε from every other point"], correct: "It has at least minPts neighbors within distance ε", explanation: "Clusters grow from core points. A point near a core point but lacking enough neighbors is a border point, and one reachable from no core point is noise." },
    { q: "In a store's baskets, 20% contain bread, 10% contain butter, and 5% contain both. What is the lift of the rule bread → butter?", a: ["2.5", "0.25", "0.5", "0.05"], correct: "2.5", explanation: "Confidence = 0.05 / 0.20 = 0.25; lift = confidence / P(butter) = 0.25 / 0.10 = 2.5. So bread buyers are 2.5× as likely as average to buy butter; 0.25 is just the confidence." },
    { q: "What does a Granger causality test check in time series analysis?", a: ["Whether X's past values improve forecasts of Y beyond Y's own past", "Whether X affects Y through an identifiable physical mechanism", "Whether X and Y share a common stochastic trend (cointegration)", "Whether X and Y are strongly correlated at the same time point"], correct: "Whether X's past values improve forecasts of Y beyond Y's own past", explanation: "Granger causality is predictive: if lagged X adds forecasting power for Y, X 'Granger-causes' Y. It does not establish a true causal mechanism." },
    { q: "What feature of financial returns are GARCH models designed to capture?", a: ["Volatility clustering (time-varying variance)", "Trend and seasonality, split into additive components", "Long memory in the mean, via fractional differencing", "Switches between a few discrete hidden regimes"], correct: "Volatility clustering (time-varying variance)", explanation: "GARCH models today's variance as depending on past squared shocks and past variance, so calm and turbulent periods cluster. ARMA alone assumes constant error variance." },
    { q: "How does Dynamic Time Warping (DTW) compare two sequences that run at different speeds?", a: ["Warps the time axis to find the lowest-cost alignment of points", "Pads the shorter sequence with zeros and compares point by point", "Compares the sequences' Fourier spectra instead of raw values", "Resamples both to equal length, then takes Euclidean distance"], correct: "Warps the time axis to find the lowest-cost alignment of points", explanation: "DTW uses dynamic programming to match points non-linearly, letting one part of a sequence stretch or compress. Uniform resampling can't handle a gesture that is fast in one part and slow in another." },
    { q: "How is a Self-Organizing Map (SOM) trained, and what does it preserve?", a: ["Competitive learning on a grid that preserves input topology", "Backpropagation of reconstruction error through a bottleneck", "Supervised training on labels to place class prototypes", "Gradient descent on KL divergence between neighbor distributions"], correct: "Competitive learning on a grid that preserves input topology", explanation: "The best-matching unit and its grid neighbors move toward each input, so nearby inputs land on nearby grid cells. Minimizing KL between neighbor distributions describes t-SNE." },
    { q: "In learning theory, what does Rademacher complexity measure about a hypothesis class?", a: ["How well it can fit random ±1 labels", "The largest set of points it can fully shatter", "How fast gradient descent converges within it", "How much memory it needs per training example"], correct: "How well it can fit random ±1 labels", explanation: "A class that correlates well with pure random signs is very flexible and prone to overfitting, giving looser generalization bounds. The largest shatterable set is the VC dimension, a related but different measure." },
    { q: "The Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) conditions extend the method of Lagrange multipliers to handle what?", a: ["Inequality constraints", "Non-differentiable objectives", "Integer-valued variables", "Noisy, stochastic objectives"], correct: "Inequality constraints", explanation: "Lagrange multipliers cover equality constraints; KKT adds inequality constraints via conditions like complementary slackness. They are central to deriving the SVM dual." },
    { q: "A function f is L-Lipschitz. Which statement must hold for all x and y?", a: ["|f(x) − f(y)| ≤ L·|x − y|", "|f(x) − f(y)| ≤ L·|x − y|²", "|f′(x)| ≥ L at every point", "|f(x)| ≤ L·|x| for every x"], correct: "|f(x) − f(y)| ≤ L·|x − y|", explanation: "Lipschitz continuity caps how fast f can change: output gaps are at most L times input gaps. WGANs enforce this on the critic to keep training stable." },
    { q: "What does Polyak-Ruppert averaging do in stochastic gradient descent?", a: ["Reports the average of the parameter iterates, not the last one", "Averages gradients over several mini-batches before each step", "Averages the predictions of several independently trained models", "Decays the learning rate in proportion to one over the step count"], correct: "Reports the average of the parameter iterates, not the last one", explanation: "SGD's iterates bounce around the optimum; averaging them cancels much of that noise and achieves optimal asymptotic variance. Averaging gradients across mini-batches is just gradient accumulation." },
    { q: "What is the key mechanism of simulated annealing?", a: ["It accepts some worse moves, less often as the temperature falls", "It accepts only better moves but restarts from random points", "It evolves a population and recombines the best solutions", "It bans revisiting recent solutions using a short memory list"], correct: "It accepts some worse moves, less often as the temperature falls", explanation: "Early on, high temperature lets it climb out of local minima; as it cools it behaves more greedily. Reaching the global minimum is only guaranteed with impractically slow cooling. A short ban list describes tabu search." },
    { q: "The conjugate gradient method is a classic choice for which problem?", a: ["Large sparse symmetric positive-definite linear systems", "Highly non-convex losses with many saddle points", "Noisy mini-batch training with a constant learning rate", "Small dense systems where direct inversion is cheap"], correct: "Large sparse symmetric positive-definite linear systems", explanation: "Conjugate gradient needs only matrix-vector products and, in exact arithmetic, solves an n×n SPD system in at most n steps by choosing mutually conjugate directions. For small dense systems, direct methods are simpler." },
    { q: "What does Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) do to improve generalization?", a: ["Averages weights saved late in training", "Adds Gaussian noise to the weights at every update step", "Randomly zeroes activations during each forward pass", "Keeps the checkpoint with the best validation loss"], correct: "Averages weights saved late in training", explanation: "SWA averages weights collected under a cyclical or high constant learning rate, landing in the center of a wide, flat minimum that tends to generalize better. Randomly zeroing activations is dropout." },
    { q: "Gradient-norm clipping in recurrent neural network training mainly targets which problem?", a: ["Exploding gradients", "Vanishing gradients", "Internal covariate shift", "Dying ReLU units"], correct: "Exploding gradients", explanation: "Backpropagation through time multiplies many Jacobians, so gradients can blow up; clipping rescales them when their norm passes a threshold. Clipping can't enlarge tiny gradients, so it doesn't fix vanishing ones (LSTMs/GRUs help there)." },
    { q: "What is truncated backpropagation through time (TBPTT)?", a: ["Backpropagating through only a fixed number of recent steps", "Cutting off gradients whose norm exceeds a set threshold", "Dropping recurrent connections at random during training", "Feeding the true previous token instead of the model's output"], correct: "Backpropagating through only a fixed number of recent steps", explanation: "For long sequences, TBPTT carries the hidden state forward but limits gradient flow to a fixed window, saving memory and compute. Capping gradient norms is gradient clipping; feeding true tokens is teacher forcing." },
    { q: "What is K-FAC (Kronecker-factored Approximate Curvature) in deep learning optimization?", a: ["A per-layer Kronecker-product approximation of the Fisher matrix", "A Hessian approximation built from recent gradient differences", "A penalty on loss-surface curvature that seeks flatter minima", "A curvature estimate from a running mean of squared gradients"], correct: "A per-layer Kronecker-product approximation of the Fisher matrix", explanation: "K-FAC approximates each layer's Fisher block as a Kronecker product of two small matrices, making approximate natural-gradient steps affordable. Building curvature from gradient differences is L-BFGS." },
    { q: "In a backtracking line search, what does the Armijo (sufficient decrease) condition require of a step?", a: ["The loss drops by at least a set fraction of what the slope predicts", "The slope at the new point is flatter than a fraction of the old slope", "The step lands exactly at the minimum along the search direction", "The step size has been halved a fixed number of times before moving"], correct: "The loss drops by at least a set fraction of what the slope predicts", explanation: "Armijo requires f(x + αd) ≤ f(x) + c·α·∇f·d, rejecting steps that overshoot. The flatter-slope requirement is the separate curvature (Wolfe) condition." },
    { q: "How do trust-region methods differ from line-search methods in optimization?", a: ["They fix a maximum step size first, then pick the direction within it", "They pick a direction first, then search along it for a step size", "They sample random points in a box and keep the best one found", "They need third-order derivatives to bound the model's error"], correct: "They fix a maximum step size first, then pick the direction within it", explanation: "Trust-region methods minimize a local (usually quadratic) model within a radius, then grow or shrink the radius based on how well the model predicted. Choosing a direction first is what line search does." },
    { q: "In model interpretability, what does a partial dependence plot (PDP) show for a feature?", a: ["The average prediction across the data as that feature varies", "The prediction curve for one individual as that feature varies", "The raw correlation between that feature and the target", "The drop in accuracy when that feature's values are shuffled"], correct: "The average prediction across the data as that feature varies", explanation: "A PDP sets the feature to each value for every row and averages the predictions, marginalizing over other features. A single individual's curve is an ICE plot; the shuffling approach is permutation importance." },
    { q: "When calibrating a classifier's probabilities, how does isotonic regression differ from Platt scaling?", a: ["It fits a free-form non-decreasing step function, not a sigmoid", "It fits a sigmoid curve, not a free-form step function", "It needs less calibration data because it has two parameters", "It rescales the input features rather than the output scores"], correct: "It fits a free-form non-decreasing step function, not a sigmoid", explanation: "Isotonic regression is non-parametric, so it can fix any monotonic distortion but needs more data to avoid overfitting. Platt scaling is the two-parameter sigmoid fit that works with less data." },
    { q: "In machine learning deployment, what does the model format name 'ONNX' stand for?", a: ["Open Neural Network Exchange", "Open Neural Network Execution", "Optimized Neural Network Export", "Open Numerical Network eXchange"], correct: "Open Neural Network Exchange", explanation: "ONNX is an open format for exchanging models between frameworks, e.g., training in PyTorch and serving with ONNX Runtime. The 'Exchange' reflects its goal of interoperability." },
    { q: "What problem is an MLOps feature store chiefly built to solve?", a: ["Serving identical features to both training and live inference", "Versioning trained models and promoting them to production", "Tracking hyperparameters and metrics for each training run", "Caching intermediate CNN activations for reuse across layers"], correct: "Serving identical features to both training and live inference", explanation: "A feature store computes features once and serves them offline for training and with low latency online, avoiding train-serve skew. Versioning and promoting models is a model registry's job." },
    { q: "In an MLOps stack such as MLflow, what is a model registry used for?", a: ["Versioning models and tracking stages like Staging → Production", "Computing and serving features for training and inference", "Logging each run's hyperparameters, metrics, and loss curves", "Routing live traffic between model versions for A/B tests"], correct: "Versioning models and tracking stages like Staging → Production", explanation: "A registry is the catalog of trained models with versions, stage labels, and metadata, making promotion and rollback easy. Per-run logging is experiment tracking, a separate component." },
    { q: "The UCB bandit algorithm plays the arm with the highest score. What is that score?", a: ["Its mean reward plus a bonus that shrinks as it is played more", "Its mean reward minus a penalty for its observed variance", "A random draw from its posterior reward distribution", "Its mean reward, except a random arm with probability ε"], correct: "Its mean reward plus a bonus that shrinks as it is played more", explanation: "UCB1's score is mean + √(2 ln t / n), so rarely tried arms get an optimistic boost ('optimism under uncertainty'). A random posterior draw is Thompson sampling." },
    { q: "In statistics, what does it mean for an estimator to be asymptotically efficient?", a: ["Its variance approaches the Cramér-Rao bound as n grows", "Its bias shrinks to zero as the sample size n grows", "It converges in probability to the true value as n grows", "Its sampling distribution becomes normal as n grows"], correct: "Its variance approaches the Cramér-Rao bound as n grows", explanation: "Efficiency is about variance: in large samples no regular unbiased estimator does better. Converging to the true value is consistency and approaching a normal shape is asymptotic normality; the MLE has all three under regularity conditions." },
    { q: "For a single parameter θ, the Wald test statistic is built from which quantity?", a: ["(θ̂ − θ₀) divided by the standard error of θ̂", "Twice the gap in maximized log-likelihoods", "The score at θ₀ scaled by the Fisher information", "The largest gap between two empirical CDFs"], correct: "(θ̂ − θ₀) divided by the standard error of θ̂", explanation: "Wald compares the estimate to the null value in standard-error units (or its square vs. a chi-squared). Twice the log-likelihood gap is the likelihood-ratio test; the score-based version is the score (Lagrange multiplier) test." },
    { q: "What is the key difference between ANOVA and MANOVA?", a: ["MANOVA tests several dependent variables at once", "MANOVA tests several independent factors at once", "MANOVA drops ANOVA's normality assumption", "MANOVA compares group medians rather than means"], correct: "MANOVA tests several dependent variables at once", explanation: "MANOVA compares group mean vectors on multiple outcomes, using their covariance. Several independent factors is just factorial (two-way) ANOVA, which still has one outcome." },
    { q: "In a Hidden Markov Model, what does the Viterbi algorithm compute?", a: ["The single most likely sequence of hidden states", "The probability of each hidden state at each step", "The total probability of the observations over all paths", "Maximum-likelihood transition and emission parameters"], correct: "The single most likely sequence of hidden states", explanation: "Viterbi uses dynamic programming with max instead of sum to find the best path. Summing over all paths gives the forward algorithm's likelihood, and parameter fitting is Baum-Welch." },
    { q: "Why are the forward-backward algorithm's outputs needed when training an HMM with Baum-Welch, rather than just the Viterbi path?", a: ["They give soft state probabilities at every step, used in the EM update", "They give the one best state path, used as hard labels for the update", "They give filtered state estimates from past observations only", "They give the parameters directly, so no iterative update is needed"], correct: "They give soft state probabilities at every step, used in the EM update", explanation: "Forward-backward computes the probability of each state (and transition) at each time given all observations, the expected counts that Baum-Welch's EM step needs. Using one hard best path is Viterbi training, a cruder approximation." },
    { q: "How does importance sampling estimate an expectation under p, such as a rare-event probability?", a: ["Samples from a proposal q and reweights each draw by p/q", "Accepts each draw from q with probability p/(M·q)", "Builds a chain of correlated draws via accept/reject moves", "Splits the domain into strata and samples each one separately"], correct: "Samples from a proposal q and reweights each draw by p/q", explanation: "Drawing from a q that hits the important region often, then weighting by p/q, keeps the estimate unbiased with lower variance. Accepting with probability p/(M·q) is rejection sampling." },
    { q: "How does a Feature Pyramid Network (FPN) help detectors find objects at many scales?", a: ["A top-down path merging deep, coarse features with shallow, fine ones", "Running the full network on several rescaled copies of each image", "Using only the single deepest feature map for objects of every size", "Using dilated convolutions to keep full resolution in every layer"], correct: "A top-down path merging deep, coarse features with shallow, fine ones", explanation: "FPN upsamples semantically strong deep features and adds them to high-resolution shallow features, giving strong maps at every scale in one pass. Running the network on rescaled images works but is far slower." },
    { q: "Masked Autoencoders (MAE) showed which self-supervised pretraining recipe works well for Vision Transformers?", a: ["Masking about 75% of patches and reconstructing their pixels", "Masking about 15% of patches and predicting their class tokens", "Contrasting two augmented views of each image as positives", "Predicting the rotation angle applied to each training image"], correct: "Masking about 75% of patches and reconstructing their pixels", explanation: "Images are highly redundant, so a high masking ratio prevents trivial copying from neighboring patches and forces holistic understanding. BERT's ~15% ratio suits language, which is far less redundant." },
    { q: "How do deformable convolutions adapt to object shapes?", a: ["Learned per-location offsets that shift each kernel sample point", "A fixed affine warp applied to the whole input before convolving", "Gaps between kernel taps set by a fixed dilation rate", "Parallel branches mixing kernel sizes such as 3×3 and 5×5"], correct: "Learned per-location offsets that shift each kernel sample point", explanation: "An extra layer predicts 2D offsets for each sampling point, so the receptive field bends to fit the object. Fixed dilation enlarges the grid but keeps it rigid." },
    { q: "What does a Spatial Transformer module add to a CNN?", a: ["A learned, differentiable warp (crop, rotate, scale) of its input", "A fixed augmentation that randomly rotates the training images", "An attention layer that routes tokens between transformer blocks", "A pooling layer that discards position to gain invariance"], correct: "A learned, differentiable warp (crop, rotate, scale) of its input", explanation: "A small localization network predicts transformation parameters per input, and the feature map is resampled accordingly, all trained end to end. Despite the name, it has nothing to do with Transformer attention." },
    { q: "A predicted box covers 60 px², the true box 40 px², and they overlap in 20 px². What is their Jaccard index (IoU)?", a: ["0.25", "0.33", "0.40", "0.50"], correct: "0.25", explanation: "Union = 60 + 40 − 20 = 80, so IoU = 20 / 80 = 0.25. The tempting 0.40 is the Dice coefficient, 2 × 20 / (60 + 40)." },
    { q: "In TF-IDF, what is sublinear TF scaling (using 1 + log(tf)) meant to do?", a: ["Dampen repeats, so 20 mentions don't count 20 times as much", "Downweight terms that appear in many documents of the corpus", "Normalize for length so long documents aren't favored", "Turn raw term counts into probabilities that sum to one"], correct: "Dampen repeats, so 20 mentions don't count 20 times as much", explanation: "With a log, each extra occurrence adds less, so keyword stuffing has diminishing returns. Downweighting terms common across documents is the IDF part, not TF scaling." },
    { q: "What distinguishes ELMo (Embeddings from Language Models) word representations from Word2Vec or GloVe vectors?", a: ["They are context-dependent, built from the layers of a pre-trained bidirectional LSTM", "They are fixed per word, learned from global co-occurrence counts over a corpus", "They are fixed per word, built by summing vectors of the word's character n-grams", "They are context-dependent, built from a transformer trained with masked tokens"], correct: "They are context-dependent, built from the layers of a pre-trained bidirectional LSTM", explanation: "ELMo gives 'bank' different vectors in 'river bank' and 'bank loan' by combining internal states of a deep biLSTM language model. Masked-token transformers describe BERT, not ELMo; fixed vectors from co-occurrence or n-grams describe GloVe and fastText." },
    { q: "Which text-generation metric compares candidate and reference texts using cosine similarity between contextual embeddings from a pre-trained transformer, rather than exact n-gram overlap?", a: ["BLEU", "METEOR", "ROUGE-L", "BERTScore"], correct: "BERTScore", explanation: "BERTScore matches tokens by embedding similarity, so valid paraphrases still score well. BLEU and ROUGE rely on n-gram or subsequence overlap, and METEOR adds stemming and synonym matching but still works on surface tokens." },
    { q: "Compared with PCA, what kind of representation does Non-negative Matrix Factorization (NMF) tend to learn?", a: ["Additive, parts-based components because factors cannot be negative", "Orthogonal components ordered by how much variance each explains", "Statistically independent components found by maximizing non-Gaussianity", "Sparse binary codes found by thresholding the leading eigenvectors"], correct: "Additive, parts-based components because factors cannot be negative", explanation: "Because NMF only allows non-negative weights and factors, parts can only be added together (e.g. eyes, nose, mouth in faces). Orthogonal variance-ordered components describe PCA; maximizing non-Gaussianity describes ICA." },
    { q: "During neural network training, what is the typical symptom of the 'exploding gradient' problem?", a: ["Loss stays flat because early-layer gradients shrink toward zero", "Loss spikes or turns into NaN as weight updates grow huge and unstable", "Training loss keeps falling while validation loss starts to rise", "Many ReLU units output zero for every input and stop learning"], correct: "Loss spikes or turns into NaN as weight updates grow huge and unstable", explanation: "Repeated multiplication by large Jacobians makes gradients grow exponentially, causing overflow and wild updates; gradient clipping is a common fix. A flat loss from shrinking gradients is the vanishing-gradient problem, and the train/validation gap is overfitting." },
    { q: "In PAC (Probably Approximately Correct) learning, what does the framework bound?", a: ["The chance that the learned hypothesis has generalization error above ε, given m samples", "The number of gradient steps needed for the training loss to drop below a tolerance ε", "The gap between training loss and validation loss after cross-validation with k folds", "The worst-case training error of the best hypothesis in the class on any dataset"], correct: "The chance that the learned hypothesis has generalization error above ε, given m samples", explanation: "PAC learning asks how many samples are needed so that, with probability at least 1 − δ, the learner's true error is at most ε. It is about generalization from samples, not optimization speed or cross-validation gaps." },
    { q: "For a decision-tree node with two classes, what is the maximum possible value of Gini impurity, and of entropy (in bits)?", a: ["Gini 0.5 and entropy 1", "Gini 1 and entropy 0.5", "Gini 1 and entropy 1", "Gini 0.25 and entropy 1"], correct: "Gini 0.5 and entropy 1", explanation: "With two classes at 50/50, Gini = 1 − (0.5² + 0.5²) = 0.5 and entropy = −2 × 0.5 log₂ 0.5 = 1 bit. The value 0.25 is p(1 − p) at p = 0.5, which is half the Gini impurity." },
    { q: "How does permutation feature importance measure how much a model relies on a feature?", a: ["By shuffling that feature's values and measuring how much the model's error rises", "By dropping that feature, retraining the model, and measuring the change in error", "By averaging that feature's marginal contribution across all feature coalitions", "By totaling the impurity reduction from every tree split made on that feature"], correct: "By shuffling that feature's values and measuring how much the model's error rises", explanation: "Shuffling breaks the link between the feature and the target without retraining; a big error increase means the model depends on it. Retraining without the feature is drop-column importance, coalitions describe SHAP, and impurity totals are tree-specific (MDI) importance." },
    { q: "In deep learning optimization, what is 'learning rate warmup'?", a: ["Starting with a tiny learning rate and ramping it up over the first steps", "Starting with a large learning rate and decaying it along a cosine curve", "Repeatedly resetting the learning rate to its peak after each training cycle", "Searching over learning rates on a short run before the real training begins"], correct: "Starting with a tiny learning rate and ramping it up over the first steps", explanation: "Early in training, randomly initialized weights and unreliable optimizer statistics (e.g. Adam's) can make large steps diverge, so warmup ramps the rate up gradually. Cosine decay and warm restarts are schedules for later in training; a short search is an LR range test." },
    { q: "In Bayesian inference, what is the main benefit of choosing a conjugate prior?", a: ["The posterior is in the same family as the prior, so updates have a closed form", "The posterior no longer depends on the prior once the sample is moderately large", "The prior adds no information, so the posterior mode equals the MLE exactly", "The marginal likelihood becomes constant, so model comparison is unnecessary"], correct: "The posterior is in the same family as the prior, so updates have a closed form", explanation: "A Beta prior with a Binomial likelihood gives a Beta posterior, so you just update the parameters with no numerical integration or MCMC. A prior adding no information describes a flat or non-informative prior, which is a different idea." },
    { q: "In nonparametric statistics, what is a U-statistic?", a: ["An average of a symmetric kernel over all subsets of the sample of a fixed size", "A sum of the ranks of one sample after pooling it with a second sample and sorting", "A maximum gap between an empirical distribution function and a reference one", "A ratio of between-group to within-group variance computed on the ranked data"], correct: "An average of a symmetric kernel over all subsets of the sample of a fixed size", explanation: "U-statistics (Hoeffding) are unbiased estimators built by averaging a kernel over every size-r subset; the sample variance and the Mann-Whitney U are examples. The maximum gap describes the Kolmogorov-Smirnov statistic, and the rank-sum is the Wilcoxon statistic form." },
    { q: "What does bootstrapping do in statistics?", a: ["Resamples the data with replacement to estimate a statistic's sampling distribution", "Resamples the data without replacement to build a null distribution for a test", "Leaves out one observation at a time to estimate a statistic's bias and variance", "Simulates new data from a fitted normal model to estimate a statistic's error"], correct: "Resamples the data with replacement to estimate a statistic's sampling distribution", explanation: "The bootstrap draws many same-size samples with replacement from the observed data to approximate standard errors and confidence intervals. Shuffling without replacement is a permutation test, leave-one-out is the jackknife, and simulating from a fitted model is a parametric bootstrap." },
    { q: "In Hadoop and Spark, what does 'data locality' refer to?", a: ["Scheduling tasks on the nodes that already store the needed data blocks", "Caching intermediate datasets in executor memory between pipeline stages", "Copying every data block to all nodes so any task can run anywhere", "Sending a small lookup table to every worker to avoid a shuffle join"], correct: "Scheduling tasks on the nodes that already store the needed data blocks", explanation: "Moving computation to the data avoids sending large blocks over the network, which is often the bottleneck. In-memory caching is Spark persistence, and sending a small table to every worker is a broadcast join." },
    { q: "Why is the Parquet file format well suited to analytic queries over large datasets?", a: ["It is row-oriented, so whole records can be appended and read back quickly", "It is columnar, so queries read only needed columns and compress them well", "It is plain text, so any tool can read it without a separate schema file", "It is a transaction log, so many concurrent writers can update the same rows"], correct: "It is columnar, so queries read only needed columns and compress them well", explanation: "Parquet stores each column contiguously with per-chunk statistics, enabling column pruning, predicate pushdown and strong compression. Row-oriented formats like Avro or CSV favor whole-record writes and reads instead." },
    { q: "In the Expectation-Maximization (EM) algorithm, what does each M-step maximize?", a: ["The expected complete-data log-likelihood under the E-step posterior", "The observed-data log-likelihood directly, by setting its gradient to zero", "The posterior probability of each cluster label for each data point", "The margin between the closest points of different latent classes"], correct: "The expected complete-data log-likelihood under the E-step posterior", explanation: "The E-step uses current parameters to form the expected complete-data log-likelihood under the posterior over latent variables, and the M-step maximizes it; since this also raises the ELBO (via Jensen's inequality), the true likelihood never decreases. Computing cluster membership probabilities is the E-step, not the M-step." },
    { q: "A population is strongly right-skewed with finite variance. By the Central Limit Theorem, what happens to the distribution of the sample mean as n grows?", a: ["It approaches a normal distribution, despite the skewed population", "It keeps the population's right skew but becomes narrower as n grows", "It approaches a normal distribution only if the population is symmetric", "It approaches the population's own distribution, reaching it at large n"], correct: "It approaches a normal distribution, despite the skewed population", explanation: "The CLT says the sampling distribution of the mean tends toward normal for any population with finite variance, though very skewed populations need larger n. The skew of the sample mean shrinks roughly like 1/√n rather than persisting." },
    { q: "What does Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE) choose as its parameter estimate?", a: ["The value that makes the observed data most probable under the model", "The value that is most probable given the data and a chosen prior", "The value whose sampling distribution has the smallest possible variance", "The value that makes the sample moments equal the theoretical moments"], correct: "The value that makes the observed data most probable under the model", explanation: "MLE maximizes the likelihood p(data | θ). Maximizing the posterior with a prior is MAP estimation, and matching moments is the method of moments." },
    { q: "A drug trial concludes the drug works, but in truth it has no effect. What kind of error is this?", a: ["Type I error (a false positive)", "Type II error (a false negative)", "Type I error (a false negative)", "Type II error (a false positive)"], correct: "Type I error (a false positive)", explanation: "Rejecting a true null hypothesis ('no effect') is a Type I error, a false positive, whose rate is controlled by α. A Type II error is failing to detect a real effect." },
    { q: "For n independent Bernoulli(p) trials, which statistic is sufficient for p?", a: ["The total number of successes", "The order in which successes occurred", "The length of the longest run of successes", "The outcome of the first trial alone"], correct: "The total number of successes", explanation: "By the Fisher-Neyman factorization, the likelihood p^k (1 − p)^(n − k) depends on the data only through k, the count of successes, so the order and run lengths add no information about p." },
    { q: "In a residuals-vs-fitted plot, the spread of residuals fans out wider as fitted values increase. What does this indicate?", a: ["Heteroscedasticity", "Multicollinearity", "Autocorrelation", "High leverage"], correct: "Heteroscedasticity", explanation: "A funnel shape means the error variance is not constant, violating an OLS assumption and making standard errors unreliable. Multicollinearity concerns correlated predictors and autocorrelation concerns errors correlated over time or order." },
    { q: "In Bayesian inference, how is the posterior distribution of a parameter obtained?", a: ["Prior × likelihood, then normalized", "Likelihood ÷ prior, then normalized", "Prior + likelihood, then normalized", "Likelihood alone, with no prior used"], correct: "Prior × likelihood, then normalized", explanation: "Bayes' theorem gives posterior ∝ likelihood × prior, divided by the marginal likelihood so it integrates to 1. Using the likelihood alone is what MLE does." },
    { q: "A test gives p = 0.03. Which statement correctly interprets this p-value?", a: ["If H₀ were true, results this extreme or more would occur 3% of the time", "Given these results, there is a 3% probability that H₀ is actually true", "Given these results, there is a 97% probability that H₁ is actually true", "If the study were repeated, the result would fail to replicate 3% of the time"], correct: "If H₀ were true, results this extreme or more would occur 3% of the time", explanation: "A p-value is P(data at least this extreme | H₀), computed assuming H₀. It is not the probability that H₀ is true, a common misconception that confuses it with a posterior probability." },
    { q: "What is the main practical consequence of severe multicollinearity in a linear regression?", a: ["Coefficient estimates become unstable with inflated standard errors", "Predictions on new data become biased even within the observed range", "The R² of the model drops sharply compared with a model with one predictor", "The residuals become correlated with each other across observations"], correct: "Coefficient estimates become unstable with inflated standard errors", explanation: "When predictors are highly correlated, the model cannot separate their effects, so individual coefficients swing wildly and have large variance (measured by VIF). Overall fit and predictions can remain fine; correlated residuals are autocorrelation." },
    { q: "For a Poisson distribution with mean λ = 4, what is the variance?", a: ["4", "2", "16", "8"], correct: "4", explanation: "A defining property of the Poisson distribution is that its mean and variance are both λ. The value 2 is the standard deviation, √4, and 16 is λ²." },
    { q: "A study has statistical power of 0.80. What does that mean?", a: ["If the real effect exists, the test rejects H₀ with probability 0.80", "If H₀ is true, the test correctly fails to reject it with probability 0.80", "If the test rejects H₀, the real effect exists with probability 0.80", "If the test is repeated, it reaches the same verdict with probability 0.80"], correct: "If the real effect exists, the test rejects H₀ with probability 0.80", explanation: "Power = 1 − β, the probability of detecting a true effect of the assumed size; here the Type II error rate is 0.20. Correctly retaining a true H₀ has probability 1 − α, not power." },
    { q: "When is a standard likelihood ratio test appropriate for comparing two models?", a: ["When one model is a restricted special case of the other", "When the models use entirely different sets of predictors", "When the models are fit to different subsets of the data", "When the models use different response distributions"], correct: "When one model is a restricted special case of the other", explanation: "The LRT compares nested models; under H₀, −2 log(L₀/L₁) is approximately chi-square with df equal to the number of extra parameters. Non-nested models are usually compared with AIC or BIC instead." },
    { q: "Which statement best describes the Law of Large Numbers?", a: ["The sample mean converges to the population mean as n grows", "The sample mean's distribution approaches a normal shape as n grows", "After a run of heads, tails becomes more likely so the average balances", "The sample variance converges to zero as the sample size n grows"], correct: "The sample mean converges to the population mean as n grows", explanation: "The LLN says averages settle near the expected value with more data. The normal shape is the Central Limit Theorem, and the 'balancing' idea is the gambler's fallacy; independent flips have no memory." },
    { q: "The sample variance formula divides by n − 1 rather than n. Why?", a: ["Dividing by n − 1 makes the estimator unbiased for the population variance", "Dividing by n − 1 gives the estimator the smallest possible mean squared error", "Dividing by n − 1 makes the estimator equal the maximum likelihood estimate", "Dividing by n − 1 makes the estimator robust to outliers in small samples"], correct: "Dividing by n − 1 makes the estimator unbiased for the population variance", explanation: "Using the sample mean uses up one degree of freedom, so dividing by n underestimates σ² on average; n − 1 (Bessel's correction) makes its expected value equal σ². The normal-model MLE actually divides by n." },
    { q: "What does '95% confidence' mean for a frequentist confidence interval?", a: ["95% of intervals built this way across repeated samples contain the parameter", "There is a 95% probability the parameter lies inside this particular interval", "95% of the individual observations in the sample fall inside the interval", "A repeat of the study would give a sample mean inside this interval 95% of the time"], correct: "95% of intervals built this way across repeated samples contain the parameter", explanation: "The 95% describes the long-run success rate of the procedure; any single computed interval either contains the fixed parameter or not. A direct probability statement about the parameter is what a Bayesian credible interval gives." },
    { q: "Which set of conditions on the errors does the Gauss-Markov theorem require for OLS to be the best linear unbiased estimator?", a: ["Mean zero, constant variance, and no correlation between errors", "Normally distributed, constant variance, and no correlation between errors", "Mean zero, variance proportional to x, and no correlation between errors", "Normally distributed, mean zero, and correlated with each predictor"], correct: "Mean zero, constant variance, and no correlation between errors", explanation: "Gauss-Markov needs zero-mean, homoscedastic, uncorrelated errors (plus exogenous predictors); normality is not required for BLUE, only for exact small-sample t and F tests. Errors correlated with predictors would bias OLS." },
    { q: "Height in centimeters and weight in kilograms have covariance 50. If height is converted to meters, what happens to the covariance?", a: ["It becomes 0.5", "It stays at 50", "It becomes 5000", "It becomes 0.05"], correct: "It becomes 0.5", explanation: "Covariance scales with the units of each variable: dividing height by 100 divides the covariance by 100, giving 0.5. This scale dependence is why correlation, which is unit-free and would stay unchanged, is used to compare strength." },
    { q: "Data follow y = x² for x from −3 to 3 exactly. What is the Pearson correlation between x and y?", a: ["0", "1", "−1", "0.5"], correct: "0", explanation: "Pearson's r measures only linear association. The symmetric U-shape has no linear trend, so r = 0 even though y is perfectly determined by x; zero correlation does not imply independence." },
    { q: "Insurance claim counts have a sample mean of 3 and a sample variance of 11. Which model is usually a better fit than a plain Poisson?", a: ["Negative binomial", "Binomial", "Geometric with p = 0.75", "Exponential"], correct: "Negative binomial", explanation: "A Poisson forces variance = mean, so variance far above the mean is overdispersion; the negative binomial has an extra parameter that allows variance > mean. A binomial has variance below its mean, and the exponential is for continuous waiting times, not counts." },
    { q: "In principal component analysis (PCA), how is the first principal component chosen?", a: ["As the direction along which the projected data have the largest variance", "As the direction that best separates the known class labels from each other", "As the original feature that has the largest variance before any scaling", "As the direction along which the data look the least like a normal distribution"], correct: "As the direction along which the projected data have the largest variance", explanation: "PC1 is the unit vector (the top eigenvector of the covariance matrix) that maximizes the variance of the projections. Maximizing class separation is LDA, and seeking non-Gaussian directions is ICA; PCs are combinations of features, not single original features." },
    { q: "Which situation violates the 'identically distributed' part of the iid assumption?", a: ["Pooling readings from two sensors that have different noise levels", "Recording daily temperatures where each day depends on the day before", "Drawing cards one at a time from a deck without putting them back", "Rolling one fair die many times and recording every roll separately"], correct: "Pooling readings from two sensors that have different noise levels", explanation: "Different noise levels mean the readings come from different distributions, breaking 'identically distributed'. Day-to-day dependence and drawing without replacement mainly break independence; repeated fair die rolls are a classic iid example." },
    { q: "Which of these is a nonparametric method?", a: ["Mann-Whitney U test", "Welch's two-sample t-test", "Poisson regression", "Linear discriminant analysis"], correct: "Mann-Whitney U test", explanation: "Nonparametric methods avoid assuming a specific distributional family; Mann-Whitney compares two groups using ranks. Welch's t-test assumes approximate normality, Poisson regression assumes Poisson counts, and LDA assumes Gaussian classes with shared covariance." },
    { q: "On a normal Q-Q plot, the points follow the line in the middle but curve away at both ends in an S-shape, with extreme sample values more extreme than expected. What does this suggest?", a: ["The data have heavier tails than a normal distribution", "The data have lighter tails than a normal distribution", "The data are right-skewed with a single long upper tail", "The data are normal but have a larger variance than assumed"], correct: "The data have heavier tails than a normal distribution", explanation: "When extreme sample quantiles sit further out than normal quantiles predict, the tails are heavier (as in a t-distribution). A larger variance alone would make a straight line with a steeper slope, and skew shows as curvature on one side only." },
    { q: "What does the Kaplan-Meier estimator estimate from time-to-event data with censoring?", a: ["The survival function S(t)", "The hazard ratio between groups", "The cumulative hazard via Nelson-Aalen", "The median follow-up time"], correct: "The survival function S(t)", explanation: "Kaplan-Meier is a product-limit estimator of the probability of surviving past time t, using censored subjects while they are at risk. Hazard ratios come from Cox regression, and the Nelson-Aalen estimator targets the cumulative hazard." },
    { q: "In a 5-year cancer study, a patient is still alive when the study ends. How is this observation treated in survival analysis?", a: ["As right-censored at 5 years", "As left-censored at 5 years", "As a death at 5 years", "As missing and dropped"], correct: "As right-censored at 5 years", explanation: "We know only that the event time exceeds 5 years, which is right censoring. Treating it as an event or dropping it would bias survival estimates; left censoring means the event happened before observation began." },
    { q: "A company tests whether a new website layout changes the conversion rate. What is the null hypothesis?", a: ["The new layout does not change the conversion rate", "The new layout changes the conversion rate", "The new layout increases the conversion rate", "The observed difference is statistically significant"], correct: "The new layout does not change the conversion rate", explanation: "The null is the default 'no effect' claim that the data must provide evidence against. 'Changes' is the two-sided alternative and 'increases' is a one-sided alternative; significance is a test outcome, not a hypothesis." },
    { q: "As model complexity increases (e.g. raising polynomial degree), how do bias and variance typically change?", a: ["Bias decreases while variance increases", "Bias increases while variance decreases", "Both bias and variance decrease together", "Both bias and variance increase together"], correct: "Bias decreases while variance increases", explanation: "Flexible models fit the training data closely (low bias) but change a lot between samples (high variance); simple models do the reverse. Test error is often U-shaped, minimized at an intermediate complexity." },
    { q: "A logistic regression has coefficient 0.69 for a predictor. Holding other variables fixed, what does a one-unit increase in that predictor do?", a: ["Roughly doubles the odds of the outcome", "Raises the probability by 0.69 points", "Raises the probability by 69 percent", "Raises the odds by 0.69 additively"], correct: "Roughly doubles the odds of the outcome", explanation: "Logistic coefficients are on the log-odds scale, so the odds multiply by e^0.69 ≈ 2. The effect on probability itself is not constant; it depends on the starting probability." },
    { q: "What is the key practical difference between Lasso (L1) and Ridge (L2) regularization?", a: ["Lasso can shrink coefficients exactly to zero; Ridge shrinks them but keeps them nonzero", "Ridge can shrink coefficients exactly to zero; Lasso shrinks them but keeps them nonzero", "Lasso penalizes the squared coefficients; Ridge penalizes the absolute coefficients", "Lasso leaves the intercept unpenalized; Ridge penalizes the intercept with the rest"], correct: "Lasso can shrink coefficients exactly to zero; Ridge shrinks them but keeps them nonzero", explanation: "The L1 penalty's corners make exact zeros possible, so Lasso performs variable selection; the smooth L2 penalty only shrinks. Lasso uses absolute values and Ridge uses squares, the reverse of the third option." },
    { q: "What is the Markov property of a stochastic process?", a: ["The next state depends only on the current state, not on earlier history", "The next state is independent of both the current state and earlier history", "The next state depends on a fixed window of the k most recent states only", "The next state's distribution is identical to the long-run stationary one"], correct: "The next state depends only on the current state, not on earlier history", explanation: "Memorylessness given the present defines a (first-order) Markov chain. Full independence is an iid sequence, and a k-step window is a higher-order Markov model that can be recast as first-order on expanded states." },
    { q: "Why are MCMC (Markov Chain Monte Carlo) methods used in Bayesian statistics?", a: ["To draw samples from a posterior that can't be computed in closed form", "To find the single posterior mode quickly by following the gradient uphill", "To choose a conjugate prior so the posterior can be written in closed form", "To split data into folds to estimate a model's out-of-sample accuracy"], correct: "To draw samples from a posterior that can't be computed in closed form", explanation: "MCMC builds a Markov chain whose stationary distribution is the posterior, so its draws approximate posterior expectations without the normalizing constant. Gradient ascent to the mode gives a MAP estimate, not samples." },
    { q: "Monthly ice-cream sales show a strong positive autocorrelation at lag 12. What does this suggest?", a: ["A yearly seasonal pattern in the series", "A steady upward trend with no seasonality", "A random walk with no repeating structure", "Sales strongly correlated with temperature"], correct: "A yearly seasonal pattern in the series", explanation: "Autocorrelation is a series' correlation with its own lagged values; a spike at lag 12 in monthly data means each month resembles the same month a year earlier. Correlation with temperature is cross-correlation with a different variable." },
    { q: "Which time series is (weakly) stationary?", a: ["White noise with mean 0 and constant variance", "A random walk built from cumulative sums of noise", "A series with a steady upward linear trend", "A series whose variance grows over time"], correct: "White noise with mean 0 and constant variance", explanation: "Weak stationarity needs constant mean, constant variance, and autocovariance depending only on lag. A random walk's variance grows with time, a trend changes the mean, and growing variance violates the constant-variance condition." },
    { q: "Model A has AIC 210 and Model B has AIC 198 on the same data. How should this be read?", a: ["Model B is preferred, as lower AIC is better", "Model A is preferred, as higher AIC is better", "Model B explains 12% more of the variance", "Neither, since AIC needs nested models"], correct: "Model B is preferred, as lower AIC is better", explanation: "AIC = 2k − 2 ln L rewards fit and penalizes parameters; the lower value is preferred. AIC differences are not variance percentages, and unlike the likelihood ratio test, AIC can compare non-nested models." },
    { q: "A chi-square test of independence is run on a 3 × 4 contingency table. How many degrees of freedom does it have?", a: ["6", "12", "7", "11"], correct: "6", explanation: "df = (rows − 1) × (columns − 1) = 2 × 3 = 6, the number of cells free to vary once row and column totals are fixed. 12 is the cell count and 11 is cells minus one." },
    { q: "In a two-group permutation test, how is the null distribution of the test statistic generated?", a: ["By repeatedly shuffling the group labels and recomputing the statistic", "By repeatedly resampling each group with replacement and recomputing", "By assuming the statistic follows a t-distribution with n − 2 df", "By repeatedly simulating both groups from a fitted normal distribution"], correct: "By repeatedly shuffling the group labels and recomputing the statistic", explanation: "Under H₀ the labels are exchangeable, so reshuffling them shows how big the statistic gets by chance alone. Resampling with replacement is bootstrapping, and a t-distribution or simulated normal data are parametric assumptions." },
    { q: "For the sample {2, 5, 5, 8}, what is the value of the empirical CDF at x = 5?", a: ["0.75", "0.5", "0.25", "1.0"], correct: "0.75", explanation: "The ECDF at x is the fraction of observations ≤ x: three of the four values (2, 5, 5) are ≤ 5, giving 0.75. The ECDF is a step function that jumps by 1/n at each data point, here by 0.5 at the tied value 5." },
    { q: "Treatment A has a higher success rate than B for both mild and severe cases, yet B looks better when all cases are pooled. What is this called?", a: ["Simpson's paradox", "Berkson's paradox", "Regression to the mean", "The ecological fallacy"], correct: "Simpson's paradox", explanation: "A trend that reverses on aggregation happens when a lurking variable (here severity) is unevenly distributed across treatments. Berkson's paradox is a spurious correlation from selection, and the ecological fallacy infers individual traits from group data." },
    { q: "A survey splits a city into districts and randomly samples residents within every district. What is this design?", a: ["Stratified sampling", "Cluster sampling", "Systematic sampling", "Quota sampling"], correct: "Stratified sampling", explanation: "Stratified sampling draws a random sample within each group, improving precision and representation. Cluster sampling would randomly pick some districts and survey them, and quota sampling fills group targets non-randomly." },
    { q: "In a mixed model of test scores for students nested in schools, what does a random intercept for school capture?", a: ["School-to-school variation in baseline scores, treated as draws from a distribution", "A separate fixed intercept estimated for each school, with no shared distribution", "Random measurement error in each individual student's test score", "The average effect of school size on students' test scores across all schools"], correct: "School-to-school variation in baseline scores, treated as draws from a distribution", explanation: "A random effect models group deviations as draws from a common (usually normal) distribution, which accounts for correlation among students in the same school and allows partial pooling. Separate fixed intercepts would be a fixed-effects (dummy variable) approach." },
    { q: "In a chi-square test of independence, how is the expected count for each cell calculated?", a: ["Row total × column total ÷ grand total", "Row total × column total ÷ number of cells", "Grand total ÷ number of cells in the table", "Row total ÷ column total × grand total"], correct: "Row total × column total ÷ grand total", explanation: "Under independence, P(row and column) = P(row) × P(column), so the expected count is (row total × column total) / N. Dividing N evenly among cells would assume uniform, not independent, margins." },
    { q: "How does a likelihood function L(θ) differ from a probability density f(x | θ)?", a: ["Same formula, but viewed as a function of θ with data fixed; need not integrate to 1", "Same formula, but viewed as a function of x with θ fixed; must integrate to 1 over x", "It is the posterior of θ, so it must integrate to 1 over θ, unlike the density", "It is the cumulative form of the density, giving P(X ≤ x) at each value of θ"], correct: "Same formula, but viewed as a function of θ with data fixed; need not integrate to 1", explanation: "The likelihood reuses the density formula but treats the observed data as fixed and the parameter as variable, so it is not a probability distribution over θ. Turning it into one requires a prior and normalization, which gives the posterior." },
    { q: "In 5-fold cross-validation, how many times is the model trained, and on what share of the data each time?", a: ["5 times, each on 80% of the data", "5 times, each on 20% of the data", "4 times, each on 80% of the data", "1 time, on 80% of the data"], correct: "5 times, each on 80% of the data", explanation: "Each fold serves once as the validation set (20%) while the other four folds (80%) are used for training, and the 5 scores are averaged. A single 80/20 split is just a holdout set." },
    { q: "A regression predicts a house price of $310,000, but the house sold for $295,000. What is the residual?", a: ["−$15,000", "+$15,000", "−$310,000", "+$295,000"], correct: "−$15,000", explanation: "Residual = observed − predicted = 295,000 − 310,000 = −15,000. A negative residual means the model overestimated; the sign flips if you mistakenly compute predicted − observed." },
    { q: "In survival analysis, what does the hazard function h(t) describe?", a: ["The instantaneous event rate at t among those who survived to t", "The probability of surviving beyond time t from the study's start", "The probability that the event has occurred by time t overall", "The expected remaining lifetime for a subject still alive at t"], correct: "The instantaneous event rate at t among those who survived to t", explanation: "The hazard is a conditional rate, h(t) = f(t) / S(t). Surviving beyond t is S(t), occurring by t is the CDF F(t), and expected remaining lifetime is the mean residual life." },
    { q: "Which combination of response distribution and link function corresponds to standard Poisson regression as a GLM?", a: ["Poisson response with a log link", "Poisson response with a logit link", "Poisson response with an identity link", "Gaussian response with a log link"], correct: "Poisson response with a log link", explanation: "Poisson regression models log(E[Y]) as linear in predictors, keeping predicted counts positive. Logit is the canonical link for binomial (logistic) regression, and identity is the canonical link for Gaussian linear regression." },
    { q: "In the Box-Cox transformation, which value of λ corresponds to taking the natural log of the data?", a: ["λ = 0", "λ = 1", "λ = 0.5", "λ = −1"], correct: "λ = 0", explanation: "Box-Cox uses (y^λ − 1)/λ, which tends to ln y as λ → 0. λ = 1 leaves the shape unchanged, λ = 0.5 is a square-root-type transform, and λ = −1 is a reciprocal transform; it requires positive data." },
    { q: "In Bayesian statistics, what does it mean for a sequence of observations to be exchangeable?", a: ["Their joint distribution is unchanged by any reordering of the observations", "Their individual values are mutually independent of each other in every case", "Their joint distribution is unchanged by shifting all observations in time", "Their marginal distributions all share the same variance, though means may differ"], correct: "Their joint distribution is unchanged by any reordering of the observations", explanation: "Exchangeability is permutation invariance; iid implies it, but exchangeable variables can be dependent. De Finetti's theorem says an infinite exchangeable sequence is iid conditional on some parameter. Invariance to time shifts is stationarity." },
    { q: "With 1,000 bootstrap estimates of a statistic, how does the percentile method form a 95% confidence interval?", a: ["Take the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the bootstrap estimates", "Take the 5th and 95th percentiles of the bootstrap estimates", "Take the original estimate ± 1.96 times the bootstrap mean", "Take the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the original data"], correct: "Take the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the bootstrap estimates", explanation: "The percentile interval uses the middle 95% of the bootstrap distribution, cutting 2.5% from each tail. The 5th and 95th percentiles would give a 90% interval, and percentiles of the raw data describe spread of observations, not of the estimate." },
    { q: "What is plotted on the two axes of a ROC curve?", a: ["True positive rate vs. false positive rate", "Precision vs. recall across all thresholds", "True positive rate vs. true negative rate", "Accuracy vs. the classification threshold"], correct: "True positive rate vs. false positive rate", explanation: "A ROC curve plots sensitivity (TPR) against 1 − specificity (FPR) as the threshold varies. Precision vs. recall is the PR curve, which is often more informative for heavily imbalanced data." },
    { q: "A classifier has AUC = 0.80. What is a correct interpretation?", a: ["A random positive outscores a random negative 80% of the time", "The classifier labels 80% of all test examples correctly", "At the default threshold, the true positive rate is 0.80", "Of the cases predicted positive, 80% are actually positive"], correct: "A random positive outscores a random negative 80% of the time", explanation: "AUC equals the probability that a randomly chosen positive gets a higher score than a randomly chosen negative; 0.5 is chance. Accuracy, TPR at a threshold, and precision are different metrics." },
    { q: "Which is a consequence of the 'curse of dimensionality' for methods like k-nearest neighbors?", a: ["Distances between points become nearly equal, so neighbors are less meaningful", "Distances between points shrink toward zero, so every point becomes a neighbor", "Data become more densely packed, so fewer samples are needed to cover the space", "Classes become linearly separable, so nearest-neighbor methods stop being needed"], correct: "Distances between points become nearly equal, so neighbors are less meaningful", explanation: "In high dimensions data are sparse and the ratio of nearest to farthest distance approaches 1, weakening distance-based methods. Covering the space actually needs exponentially more samples, not fewer." },
    { q: "Which of these is an example of a latent variable?", a: ["General intelligence inferred from scores on several tests", "Household income reported directly on a survey form", "A student's score on one final math exam", "A patient's blood pressure from a cuff reading"], correct: "General intelligence inferred from scores on several tests", explanation: "A latent variable is not observed directly but inferred from observed indicators, as in factor analysis. Reported income, an exam score, and a blood-pressure reading are all directly measured (even if noisily)." },
    { q: "How does exploratory factor analysis differ from PCA?", a: ["It models shared variance via latent factors plus unique error per variable", "It finds orthogonal directions that capture as much total variance as possible", "It requires class labels and finds directions that best separate the classes", "It finds components that are statistically independent and non-Gaussian"], correct: "It models shared variance via latent factors plus unique error per variable", explanation: "Factor analysis assumes each observed variable = loadings × common factors + its own unique error, explaining correlations among variables. Capturing total variance is PCA, class separation is LDA, and independent non-Gaussian components is ICA." },
    { q: "In Bayesian analysis, how does the posterior predictive distribution forecast a new observation?", a: ["It averages the predictive distribution over the posterior of the parameters", "It plugs the single posterior mean of the parameters into the likelihood", "It averages the predictive distribution over the prior of the parameters", "It plugs the maximum likelihood estimate into the sampling distribution"], correct: "It averages the predictive distribution over the posterior of the parameters", explanation: "p(ỹ | data) = ∫ p(ỹ | θ) p(θ | data) dθ, so it carries parameter uncertainty and is wider than a plug-in forecast. Averaging over the prior instead gives the prior predictive distribution." },
    { q: "A regression's residuals give a Durbin-Watson statistic of about 0.6. What does this suggest?", a: ["Positive autocorrelation in the residuals", "Negative autocorrelation in the residuals", "No autocorrelation in the residuals", "Non-constant variance in the residuals"], correct: "Positive autocorrelation in the residuals", explanation: "The Durbin-Watson statistic ranges from 0 to 4: about 2 means no first-order autocorrelation, values well below 2 indicate positive autocorrelation, and values near 4 indicate negative. It does not test heteroscedasticity." },
    { q: "A study finds that coffee drinkers have higher rates of lung cancer, but coffee drinkers also smoke more. In this setting, what is smoking?", a: ["A confounder", "A mediator", "A collider", "An instrumental variable"], correct: "A confounder", explanation: "Smoking is associated with both coffee drinking and lung cancer, creating a spurious association unless adjusted for. A mediator lies on the causal path from exposure to outcome, and a collider is caused by both." },
    { q: "In propensity score matching, what is the propensity score?", a: ["The probability of receiving treatment given the observed covariates", "The probability of a good outcome given the treatment and covariates", "The estimated treatment effect for each individual in the study", "The distance between a treated unit and its closest untreated match"], correct: "The probability of receiving treatment given the observed covariates", explanation: "Matching treated and untreated units with similar propensity scores balances observed covariates, reducing confounding in observational studies. It cannot fix confounding from unobserved variables, and it models treatment assignment, not the outcome." },
    { q: "If a device's lifetime T follows an exponential distribution with rate λ, what is its survival function S(t)?", a: ["e^(−λt)", "1 − e^(−λt)", "λe^(−λt)", "λ"], correct: "e^(−λt)", explanation: "S(t) = P(T > t) = e^(−λt). 1 − e^(−λt) is the CDF (failure by t), λe^(−λt) is the density, and the constant λ is the exponential's hazard rate." },
    { q: "In a logistic regression viewed as a GLM, what does the logit link connect?", a: ["The log-odds of the mean response to the linear predictor", "The log of the mean response to the linear predictor", "The mean response directly to the linear predictor", "The log-odds of each predictor to the response mean"], correct: "The log-odds of the mean response to the linear predictor", explanation: "A link function g sets g(E[Y]) = Xβ; for logistic regression g(p) = log(p / (1 − p)). The log link is used in Poisson regression and the identity link in ordinary linear regression; links transform the mean, not the predictors." },
    { q: "A regression coefficient is estimated as 2.4 with a standard error of 0.8. What is its Wald z-statistic?", a: ["3.0", "1.92", "0.33", "3.2"], correct: "3.0", explanation: "The Wald statistic is estimate ÷ standard error = 2.4 / 0.8 = 3.0, compared with a standard normal (or its square with chi-square, 1 df). 1.92 is the product and 3.2 is the sum of the two values." },
    { q: "What is the Shannon entropy, in bits, of a fair 8-sided die roll?", a: ["3 bits", "8 bits", "2 bits", "4 bits"], correct: "3 bits", explanation: "For n equally likely outcomes, entropy = log₂ n = log₂ 8 = 3 bits, the maximum uncertainty possible for 8 outcomes. Any non-uniform die would have lower entropy." },
    { q: "In kernel density estimation, what is the main effect of choosing too small a bandwidth?", a: ["A spiky, undersmoothed estimate that overfits", "A flat, oversmoothed estimate that hides modes", "A density that no longer integrates to one", "A density forced to be symmetric about its mean"], correct: "A spiky, undersmoothed estimate that overfits", explanation: "A small bandwidth makes each data point's kernel narrow, producing many spurious bumps (high variance); too large a bandwidth oversmooths and can merge real modes. With a valid kernel the estimate still integrates to one." },
    { q: "When EM is used to fit a Gaussian mixture model, what does the E-step compute?", a: ["Each point's membership probability for every component", "New component means and covariances from weighted data", "A hard assignment of each point to its nearest mean", "The number of components that best fits the data"], correct: "Each point's membership probability for every component", explanation: "The E-step computes 'responsibilities', soft posterior probabilities that each component generated each point; the M-step then updates means, covariances and weights. Hard nearest-mean assignment is k-means, and EM does not choose the number of components." },
    { q: "How does clustering with a Gaussian mixture model (GMM) differ from k-means?", a: ["Soft probabilistic assignments, and clusters may be elliptical", "Hard assignments, and clusters are assumed to be spherical", "Clusters are found by density, so the count is not set in advance", "Clusters are merged step by step into a tree of nested groups"], correct: "Soft probabilistic assignments, and clusters may be elliptical", explanation: "A GMM models data as a weighted sum of Gaussians, each with its own covariance, and gives each point a probability for each cluster. Hard spherical clusters describe k-means, density-based discovery is DBSCAN, and nested merging is hierarchical clustering." },
    { q: "Ridge regression (an L2 penalty on the coefficients) is equivalent to MAP estimation under which prior on the coefficients?", a: ["A zero-mean Gaussian prior", "A zero-mean Laplace prior", "A flat (uniform) prior", "A Cauchy prior centered at zero"], correct: "A zero-mean Gaussian prior", explanation: "The negative log of a Gaussian prior is proportional to β², which is the L2 penalty; a Laplace prior gives the L1 (Lasso) penalty. A flat prior adds no penalty, so MAP reduces to ordinary maximum likelihood." },
    { q: "Why are noncentral t, chi-square and F distributions important in hypothesis testing?", a: ["They give a test statistic's distribution when H₁ holds, for power calculations", "They give a test statistic's distribution when H₀ holds, for computing p-values", "They correct a test statistic for small samples when the data are not normal", "They describe a test statistic computed on ranks rather than raw data values"], correct: "They give a test statistic's distribution when H₁ holds, for power calculations", explanation: "When the null is false, the statistic follows a noncentral version with a nonzero noncentrality parameter tied to the effect size, which is what power depends on. P-values use the ordinary (central) distribution under H₀." },
    { q: "How is the bootstrap estimate of an estimator's bias computed?", a: ["Mean of the bootstrap estimates minus the original estimate", "Original estimate minus the true population parameter value", "Standard deviation of the bootstrap estimates across resamples", "Median of the bootstrap estimates minus the mean of the data"], correct: "Mean of the bootstrap estimates minus the original estimate", explanation: "The original sample estimate plays the role of the 'true' value in the bootstrap world, so bias ≈ mean(θ*) − θ̂. The true parameter is unknown, and the bootstrap standard deviation estimates standard error, not bias." },
    { q: "Why might an analyst choose the Wilcoxon signed-rank test over a paired t-test?", a: ["The differences have outliers or are clearly non-normal", "The two samples are independent, not paired measurements", "The data are counts in categories, not numeric values", "The sample is very large and the differences look normal"], correct: "The differences have outliers or are clearly non-normal", explanation: "Rank-based tests use the order of values rather than their magnitudes, so they are robust to outliers and non-normality. Independent samples call for Mann-Whitney instead, categorical counts call for a chi-square test, and large normal samples favor the t-test." },
    { q: "What kind of relationship does Spearman's rank correlation coefficient measure?", a: ["Monotonic association between the ranked values", "Linear association between the raw values", "Agreement between two raters on categories", "Association of a binary and a numeric variable"], correct: "Monotonic association between the ranked values", explanation: "Spearman's ρ is Pearson correlation applied to ranks, so y = x³ gives ρ = 1 even though the relationship is not linear. Linear association on raw values is Pearson's r." },
    { q: "The Mann–Whitney U test is the rank-based (nonparametric) alternative to which test?", a: ["Independent two-sample t-test", "Paired-samples t-test", "One-way ANOVA", "Chi-square test of independence"], correct: "Independent two-sample t-test", explanation: "Mann–Whitney compares two independent groups without assuming normality. The paired t-test's rank analogue is the Wilcoxon signed-rank test, and one-way ANOVA's is Kruskal–Wallis." },
    { q: "What does shrinkage estimation (e.g., the James–Stein estimator) do to parameter estimates?", a: ["Pulls them toward a target, adding a little bias to cut variance", "Pushes them away from zero, adding variance to remove all bias", "Removes their bias entirely while keeping the variance unchanged", "Inflates their standard errors to account for multiple testing"], correct: "Pulls them toward a target, adding a little bias to cut variance", explanation: "Shrinkage deliberately accepts some bias in exchange for a larger drop in variance, often lowering overall mean squared error. It does not make estimates unbiased." },
    { q: "What distinguishes empirical Bayes from a fully Bayesian hierarchical analysis?", a: ["The prior's hyperparameters are estimated from the observed data", "The prior is fixed in advance purely from expert opinion", "The hyperparameters receive their own hyperprior distributions", "The posterior is computed without using any likelihood"], correct: "The prior's hyperparameters are estimated from the observed data", explanation: "Empirical Bayes plugs in data-based estimates of the hyperparameters. Putting hyperpriors on them instead is the fully Bayesian hierarchical approach." },
    { q: "In statistics, what does a copula do?", a: ["Joins marginal distributions into a joint one with a chosen dependence", "Mixes several component densities into one multimodal density", "Transforms a skewed variable so it approximates a normal shape", "Smooths a histogram into a continuous density estimate"], correct: "Joins marginal distributions into a joint one with a chosen dependence", explanation: "By Sklar's theorem, any joint distribution can be split into its marginals plus a copula describing their dependence. Mixing component densities describes a mixture model, not a copula." },
    { q: "What does canonical correlation analysis (CCA) find?", a: ["Maximally correlated linear combinations of two variable sets", "Uncorrelated components that maximize variance in one variable set", "Linear combinations that best separate known class labels", "Latent factors that explain shared variance within one set"], correct: "Maximally correlated linear combinations of two variable sets", explanation: "CCA pairs a combination of set X with a combination of set Y so their correlation is as large as possible. Maximizing variance within a single set is PCA; separating classes is LDA." },
    { q: "A predictor's variance inflation factor is VIF = 1 / (1 − R²), where R² comes from regressing it on the other predictors. If that R² is 0.8, what is the VIF?", a: ["5", "1.25", "0.2", "4"], correct: "5", explanation: "1 / (1 − 0.8) = 1 / 0.2 = 5, meaning multicollinearity inflates that coefficient's variance fivefold. 1.25 comes from wrongly using 1/R²." },
    { q: "In spline regression, what are the 'knots'?", a: ["Points where the piecewise polynomial segments join", "Observations flagged as high-leverage outliers", "Interaction terms between pairs of predictors", "Candidate values for the ridge penalty strength"], correct: "Points where the piecewise polynomial segments join", explanation: "A spline fits separate polynomials between knots and constrains them to meet smoothly at the knots, giving a flexible nonlinear fit." },
    { q: "Robust statistics favors measures that resist outliers. Which of these is most robust to a single extreme value?", a: ["Median", "Mean", "Standard deviation", "Range"], correct: "Median", explanation: "The median has a 50% breakdown point: up to half the data can be corrupted before it can be pushed arbitrarily far. One huge value can drag the mean, standard deviation, and range anywhere." },
    { q: "How does the Huber loss treat regression residuals?", a: ["Squared for small residuals, linear for large ones", "Linear for small residuals, squared for large ones", "Squared for all residuals, scaled by their variance", "Zero inside a margin, linear outside the margin"], correct: "Squared for small residuals, linear for large ones", explanation: "Huber loss behaves like squared error near zero but grows only linearly for big residuals, so outliers have less pull. Zero-inside-a-margin is the epsilon-insensitive loss used in support vector regression." },
    { q: "In a mixed-effects model, how are random effects treated differently from fixed effects?", a: ["As draws from a distribution whose variance is estimated", "As separate constants estimated with no shared distribution", "As pure noise terms that are dropped before model fitting", "As predictors whose values the experimenter assigns randomly"], correct: "As draws from a distribution whose variance is estimated", explanation: "Random effects (e.g., per-school intercepts) are modeled as coming from a common distribution, typically normal with an estimated variance. Treating each as its own unrelated constant is the fixed-effects approach." },
    { q: "If time series X 'Granger-causes' time series Y, what has actually been shown?", a: ["Past X improves forecasts of Y beyond Y's own past", "Changes in X directly produce the changes seen in Y", "X and Y share a common long-run stochastic trend", "Past Y improves forecasts of X beyond X's own past"], correct: "Past X improves forecasts of Y beyond Y's own past", explanation: "Granger causality is about predictive usefulness, not proof of a causal mechanism. A shared stochastic trend is cointegration, a separate concept." },
    { q: "In a hidden Markov model (HMM), what exactly is 'hidden'?", a: ["The sequence of states that produces the observations", "The hidden-layer weights of a recurrent neural network", "The emission values recorded at each time step", "The future observations the model must forecast"], correct: "The sequence of states that produces the observations", explanation: "An HMM has an unobserved Markov chain of states, each emitting an observed value; algorithms like Viterbi infer the hidden state path. The emissions themselves are what you observe." },
    { q: "The partial correlation between X and Y given Z measures what?", a: ["Their association after removing Z's linear effect from both", "Their association after removing Z's linear effect from Y only", "The share of Y's variance that X and Z explain jointly", "The product of corr(X, Z) and corr(Y, Z)"], correct: "Their association after removing Z's linear effect from both", explanation: "Partial correlation correlates the residuals of X and Y after each is regressed on Z. Removing Z from only one variable gives the semipartial (part) correlation." },
    { q: "In a linear regression written as y = Xβ + ε, what is X?", a: ["The design matrix of predictor values, one row per observation", "The covariance matrix of the residual error terms", "The hat matrix that maps observed y onto fitted values", "The vector of regression coefficients to be estimated"], correct: "The design matrix of predictor values, one row per observation", explanation: "The design matrix holds the predictors (often plus a column of 1s for the intercept); β is the coefficient vector. The hat matrix is built from X but is a different object." },
    { q: "What does a prior predictive check in Bayesian analysis simulate?", a: ["Data generated from the prior alone, before fitting to observations", "Data generated from the posterior, after fitting to observations", "Datasets resampled from the observations with replacement", "Parameter values that maximize the likelihood of the data"], correct: "Data generated from the prior alone, before fitting to observations", explanation: "Prior predictive simulation shows what data your priors imply, revealing unreasonable assumptions before seeing data. Simulating from the posterior is a posterior predictive check." },
    { q: "What does a quantile regression at τ = 0.9 model?", a: ["The 90th percentile of y given the predictors", "The mean of y among the top 10% of observations", "The mean of y given the predictors, with 90% bands", "The 90th percentile of the predictors given y"], correct: "The 90th percentile of y given the predictors", explanation: "Quantile regression minimizes an asymmetric (pinball) loss to estimate a conditional quantile of the response. It uses all the data, not just the top 10%." },
    { q: "You run 20 independent tests at α = 0.05 and every null hypothesis is true. What is the chance of at least one false positive?", a: ["About 64%", "About 5%", "About 100%", "About 0.25%"], correct: "About 64%", explanation: "P(at least one) = 1 − 0.95²⁰ ≈ 0.64. This is the multiple comparisons problem; simply adding 20 × 0.05 = 1 overstates it." },
    { q: "Using a Bonferroni correction for 10 tests with an overall α of 0.05, what significance threshold does each individual test use?", a: ["0.005", "0.05", "0.0005", "0.5"], correct: "0.005", explanation: "Bonferroni divides α by the number of tests: 0.05 / 10 = 0.005, which keeps the family-wise error rate at or below 0.05." },
    { q: "What quantity does false discovery rate (FDR) control, e.g. Benjamini–Hochberg, keep below the target level?", a: ["Expected share of rejected nulls that are actually true", "Probability of making even one false rejection", "Share of true nulls that end up being rejected", "Probability of missing a real effect that exists"], correct: "Expected share of rejected nulls that are actually true", explanation: "FDR is the expected proportion of false positives among your discoveries. Controlling the chance of even one false rejection is family-wise error rate control, which is stricter." },
    { q: "A 95% Bayesian credible interval for θ is [2, 5]. Which interpretation is correct?", a: ["Given the data and prior, θ lies in [2, 5] with probability 0.95", "95% of intervals built this way would contain the true θ", "95% of future observations will fall between 2 and 5", "The observed data had a 95% chance of arising if θ is in [2, 5]"], correct: "Given the data and prior, θ lies in [2, 5] with probability 0.95", explanation: "A credible interval is a direct posterior probability statement about the parameter. The repeated-sampling interpretation belongs to a frequentist confidence interval." },
    { q: "What does posterior consistency mean in Bayesian statistics?", a: ["The posterior concentrates around the true value as data grow", "The posterior stays in the same distribution family as the prior", "The posterior mean is unbiased at every sample size", "Posteriors from separate MCMC chains agree with each other"], correct: "The posterior concentrates around the true value as data grow", explanation: "Under suitable conditions, more data make the posterior pile up near the true parameter. Staying in the prior's family is conjugacy; agreement between chains is a convergence diagnostic." },
    { q: "In Bayesian regression, placing a Laplace (double-exponential) prior on the coefficients makes the MAP estimate equivalent to which penalized regression?", a: ["Lasso (L1 penalty)", "Ridge (L2 penalty)", "Elastic net (L1 + L2)", "Ordinary least squares"], correct: "Lasso (L1 penalty)", explanation: "The log of a Laplace prior is proportional to −|β|, giving an L1 penalty. A Gaussian prior gives −β², the L2 penalty of ridge regression." },
    { q: "A price model is trained on houses of 800–3,000 sq ft. Using it to predict the price of a 6,000 sq ft house is an example of what?", a: ["Extrapolation", "Interpolation", "Imputation", "Regularization"], correct: "Extrapolation", explanation: "Predicting outside the range of the training data is extrapolation, which is risky because the fitted relationship may not hold there. Predicting a 2,000 sq ft house would be interpolation." },
    { q: "What makes a statistical model 'semiparametric'?", a: ["A finite set of parameters of interest plus an unspecified function", "A fully specified distribution with a finite set of parameters", "Parameters whose number grows with the sample size, with no fixed form", "A parametric model whose parameters are given prior distributions"], correct: "A finite set of parameters of interest plus an unspecified function", explanation: "Semiparametric models mix a parametric part (e.g., regression coefficients) with a nonparametric part left unspecified (e.g., the Cox model's baseline hazard). A fully specified finite-parameter model is parametric." },
    { q: "In a Cox proportional hazards model, the treatment coefficient is β = 0.693 (so e^β ≈ 2). What does this say about the treatment group?", a: ["Its hazard is about double the control hazard at every time", "Its hazard is about 69% higher than the control hazard", "Its hazard is about half the control hazard at every time", "Its median survival is about double that of the control group"], correct: "Its hazard is about double the control hazard at every time", explanation: "Cox coefficients are log hazard ratios, so e^0.693 ≈ 2 means twice the hazard (risk of the event at any moment). A higher hazard means shorter, not longer, survival." },
    { q: "What does the proportional hazards assumption require?", a: ["The hazard ratio between groups stays constant over time", "Each group's own hazard stays constant over time", "Censoring is independent of the event process", "Survival probabilities differ by a constant amount"], correct: "The hazard ratio between groups stays constant over time", explanation: "The hazards themselves may rise or fall, but their ratio must not change. A constant hazard within each group is the exponential model; independent censoring is a separate assumption." },
    { q: "Which description defines a martingale?", a: ["A process whose expected next value, given the past, equals its current value", "A process whose next value depends only on its current state, not its history", "A process whose values are independent of all the values that came before", "A process whose mean and variance remain constant throughout time"], correct: "A process whose expected next value, given the past, equals its current value", explanation: "A fair gambler's fortune is the classic martingale: on average it neither rises nor falls. Depending only on the current state is the Markov property, which is a different condition." },
    { q: "The periodogram of a monthly time series has a sharp peak at frequency 1/12 cycles per month. What does this suggest?", a: ["A yearly seasonal cycle", "A cycle repeating every 12 years", "A steady linear trend", "White noise with no structure"], correct: "A yearly seasonal cycle", explanation: "Spectral analysis splits variance by frequency; 1/12 cycles per month is a period of 12 months. White noise would give a roughly flat spectrum." },
    { q: "What does a Kalman filter do at each time step?", a: ["Blends a model prediction with a noisy measurement to update the state", "Averages a fixed window of past values to remove high-frequency noise", "Reweights many sampled particles to track a nonlinear hidden state", "Finds the single most likely sequence of discrete hidden states"], correct: "Blends a model prediction with a noisy measurement to update the state", explanation: "The Kalman filter predicts the next state, then corrects it using the new measurement, weighting each by its uncertainty. Particle filters handle nonlinear cases by sampling; the Viterbi algorithm finds HMM state paths." },
    { q: "For n i.i.d. observations, how does the total Fisher information about a parameter scale with n?", a: ["It grows in proportion to n", "It grows in proportion to √n", "It stays fixed regardless of n", "It shrinks in proportion to 1/n"], correct: "It grows in proportion to n", explanation: "Fisher information adds across independent observations, so I_n = n·I_1. The MLE's variance is about 1/I_n, which is why standard errors shrink like 1/√n." },
    { q: "What does it mean that a maximum likelihood estimator is 'asymptotically normal'?", a: ["Its sampling distribution approaches a normal as n grows", "The underlying data become normally distributed as n grows", "Its bias drops to exactly zero once n is larger than 30", "Its residuals follow a normal distribution in large samples"], correct: "Its sampling distribution approaches a normal as n grows", explanation: "Asymptotic normality concerns the estimator's distribution across repeated samples, not the data; it justifies the usual estimate ± 1.96·SE intervals." },
    { q: "An estimate is θ̂ = 3 with standard error 0.1. Using the delta method, what is the approximate standard error of θ̂²?", a: ["0.6", "0.01", "0.3", "0.9"], correct: "0.6", explanation: "The delta method gives SE[g(θ̂)] ≈ |g′(θ̂)|·SE = |2θ̂|·0.1 = 2·3·0.1 = 0.6. Squaring the SE (0.01) is a common mistake." },
    { q: "Suppose Xₙ converges in distribution to N(0, 1) and Yₙ converges in probability to 2. By Slutsky's theorem, Xₙ·Yₙ converges in distribution to what?", a: ["N(0, 4)", "N(0, 2)", "N(2, 1)", "N(0, 1)"], correct: "N(0, 4)", explanation: "Slutsky lets you treat Yₙ as the constant 2, so the limit is 2·Z with Z ~ N(0, 1). Multiplying by 2 multiplies the variance by 4." },
    { q: "Which estimator is consistent but biased?", a: ["Sample variance dividing by n instead of n − 1", "Sample mean of n i.i.d. observations, for μ", "The first observation X₁ alone, used to estimate μ", "A fixed guess of 0 for μ, ignoring the data"], correct: "Sample variance dividing by n instead of n − 1", explanation: "Dividing by n underestimates variance by a factor (n − 1)/n, but that bias vanishes as n grows, so it is consistent. X₁ alone is unbiased but never improves with more data, so it is not consistent." },
    { q: "What does Rao–Blackwellization do to an unbiased estimator?", a: ["Conditions it on a sufficient statistic, never increasing its variance", "Adds a penalty that shrinks it toward zero, trading bias for variance", "Resamples the data to estimate its bias and then subtract that bias", "Averages it over leave-one-out subsets of the data to reduce bias"], correct: "Conditions it on a sufficient statistic, never increasing its variance", explanation: "Taking E[estimator | sufficient statistic] keeps it unbiased and gives variance no larger than before. Leave-one-out averaging is the jackknife, a different technique." },
    { q: "In logistic regression, what happens when a predictor causes complete separation of the outcome classes?", a: ["The MLE does not exist; a coefficient diverges toward infinity", "Every coefficient is estimated as exactly zero", "The model reduces to ordinary least squares regression", "Standard errors shrink to zero, giving tiny p-values"], correct: "The MLE does not exist; a coefficient diverges toward infinity", explanation: "If a predictor perfectly splits 0s from 1s, the likelihood keeps increasing as the coefficient grows, so no finite maximum exists. Standard errors blow up rather than shrink; penalized (e.g., Firth) regression is a common fix." },
    { q: "When is a zero-inflated Poisson model a better choice than a plain Poisson model?", a: ["When counts have more zeros than a Poisson fit predicts", "When counts have fewer zeros than a Poisson fit predicts", "When zero counts cannot occur at all in the data", "When the variance of the counts equals their mean"], correct: "When counts have more zeros than a Poisson fit predicts", explanation: "Zero-inflated models add a separate process that produces extra zeros (e.g., non-smokers reporting 0 cigarettes). When zeros are impossible, a zero-truncated model is used instead." },
    { q: "In a Bayesian hierarchical model, what is a hyperprior?", a: ["A prior placed on the parameters of another prior", "A prior chosen to be as flat as possible", "A prior built from a previous study's posterior", "A prior in the same family as the likelihood's conjugate"], correct: "A prior placed on the parameters of another prior", explanation: "Hierarchical models stack levels: group parameters get a prior, and that prior's parameters (hyperparameters) get a hyperprior. A deliberately flat prior is a noninformative prior, not a hyperprior." },
    { q: "With partial pooling in a hierarchical model, a school with only 5 students gets an estimated mean that is…", a: ["Pulled strongly toward the overall mean of all schools", "Equal to its own 5-student average, with no adjustment", "Equal to the overall mean of all schools, ignoring its data", "Pushed away from the overall mean to keep it distinctive"], correct: "Pulled strongly toward the overall mean of all schools", explanation: "Small groups have noisy averages, so partial pooling shrinks them heavily toward the population mean, while large groups stay closer to their own data. The two 'equal to' options are no pooling and complete pooling." },
    { q: "In a posterior predictive check, what is compared?", a: ["Observed data versus data simulated from the fitted model", "Each parameter's posterior versus its original prior", "Draws from several MCMC chains run in parallel", "Training-set accuracy versus held-out test accuracy"], correct: "Observed data versus data simulated from the fitted model", explanation: "If replicated data drawn from the posterior predictive distribution look systematically different from the real data, the model is missing something. Comparing MCMC chains checks convergence, not fit." },
    { q: "Which statement about exchangeable random variables is correct?", a: ["Every i.i.d. sequence is exchangeable, but not vice versa", "Every exchangeable sequence is i.i.d., but not vice versa", "Exchangeable variables must be pairwise uncorrelated", "Exchangeable variables must all be normally distributed"], correct: "Every i.i.d. sequence is exchangeable, but not vice versa", explanation: "Exchangeability only requires the joint distribution to be unchanged when the order is permuted. Draws without replacement from an urn are exchangeable yet correlated, so not independent." },
    { q: "Because log is a concave function, what does Jensen's inequality say for a positive random variable X?", a: ["E[log X] ≤ log E[X]", "E[log X] ≥ log E[X]", "E[log X] = log E[X]", "E[log X] ≥ E[X]"], correct: "E[log X] ≤ log E[X]", explanation: "For a concave function, the function of the average is at least the average of the function. This step is exactly how the ELBO lower bound in variational inference is derived." },
    { q: "How does variational inference approximate a Bayesian posterior?", a: ["By optimizing a simpler distribution to be close to it", "By running a Markov chain whose stationary distribution is it", "By evaluating it exactly on a fine grid of parameter values", "By fitting a Gaussian at its mode using the local curvature"], correct: "By optimizing a simpler distribution to be close to it", explanation: "Variational inference turns inference into optimization over a family of tractable distributions, often making it faster than MCMC. A Gaussian at the mode is the Laplace approximation." },
    { q: "In variational inference, maximizing the evidence lower bound (ELBO) is equivalent to what?", a: ["Minimizing KL(q ‖ posterior) for the approximation q", "Maximizing KL(q ‖ prior) for the approximation q", "Minimizing the log marginal likelihood of the data", "Maximizing the entropy of the true posterior"], correct: "Minimizing KL(q ‖ posterior) for the approximation q", explanation: "log p(x) = ELBO + KL(q ‖ posterior), and log p(x) does not depend on q, so raising the ELBO must lower the KL divergence. The ELBO is a lower bound on the log marginal likelihood, not something to minimize." },
    { q: "Which property does the Kullback–Leibler divergence have?", a: ["It is asymmetric: KL(P‖Q) generally differs from KL(Q‖P)", "It is a true distance metric obeying the triangle inequality", "It can turn negative when Q fits the data better than P", "It is bounded between 0 and 1, much like a probability"], correct: "It is asymmetric: KL(P‖Q) generally differs from KL(Q‖P)", explanation: "KL divergence is nonnegative and zero only when the distributions match, but it is not symmetric and can be infinite, so it is not a metric." },
    { q: "What distinguishes the bootstrap-t confidence interval from the simple percentile bootstrap interval?", a: ["It bootstraps a studentized statistic, (estimate − original) / SE", "It uses t-table quantiles with the bootstrap standard error", "It resamples model residuals instead of whole observations", "It subtracts a jackknife estimate of bias from each replicate"], correct: "It bootstraps a studentized statistic, (estimate − original) / SE", explanation: "Bootstrap-t computes a t-like pivot in every resample and uses its quantiles, which often improves coverage. Plugging a bootstrap SE into a t-table is a simpler, different interval." },
    { q: "In regression diagnostics, an observation is most likely to be highly influential when it has which combination?", a: ["High leverage and a large residual", "Low leverage and a large residual", "High leverage and a near-zero residual", "Low leverage and a near-zero residual"], correct: "High leverage and a large residual", explanation: "Influence requires both an unusual X position (leverage) and a poor fit to the rest (residual). A high-leverage point lying right on the trend barely changes the fitted line." },
    { q: "What does Cook's distance for observation i summarize?", a: ["How much all fitted values change when i is deleted", "How far i's predictor values lie from the predictor means", "How large i's residual is relative to the residual SD", "How much i lowers the model's R² when it is included"], correct: "How much all fitted values change when i is deleted", explanation: "Cook's distance measures the overall shift in fitted values (equivalently, coefficients) from dropping one point, combining leverage and residual size. Distance from the predictor means describes leverage alone." },
    { q: "In linear regression, an observation's leverage depends on which of these?", a: ["Only its predictor (X) values", "Only its response (y) value", "Its residual from the fitted line", "Both its residual and its y value"], correct: "Only its predictor (X) values", explanation: "Leverage is the diagonal of the hat matrix X(XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀ, which involves only the predictors. A point can have high leverage and still sit exactly on the line." },
    { q: "What problem do 'sandwich' (Huber–White) standard errors mainly address?", a: ["Heteroskedastic errors that make usual SEs wrong", "Biased coefficients caused by omitted variables", "Multicollinearity that inflates coefficient variance", "Non-normal predictors that violate OLS assumptions"], correct: "Heteroskedastic errors that make usual SEs wrong", explanation: "The sandwich estimator gives valid standard errors when error variance is not constant (and under some other misspecifications). It changes only the SEs, so it cannot fix bias in the coefficients." },
    { q: "Empirical process theory centrally studies the behavior of which object?", a: ["The scaled gap between the empirical CDF and the true CDF", "The sequence of states visited by an MCMC sampler", "The path of a model's training loss during optimization", "The distribution of p-values across many hypothesis tests"], correct: "The scaled gap between the empirical CDF and the true CDF", explanation: "Results like Glivenko–Cantelli and Donsker's theorem describe how √n·(empirical CDF − true CDF) behaves, which underpins much of asymptotic statistics and learning theory." },
    { q: "Which pair of results most clearly indicates an overfit model?", a: ["Training error 2%, validation error 25%", "Training error 25%, validation error 26%", "Training error 3%, validation error 4%", "Training error 12%, validation error 12%"], correct: "Training error 2%, validation error 25%", explanation: "Overfitting shows up as a large gap: the model memorizes the training data but fails on new data. High error on both sets (25% / 26%) points to underfitting instead." },
    { q: "Fitting a straight line to data that follow a clear U-shaped curve is likely to cause what?", a: ["Underfitting, due to high bias", "Overfitting, due to high variance", "Overfitting, due to high bias", "Underfitting, due to high variance"], correct: "Underfitting, due to high bias", explanation: "A line is too simple to capture the curve, so it is systematically wrong (high bias) on training and test data alike. Overfitting comes from models that are too flexible." },
    { q: "Which property must a relational database primary key have?", a: ["Unique and non-NULL in every row", "Unique, but NULL allowed in one row", "Matches a key in another table", "Auto-incrementing integer values"], correct: "Unique and non-NULL in every row", explanation: "A primary key identifies each row, so it must be unique and cannot be NULL. Auto-increment is a common convenience, not a requirement; referencing another table describes a foreign key." },
    { q: "What does a foreign key constraint enforce?", a: ["Every non-NULL value matches a key in the referenced table", "Every value is unique within the column's own table", "Every value is non-NULL within the column's own table", "Every value is copied into the referenced table on insert"], correct: "Every non-NULL value matches a key in the referenced table", explanation: "Foreign keys enforce referential integrity: you cannot reference a row that does not exist. They allow duplicates (many orders per customer) and, unless declared NOT NULL, NULLs." },
    { q: "Database normalization is mainly intended to do what?", a: ["Reduce redundancy and update anomalies", "Speed up reads by duplicating data", "Rescale numeric columns to a 0–1 range", "Compress tables to save disk space"], correct: "Reduce redundancy and update anomalies", explanation: "Normalization splits data into related tables so each fact is stored once. Duplicating data for faster reads is denormalization; rescaling to 0–1 is feature normalization in ML, a different meaning." },
    { q: "Which description fits a CSV file?", a: ["Plain text, with commas separating the fields", "Binary, with a schema storing column types", "Plain text, with one JSON object per line", "Binary, with each column stored contiguously"], correct: "Plain text, with commas separating the fields", explanation: "CSV is a simple text format with no built-in types; fields containing commas are wrapped in quotes. Binary columnar formats with schemas describe Parquet; one JSON object per line is JSON Lines." },
    { q: "In an ETL data pipeline, what does the 'T' step do?", a: ["Cleans and reshapes data into the target format", "Transfers data from the source over the network", "Writes the processed data into the warehouse", "Trains a model on the newly extracted data"], correct: "Cleans and reshapes data into the target format", explanation: "ETL is Extract, Transform, Load; the Transform step cleans, joins, and reshapes data. Writing into the warehouse is the Load step." },
    { q: "Which workload is a data warehouse primarily designed for?", a: ["Analytical queries aggregating large history", "High volumes of small transactions like orders", "Low-cost storage of raw files in any format", "Streaming events between services in real time"], correct: "Analytical queries aggregating large history", explanation: "Warehouses are optimized for OLAP-style reporting over large historical data. Many small writes are OLTP databases' job, and cheap raw-file storage describes a data lake." },
    { q: "How does a data lake typically differ from a data warehouse?", a: ["Raw data in native formats, with schema applied on read", "Cleaned, structured data, with schema enforced on write", "Current transactional records for live applications", "Pre-aggregated summary tables for dashboard reporting"], correct: "Raw data in native formats, with schema applied on read", explanation: "Data lakes store structured, semi-structured, and unstructured data as-is and interpret it when queried (schema-on-read). Enforcing a schema on write is characteristic of a warehouse." },
    { q: "What style of language is SQL primarily?", a: ["Declarative: you describe the result you want", "Imperative: you write step-by-step loops", "Object-oriented: you define classes and methods", "Assembly-like: you manage memory and registers"], correct: "Declarative: you describe the result you want", explanation: "In SQL you state what rows you want from a relational database, and the query optimizer decides how to retrieve them." },
    { q: "In SQL's logical order of query processing, which clause is evaluated before SELECT?", a: ["WHERE", "ORDER BY", "LIMIT", "DISTINCT"], correct: "WHERE", explanation: "The logical order is FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, SELECT, DISTINCT, ORDER BY, LIMIT. That is why a column alias defined in SELECT usually cannot be used in WHERE." },
    { q: "Table A has 3 rows and table B has 4 rows. A CROSS JOIN (a join with no matching condition) returns how many rows?", a: ["12", "7", "4", "3"], correct: "12", explanation: "A cross join pairs every row of A with every row of B: 3 × 4 = 12. Joins with an ON condition keep only the pairs whose related columns match." },
    { q: "In an INNER JOIN, what happens to a row from either table that has no match in the other?", a: ["It is dropped from the result", "It is kept, with NULLs filled in", "It is matched to the nearest key", "It causes the query to raise an error"], correct: "It is dropped from the result", explanation: "An inner join returns only rows where the join condition matches in both tables. Keeping unmatched rows with NULLs is what outer joins (LEFT, RIGHT, FULL) do." },
    { q: "Customers (10 rows) LEFT JOIN Orders ON customer_id. Three customers have no orders and every other customer has exactly one. How many rows are returned?", a: ["10", "7", "13", "3"], correct: "10", explanation: "A LEFT JOIN keeps every left-table row: 7 customers match one order each, and the 3 without orders appear once with NULL order columns, for 10 rows. An inner join would return 7." },
    { q: "In standard SQL, what does the comparison NULL = NULL evaluate to?", a: ["UNKNOWN", "TRUE", "FALSE", "0"], correct: "UNKNOWN", explanation: "NULL means a missing or unknown value, so comparing two unknowns is itself unknown and a WHERE clause will not keep the row. Use IS NULL to test for NULLs." },
    { q: "What is the main trade-off of adding an index to a database table?", a: ["Faster reads, but slower writes and more storage", "Faster writes, but slower reads and more storage", "Less storage, but slower reads and slower writes", "Faster reads and writes, but weaker data integrity"], correct: "Faster reads, but slower writes and more storage", explanation: "An index lets the database find rows without scanning the whole table, but every insert, update, or delete must also update the index, which takes extra disk space." },
    { q: "How does a pandas DataFrame differ from a plain 2-D NumPy array?", a: ["Labeled rows and columns, with per-column data types", "Unlabeled axes, with one data type for every cell", "Labeled rows and columns, with one shared data type", "Per-column data types, but no row labels at all"], correct: "Labeled rows and columns, with per-column data types", explanation: "A DataFrame is a 2-D labeled table where each column can have its own type (numbers, strings, dates). A NumPy array has positional axes and a single dtype." },
    { q: "Which pandas method splits rows into groups so each group can be aggregated (e.g., mean sales per region)?", a: ["groupby()", "merge()", "melt()", "concat()"], correct: "groupby()", explanation: "groupby() implements split-apply-combine, e.g. df.groupby('region')['sales'].mean(). merge() joins tables, melt() reshapes wide to long, and concat() stacks DataFrames." },
    { q: "What is the main reason NumPy array arithmetic is much faster than looping over Python lists?", a: ["Vectorized compiled loops over contiguous, typed memory", "Automatic use of GPU cores for every array operation", "Lazy evaluation that skips elements never used later", "Caching that reuses results of earlier calculations"], correct: "Vectorized compiled loops over contiguous, typed memory", explanation: "NumPy stores same-typed values in contiguous memory and runs operations in compiled C, avoiding per-element Python overhead. Plain NumPy runs on the CPU and evaluates eagerly." },
    { q: "Which description fits a histogram?", a: ["Bars counting numeric values that fall in adjacent bins", "Bars counting separate categories, drawn with gaps", "Points showing the relationship of two numeric variables", "Boxes showing the quartiles and outliers of one variable"], correct: "Bars counting numeric values that fall in adjacent bins", explanation: "A histogram shows the distribution of a numeric variable using touching bins. Separated bars for categories make a bar chart; quartile boxes make a box plot." },
    { q: "Five salaries are $40k, $45k, $50k, $55k, and $310k. What is the mean salary?", a: ["$100k", "$50k", "$270k", "$125k"], correct: "$100k", explanation: "The mean is the sum divided by the count: $500k / 5 = $100k. The median is $50k; the outlier pulls the mean far above it." },
    { q: "What is the median of the values 9, 2, 15, 4?", a: ["6.5", "8.5", "7.5", "13"], correct: "6.5", explanation: "Sorted, the data are 2, 4, 9, 15; with an even count the median is the average of the middle two, (4 + 9) / 2 = 6.5. Averaging the unsorted middle values gives 8.5, and 7.5 is the mean." },
    { q: "For nominal data such as eye color, which measure of center can be meaningfully reported?", a: ["Mode", "Median", "Mean", "Midrange"], correct: "Mode", explanation: "The mode is simply the most frequent value, so it works for unordered categories. The median needs an ordering, and the mean and midrange need numbers." },
    { q: "If every value in a dataset is multiplied by 3, what happens to the standard deviation?", a: ["It triples", "It becomes 9 times larger", "It stays the same", "It increases by 3"], correct: "It triples", explanation: "Standard deviation scales with the data, so multiplying by 3 triples it; the variance is what grows 9 times. Adding a constant, by contrast, leaves the spread unchanged." },
    { q: "What is the population variance of the values 2, 4, 6?", a: ["8/3 ≈ 2.67", "4", "8", "√(8/3) ≈ 1.63"], correct: "8/3 ≈ 2.67", explanation: "The mean is 4, the squared deviations are 4, 0, 4, and their average is 8/3. Dividing by n − 1 instead gives the sample variance of 4; 1.63 is the standard deviation." },
    { q: "Two fair coins are tossed. What is the probability of getting at least one head?", a: ["3/4", "1/2", "1/4", "2/3"], correct: "3/4", explanation: "The outcomes HH, HT, TH, TT are equally likely, and only TT has no head, so 1 − 1/4 = 3/4. Treating 'no heads, one head, two heads' as equally likely gives the wrong answer 2/3." },
    { q: "Formally, what is a random variable?", a: ["A function that maps each outcome of an experiment to a number", "A quantity whose value drifts unpredictably over time", "A probability assigned to each event in a sample space", "The set of all possible outcomes of an experiment"], correct: "A function that maps each outcome of an experiment to a number", explanation: "For example, 'number of heads in 3 tosses' maps each outcome like HTH to 2. The set of all outcomes is the sample space, and assigning probabilities to events is the probability measure." },
    { q: "Which probability distribution models the number of successes in n independent trials that share the same success probability p?", a: ["Binomial", "Poisson", "Geometric", "Bernoulli"], correct: "Binomial", explanation: "The binomial counts successes in a fixed number of independent trials. Bernoulli is a single trial, the geometric counts trials until the first success, and the Poisson counts events in an interval." },
    { q: "For a normal distribution, about what percentage of values lie within one standard deviation of the mean?", a: ["68%", "95%", "50%", "99.7%"], correct: "68%", explanation: "The 68–95–99.7 rule: about 68% within 1σ, 95% within 2σ, and 99.7% within 3σ of the mean." },
    { q: "An exam has mean 70 and standard deviation 8. A student scores 82. What is the student's z-score?", a: ["1.5", "12", "−1.5", "0.67"], correct: "1.5", explanation: "z = (x − μ) / σ = (82 − 70) / 8 = 1.5, so the score is 1.5 standard deviations above the mean. 12 is the raw difference before dividing by σ." },
    { q: "Two variables have a Pearson correlation of r = −0.9. What does this indicate?", a: ["A strong negative linear association", "A weak negative linear association", "Y falls 0.9 units for each unit of X", "90% of Y's variance is explained by X"], correct: "A strong negative linear association", explanation: "|r| near 1 means a strong linear relationship and the sign gives the direction. r is not the slope, and the variance explained is r² = 0.81, not 0.9." },
    { q: "Ice cream sales and drowning deaths are positively correlated. What best explains this?", a: ["Hot weather, a confounder, drives both", "Eating ice cream directly causes drowning", "Simpson's paradox in the aggregated data", "Regression to the mean across the seasons"], correct: "Hot weather, a confounder, drives both", explanation: "Summer heat increases both ice cream sales and swimming, so the two move together without one causing the other. Correlation alone does not establish causation." },
    { q: "Which plot is the standard choice for checking whether two continuous variables have a linear relationship?", a: ["Scatter plot", "Histogram", "Side-by-side box plots", "Q-Q plot"], correct: "Scatter plot", explanation: "A scatter plot puts one variable on each axis, so linear trends, curves, and outliers are visible. A Q-Q plot compares a distribution to a reference distribution, not two paired variables." },
    { q: "What most distinguishes machine learning from traditional rule-based programming?", a: ["Behavior is learned from example data", "Rules are written by domain experts", "Programs run on GPUs instead of CPUs", "Programs are compiled, not interpreted"], correct: "Behavior is learned from example data", explanation: "In ML the mapping from inputs to outputs is fit from data rather than hand-coded. Hand-written expert rules are the traditional approach ML replaces." },
    { q: "Training a spam filter on emails already tagged 'spam' or 'not spam' is an example of what?", a: ["Supervised learning", "Unsupervised learning", "Reinforcement learning", "Self-supervised learning"], correct: "Supervised learning", explanation: "The training examples come with known target labels, which is the defining feature of supervised learning. Self-supervised learning creates its own labels from unlabeled data." },
    { q: "Which of these is a typical use of unsupervised learning?", a: ["Segmenting customers with no labels", "Predicting house prices from sales", "Classifying tumors as benign or not", "Training a robot by trial and reward"], correct: "Segmenting customers with no labels", explanation: "Unsupervised learning finds structure, such as customer segments, in data without target labels. Price prediction and tumor classification need labels (supervised); trial-and-reward is reinforcement learning." },
    { q: "Predicting whether a loan applicant will default (yes or no) is what kind of task?", a: ["Binary classification", "Linear regression", "Clustering", "Dimensionality reduction"], correct: "Binary classification", explanation: "The output is one of two discrete classes, so it is binary classification. Logistic regression is often used for it, but the task itself is classification, not linear regression." },
    { q: "Which of these is a regression problem rather than a classification problem?", a: ["Predicting tomorrow's temperature in °C", "Predicting whether an email is spam", "Predicting a digit from a handwritten image", "Predicting which of 5 products a user buys"], correct: "Predicting tomorrow's temperature in °C", explanation: "Temperature is a continuous numeric target, which makes it regression. Digits and product choices are numbered but are still discrete classes." },
    { q: "Clustering differs from classification mainly because clustering...", a: ["uses no predefined group labels", "requires labeled training examples", "predicts continuous numeric outputs", "can handle just two groups at a time"], correct: "uses no predefined group labels", explanation: "Clustering is unsupervised: it discovers groups from similarity alone. Classification learns from examples whose classes are already known." },
    { q: "In each iteration of k-means clustering, how is a data point assigned to a cluster?", a: ["To the nearest cluster centroid", "To the cluster with most members", "By majority vote of k neighbors", "To the cluster with highest density"], correct: "To the nearest cluster centroid", explanation: "k-means alternates between assigning each point to its closest centroid and recomputing centroids as cluster means. Voting among k neighbors is k-nearest neighbors, a different algorithm." },
    { q: "In machine learning, what is the 'model' that training produces?", a: ["A function with parameters fit to data", "The labeled dataset used for fitting", "The loss metric used to score results", "The algorithm before it sees any data"], correct: "A function with parameters fit to data", explanation: "A trained model is the learned mapping (e.g., a set of fitted weights) from inputs to outputs. The algorithm is the procedure; the model is what it produces from data." },
    { q: "Why must a model's training data be kept separate from its test data?", a: ["Scores on seen data overstate real accuracy", "Training data must be larger than test data", "Test data needs different feature columns", "Mixing them makes training run more slowly"], correct: "Scores on seen data overstate real accuracy", explanation: "A model can memorize examples it trained on, so only unseen data gives an honest estimate of how it generalizes." },
    { q: "An analyst tunes hyperparameters repeatedly by checking scores on the test set. What is the main problem?", a: ["Test score becomes optimistically biased", "The model will underfit the training data", "The test set's variance becomes too low", "Training will no longer converge"], correct: "Test score becomes optimistically biased", explanation: "Choosing settings based on the test set leaks information into the model, so the test score overstates real performance. Tuning belongs on a separate validation set or in cross-validation." },
    { q: "A model predicts house price from square footage and age of the house. What are square footage and age?", a: ["Features", "Labels", "Hyperparameters", "Parameters"], correct: "Features", explanation: "Features are the input variables used for prediction; the price is the label. Parameters are values the model learns, and hyperparameters are settings chosen before training." },
    { q: "In supervised learning, what is the 'label' of a training example?", a: ["The known target value to predict", "An input column used as a predictor", "The cluster ID the algorithm assigns", "A name given to each feature column"], correct: "The known target value to predict", explanation: "The label is the correct output paired with each example, such as 'spam' or a sale price. Input columns are features, not labels." },
    { q: "Income data is missing because high earners tend to skip the income question. What type of missingness is this?", a: ["Missing not at random (MNAR)", "Missing completely at random (MCAR)", "Missing at random (MAR)", "Structurally missing data"], correct: "Missing not at random (MNAR)", explanation: "When the chance of being missing depends on the unobserved value itself (high income), the data are MNAR. MCAR means missingness is unrelated to any data." },
    { q: "What is a known drawback of filling missing values with the column mean?", a: ["It shrinks the column's variance", "It shifts the column's mean upward", "It deletes rows with missing values", "It adds outliers to the column"], correct: "It shrinks the column's variance", explanation: "Every filled value sits exactly at the mean, so spread and correlations are understated. Mean imputation leaves the column mean unchanged, so it does not shift it." },
    { q: "Which of these is a data-cleaning step rather than a modeling step?", a: ["Standardizing 'NY' and 'New York'", "Tuning a random forest's depth", "Choosing a loss function", "Computing cross-validated accuracy"], correct: "Standardizing 'NY' and 'New York'", explanation: "Fixing inconsistent spellings of the same value is classic data cleaning. The other steps happen while building or evaluating the model." },
    { q: "In scikit-learn, why put preprocessing (like scaling) inside a Pipeline instead of fitting it on the full dataset first?", a: ["It prevents data leakage from test folds", "It makes the model train on a GPU", "It removes the need for a test set", "It automatically picks the best model"], correct: "It prevents data leakage from test folds", explanation: "A Pipeline refits the scaler on each training fold only, so statistics from held-out data never leak into training and inflate the score." },
    { q: "The classic '3 Vs' of big data are volume, velocity, and what?", a: ["Variety", "Validity", "Visibility", "Volatility"], correct: "Variety", explanation: "Volume (how much), velocity (how fast), and variety (how many formats and sources) are the original 3 Vs; extra Vs like veracity were added later." },
    { q: "Which programming model did Hadoop originally use to process data across a cluster?", a: ["MapReduce", "Bulk synchronous", "Actor model", "Message passing (MPI)"], correct: "MapReduce", explanation: "Hadoop pairs the HDFS distributed file system with MapReduce, which splits work into parallel map tasks and aggregating reduce tasks. MPI is used in traditional HPC, not core Hadoop." },
    { q: "Why is Apache Spark often much faster than Hadoop MapReduce for iterative jobs like ML training?", a: ["It keeps intermediate data in memory", "It writes each step's output to disk", "It runs on a single machine instead", "It compiles queries to GPU kernels"], correct: "It keeps intermediate data in memory", explanation: "Spark can cache datasets in memory between steps, while MapReduce writes intermediate results to disk after each job, which is slow for repeated passes." },
    { q: "Which task belongs most clearly to a data engineer rather than a data scientist?", a: ["Building ETL jobs that load the warehouse", "Testing whether a new feature lifts sales", "Fitting a churn model on customer data", "Interpreting regression coefficients"], correct: "Building ETL jobs that load the warehouse", explanation: "Data engineers build the infrastructure and pipelines that collect, transform, and store data; data scientists analyze and model that data." },
    { q: "Drew Conway's data science Venn diagram overlaps hacking skills, math and statistics knowledge, and what third area?", a: ["Domain expertise", "Machine learning", "Database design", "Data visualization"], correct: "Domain expertise", explanation: "Conway's third circle is substantive (domain) expertise. Machine learning appears as the overlap of hacking and math, not as its own circle." },
    { q: "A dashboard tile shows only this month's revenue figure. What is most important to add so viewers can judge it?", a: ["A comparison to target or prior period", "A 3D effect to make the number stand out", "The raw transaction table underneath", "More decimal places for precision"], correct: "A comparison to target or prior period", explanation: "A metric needs context, like a goal or last month's value, to show whether it is good or bad. Extra precision or decoration adds no meaning." },
    { q: "What distinguishes a KPI (key performance indicator) from an ordinary metric?", a: ["It is tied to a key business objective", "It is computed in real time from logs", "It is expressed as a percentage or ratio", "It is any metric shown on a dashboard"], correct: "It is tied to a key business objective", explanation: "Every KPI is a metric, but only metrics chosen to track progress toward a critical goal are KPIs. KPIs can be counts, dollars, or rates, and need not be real-time." },
    { q: "Why are users randomly assigned to groups in an A/B test?", a: ["So other factors balance out between groups", "So each group has exactly the same size", "So results reach significance faster", "So the test needs fewer total users"], correct: "So other factors balance out between groups", explanation: "Randomization makes the groups comparable on everything except the change being tested, so differences in outcome can be attributed to that change." },
    { q: "A hypothesis test gives p = 0.03. Which interpretation is correct?", a: ["P(data this extreme | H₀ true) = 0.03", "There's a 3% chance the null is true", "There's a 97% chance the effect is real", "The result will replicate 97% of the time"], correct: "P(data this extreme | H₀ true) = 0.03", explanation: "A p-value is the probability of results at least this extreme assuming the null hypothesis is true. It is not the probability that the null (or the alternative) is true." },
    { q: "How does a histogram differ from a bar chart?", a: ["It bins a continuous variable; bars touch", "It compares separate categories; bars gap", "It plots two variables against each other", "It shows parts of a whole as stacked bars"], correct: "It bins a continuous variable; bars touch", explanation: "A histogram groups numeric values into adjacent intervals to show a distribution, so bars touch. Separate, gapped bars for categories describe a bar chart." },
    { q: "What does Anscombe's quartet famously demonstrate?", a: ["Identical stats can hide different shapes", "Correlation implies a linear trend", "Small samples give unstable correlations", "Pie charts distort part-to-whole ratios"], correct: "Identical stats can hide different shapes", explanation: "Its four datasets share nearly the same means, variances, correlation, and regression line but look completely different when plotted, which is why you should visualize data." },
    { q: "Why should a bar chart's value axis normally start at zero?", a: ["Bar length encodes the value itself", "Negative values cannot be displayed", "It makes bars sort in descending order", "Software rejects axes not starting at 0"], correct: "Bar length encodes the value itself", explanation: "Readers compare bar lengths, so a truncated axis exaggerates differences. Line charts can use non-zero axes because they encode position, not length." },
    { q: "Which property of time series data breaks the assumption behind randomly shuffled train/test splits?", a: ["Observations are autocorrelated over time", "Values are recorded as categorical labels", "Each observation has many missing features", "The data follow a uniform distribution"], correct: "Observations are autocorrelated over time", explanation: "Nearby time points depend on each other, so random splits let the model peek at neighbors of test points and overstate accuracy." },
    { q: "When validating a forecasting model, how should the data be split?", a: ["Train on earlier dates, test on later ones", "Shuffle rows randomly, then split 80/20", "Test on earlier dates, train on later ones", "Use k-fold CV with randomly shuffled folds"], correct: "Train on earlier dates, test on later ones", explanation: "A forecast must only use the past to predict the future, so validation should mimic that order. Shuffling leaks future information into training." },
    { q: "Which SQL clause restricts rows before grouping and cannot reference aggregates like SUM()?", a: ["WHERE", "HAVING", "GROUP BY", "ORDER BY"], correct: "WHERE", explanation: "WHERE filters individual rows before aggregation. HAVING filters groups after aggregation and can use aggregate functions." },
    { q: "What does SELECT dept, AVG(salary) FROM emp GROUP BY dept return?", a: ["One row per department with its average", "One row per employee with the overall average", "One row with the average of all salaries", "Every row, sorted by department"], correct: "One row per department with its average", explanation: "GROUP BY collapses rows sharing a dept value into one group, and AVG is computed within each group. It does not sort; that is ORDER BY." },
    { q: "In SQL, how does COUNT(column) differ from COUNT(*)?", a: ["COUNT(column) skips NULLs in that column", "COUNT(column) counts only distinct values", "COUNT(*) skips rows that contain any NULL", "They are identical in every SQL dialect"], correct: "COUNT(column) skips NULLs in that column", explanation: "COUNT(*) counts all rows, while COUNT(column) counts non-NULL values in that column. Counting distinct values requires COUNT(DISTINCT column)." },
    { q: "Renting virtual machines and storage that you configure and manage yourself is which cloud service model?", a: ["IaaS", "PaaS", "SaaS", "FaaS"], correct: "IaaS", explanation: "Infrastructure as a Service provides raw compute, storage, and networking. PaaS manages the runtime for you, SaaS delivers finished software, and FaaS runs individual functions." },
    { q: "Which AWS service provides object storage commonly used as the foundation of a data lake?", a: ["Amazon S3", "Amazon EC2", "AWS Lambda", "Amazon RDS"], correct: "Amazon S3", explanation: "S3 (Simple Storage Service) stores files as objects cheaply at scale. EC2 is virtual servers, Lambda is serverless functions, and RDS is managed relational databases." },
    { q: "In a REST API, which HTTP method is conventionally used to retrieve data without changing it?", a: ["GET", "POST", "PUT", "PATCH"], correct: "GET", explanation: "GET is a safe, read-only request. POST creates resources, while PUT and PATCH replace or modify them." },
    { q: "Which of these is valid JSON?", a: ["{\"a\": 1}", "{'a': 1}", "{a: 1}", "{\"a\": 1,}"], correct: "{\"a\": 1}", explanation: "JSON requires keys in double quotes and forbids trailing commas. Single quotes and unquoted keys are valid in JavaScript or Python, but not in JSON." },
    { q: "In pandas, a numeric column read from CSV contains one stray entry '12kg'. What dtype does the column usually get?", a: ["object", "float64", "int64", "category"], correct: "object", explanation: "One non-numeric string forces pandas to store the whole column as generic Python objects (strings). Standard missing markers like 'NA' are instead read as NaN, giving float64." },
    { q: "Which Python library provides the DataFrame object widely used for tabular data analysis?", a: ["pandas", "NumPy", "Matplotlib", "SciPy"], correct: "pandas", explanation: "pandas provides DataFrame and Series. It is built on NumPy, which supplies n-dimensional arrays but not labeled tables." },
    { q: "The R language was created as an open-source implementation of which earlier statistical language?", a: ["S", "SAS", "SPSS", "Stata"], correct: "S", explanation: "R is a dialect of S, the language developed at Bell Labs; R itself was created by Ross Ihaka and Robert Gentleman at the University of Auckland. SAS, SPSS, and Stata are separate commercial statistics packages." },
    { q: "In Python, how many times does the body of for i in range(2, 10, 3): execute?", a: ["3", "2", "4", "8"], correct: "3", explanation: "range(2, 10, 3) yields 2, 5, and 8; the next value, 11, is past the exclusive stop of 10." },
    { q: "In Python 3.7+, what does calling breakpoint() inside your code do?", a: ["Pauses execution and opens the debugger", "Stops the program and prints the stack", "Skips the rest of the current loop", "Marks the line for the profiler to time"], correct: "Pauses execution and opens the debugger", explanation: "breakpoint() drops into pdb by default, letting you inspect variables and step through code. Skipping the rest of a loop iteration is what continue does." },
    { q: "Which Git command records staged changes as a new snapshot in the local repository?", a: ["git commit", "git push", "git add", "git checkout"], correct: "git commit", explanation: "git add stages changes and git commit records them in local history. git push sends commits to a remote." },
    { q: "What does git clone <url> do?", a: ["Copies a remote repository with its history", "Downloads only the latest files, no history", "Creates a new empty local repository", "Links an existing folder to a remote"], correct: "Copies a remote repository with its history", explanation: "A clone is a full local copy of the repository, including all commits. An empty repository is created with git init, and git remote add links a folder to a remote." },
    { q: "When does a Git merge produce a merge conflict?", a: ["Both branches changed the same lines", "Both branches added different new files", "One branch is behind the other by a commit", "The branches were made by different authors"], correct: "Both branches changed the same lines", explanation: "Git cannot decide automatically between competing edits to the same part of a file. Changes to different files or lines merge cleanly." },
    { q: "Technically, what is a branch in Git?", a: ["A movable pointer to a commit", "A full copy of the repository's files", "A separate remote on another server", "A saved diff of uncommitted changes"], correct: "A movable pointer to a commit", explanation: "A branch is a lightweight reference that advances as you commit, which is why creating branches is cheap. Saving uncommitted changes aside is git stash." },
    { q: "A dashboard query takes 4 seconds before returning its single result. This delay is best described as what?", a: ["Latency", "Throughput", "Bandwidth", "Jitter"], correct: "Latency", explanation: "Latency is the time from request to response. Throughput is how much work completes per unit time, and jitter is variation in latency." },
    { q: "A pipeline processes 12,000 records per minute. What is its throughput in records per second?", a: ["200", "120", "720", "20"], correct: "200", explanation: "Throughput is work per unit time: 12,000 ÷ 60 seconds = 200 records per second." },
    { q: "Adding more machines to a cluster to handle growing load is called what?", a: ["Horizontal scaling", "Vertical scaling", "Load shedding", "Vertical partitioning"], correct: "Horizontal scaling", explanation: "Scaling out (horizontal) adds nodes; scaling up (vertical) gives one machine more CPU or memory. Load shedding drops work rather than adding capacity." },
    { q: "Python's logging module ranks levels by severity. Which of these is more severe than WARNING?", a: ["ERROR", "INFO", "DEBUG", "NOTSET"], correct: "ERROR", explanation: "The order from least to most severe is NOTSET, DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR, CRITICAL, so only ERROR here outranks WARNING." },
    { q: "Which workload is the best fit for batch processing rather than stream processing?", a: ["Computing nightly sales totals for reports", "Flagging card fraud as each payment occurs", "Updating a live leaderboard every second", "Triggering alerts when a sensor overheats"], correct: "Computing nightly sales totals for reports", explanation: "Batch jobs process accumulated data on a schedule when results can wait. The other tasks need low-latency reactions to each event, which suits streaming." },
    { q: "In stream processing, what is a 'tumbling window'?", a: ["Fixed-size, non-overlapping time intervals", "Fixed-size windows that overlap by a slide", "Windows that close after an idle gap", "A window covering all events seen so far"], correct: "Fixed-size, non-overlapping time intervals", explanation: "Tumbling windows split the stream into back-to-back chunks such as every 5 minutes. Overlapping windows are sliding (hopping) windows, and gap-based ones are session windows." },
    { q: "Change data capture (CDC) ingests data from a source database by doing what?", a: ["Streaming row-level changes as they occur", "Copying the full table on every run", "Sampling a random subset of rows daily", "Compressing tables before they are moved"], correct: "Streaming row-level changes as they occur", explanation: "CDC captures inserts, updates, and deletes (often from the transaction log) so only changes are moved. Full-table copies are simpler but far more expensive." },
    { q: "Which file format stores data column by column, making analytical queries on a few columns efficient?", a: ["Parquet", "CSV", "JSON Lines", "Avro"], correct: "Parquet", explanation: "Parquet is a columnar format, so a query reads only the columns it needs and compresses well. CSV, JSON Lines, and Avro are row-oriented." },
    { q: "'Schema-on-read', typical of data lakes, means what?", a: ["Structure is applied when data is queried", "Structure must be defined before loading", "The schema is stored inside every row", "Data can be read but never modified"], correct: "Structure is applied when data is queried", explanation: "Data lakes store raw files and interpret their structure at query time. Defining structure before loading is schema-on-write, typical of traditional warehouses." },
    { q: "In SQL, in what order are a table's rows returned by a SELECT with no ORDER BY clause?", a: ["No guaranteed order", "Insertion order", "Primary key order", "Alphabetical by first column"], correct: "No guaranteed order", explanation: "Relational tables are unordered sets of rows, so the database may return them in any order. Results often look like insertion or key order, but you must use ORDER BY to rely on it." },
    { q: "In a 'tidy data' table, what should each row represent?", a: ["One observation", "One variable", "One value type", "One summary group"], correct: "One observation", explanation: "Tidy data puts each observation in a row, each variable in a column, and each value in a cell. Variables belong in columns, not rows." },
    { q: "In relational database theory, what is a column of a table formally called?", a: ["An attribute", "A tuple", "A relation", "A domain"], correct: "An attribute", explanation: "A column is an attribute, a row is a tuple, and the table itself is a relation. A domain is the set of allowed values for an attribute." },
    { q: "Which database design process reduces redundancy by splitting tables so each fact is stored once?", a: ["Normalization", "Denormalization", "Indexing", "Partitioning"], correct: "Normalization", explanation: "Normalization removes duplicated data to prevent update anomalies. Denormalization deliberately adds redundancy to speed up reads." },
    { q: "A foreign key constraint mainly protects which kind of data integrity?", a: ["Referential integrity", "Entity integrity", "Domain integrity", "User-defined integrity"], correct: "Referential integrity", explanation: "A foreign key ensures each reference points to an existing row in the parent table. Entity integrity concerns primary keys being unique and non-null." },
    { q: "Applying a log transformation to a right-skewed income variable mainly does what?", a: ["Pulls in the long right tail", "Converts it to a uniform shape", "Scales it into the 0 to 1 range", "Centers it at a mean of zero"], correct: "Pulls in the long right tail", explanation: "Logs compress large values more than small ones, making skewed data more symmetric. Rescaling to 0 to 1 is min-max scaling, and centering at zero is standardization." },
    { q: "Three groups of sizes 1, 1, and 8 have means 10, 20, and 30. What is the overall mean of all 10 values?", a: ["27", "20", "24", "30"], correct: "27", explanation: "Weight each mean by group size: (1×10 + 1×20 + 8×30) ÷ 10 = 270 ÷ 10 = 27. Simply averaging the three group means (20) ignores the sizes." },
    { q: "In Python, what does a function return if it reaches the end without a return statement?", a: ["None", "0", "False", "NaN"], correct: "None", explanation: "Python functions implicitly return None. None is falsy, but it is not equal to False or 0." },
    { q: "Binary search on a sorted list of 1,000,000 items needs at most about how many comparisons?", a: ["20", "1,000", "500,000", "100"], correct: "20", explanation: "Each comparison halves the search range, so it takes about log₂(1,000,000) ≈ 20 steps. About 500,000 is the average for a linear scan." },
    { q: "A nested loop that compares every pair among n items has what time complexity?", a: ["O(n²)", "O(n)", "O(n log n)", "O(2ⁿ)"], correct: "O(n²)", explanation: "There are n(n−1)/2 pairs, which grows proportionally to n². O(n log n) is typical of efficient sorting; O(2ⁿ) is enumerating all subsets." },
    { q: "In Python, after a = [1, 2]; b = a; b.append(3), what is the value of a?", a: ["[1, 2, 3]", "[1, 2]", "[3]", "[1, 2, [3]]"], correct: "[1, 2, 3]", explanation: "b = a makes both names refer to the same list object, so appending through b changes a too. Use a.copy() to get an independent list." },
    { q: "In Python 3, what is the result of 7 // 2?", a: ["3", "3.5", "4", "3.0"], correct: "3", explanation: "// is floor division, which returns the integer 3 for two ints. 7 / 2 would give 3.5, and 7.0 // 2 would give 3.0." },
    { q: "In Python, what does 'data'[1:3] return?", a: ["'at'", "'dat'", "'ata'", "'da'"], correct: "'at'", explanation: "Indexing starts at 0 and the stop index is excluded, so the slice takes characters at positions 1 and 2: 'a' and 't'." },
    { q: "In Python, what is the result of True + True?", a: ["2", "True", "1", "TypeError"], correct: "2", explanation: "bool is a subclass of int with True equal to 1, so True + True = 2. This is why sum() over a boolean list counts the True values." },
    { q: "What is a key difference between a Python list and a tuple?", a: ["Lists are mutable; tuples are not", "Tuples are mutable; lists are not", "Lists are ordered; tuples are not", "Tuples hold one type; lists mix types"], correct: "Lists are mutable; tuples are not", explanation: "Lists can be changed in place, while tuples cannot be modified after creation. Both are ordered and both can hold mixed types." },
    { q: "Why are NumPy arrays usually much faster than Python lists for numeric math?", a: ["They store one fixed type contiguously", "They compile to GPU code by default", "They cache every result automatically", "They allow mixed types in one array"], correct: "They store one fixed type contiguously", explanation: "A homogeneous, contiguous block of memory lets NumPy run vectorized loops in compiled C. Standard NumPy runs on the CPU, not the GPU." },
    { q: "Which Python library is best known for classic ML tools like random forests and cross-validation?", a: ["scikit-learn", "Seaborn", "statsmodels", "PyTorch"], correct: "scikit-learn", explanation: "scikit-learn provides standard ML estimators plus model selection tools. statsmodels focuses on statistical models and tests, and PyTorch on deep learning." },
    { q: "In Python, which exception does int('abc') raise?", a: ["ValueError", "TypeError", "SyntaxError", "KeyError"], correct: "ValueError", explanation: "The argument has the right type (a string) but an invalid value for conversion, so Python raises ValueError. TypeError would come from an unsupported type, like int([1])." },
    { q: "In Python's try / except / else / finally, when does the finally block run?", a: ["In every case, error or not", "When no exception was raised", "When an exception was caught", "When the except block re-raises"], correct: "In every case, error or not", explanation: "finally runs regardless of what happened, which makes it the place for cleanup like closing files. The block that runs only when no exception occurred is else." },
    { q: "Which of these is an IDE (integrated development environment) built for scientific Python?", a: ["Spyder", "Pandas", "Conda", "Docker"], correct: "Spyder", explanation: "Spyder is the Scientific Python Development Environment, with an editor, console, and variable explorer. Conda is a package and environment manager, not an IDE." },
    { q: "The 3-2-1 backup rule recommends 3 copies of data on 2 types of media, with how many copies offsite?", a: ["1", "2", "3", "0"], correct: "1", explanation: "3-2-1 means three copies, two different media, and one copy offsite so a single disaster cannot destroy everything." },
    { q: "In disaster recovery planning, what does the RPO (recovery point objective) measure?", a: ["Max acceptable data loss, in time", "Max acceptable downtime to restore", "Percent of data successfully restored", "Number of backup copies kept offsite"], correct: "Max acceptable data loss, in time", explanation: "RPO is how far back in time you can afford to lose data (e.g., 1 hour). Maximum acceptable downtime is the RTO, recovery time objective." },
    { q: "Which is true of lossless compression such as gzip?", a: ["The original is restored bit for bit", "It discards detail the eye can't see", "It shrinks random data very well", "It is the method used by JPEG photos"], correct: "The original is restored bit for bit", explanation: "Lossless compression removes redundancy without losing information. Discarding imperceptible detail is lossy compression like JPEG, and random data has little redundancy to remove." },
    { q: "How does symmetric encryption differ from asymmetric encryption?", a: ["One shared key both encrypts and decrypts", "It uses a public and a private key pair", "It is irreversible, like a hash function", "It encrypts data only while in transit"], correct: "One shared key both encrypts and decrypts", explanation: "Symmetric ciphers like AES use the same secret key on both ends. Public/private key pairs define asymmetric encryption, and hashing, unlike encryption, cannot be reversed." },
    { q: "A file was encrypted with AES using a secret key. Which process turns the ciphertext back into the original plaintext?", a: ["Decryption with the same key", "Hashing the ciphertext again", "Decoding it from Base64 text", "Decompressing it with gzip"], correct: "Decryption with the same key", explanation: "Decryption reverses encryption, and AES is symmetric so the same key is used. Hashing is one-way and Base64 is just an encoding, so neither recovers protected plaintext." },
    { q: "In the client-server model, what is the role of the server?", a: ["It listens for requests and returns data or services", "It initiates requests and displays the results to users", "It routes packets between networks using IP addresses", "It translates domain names into numeric IP addresses"], correct: "It listens for requests and returns data or services", explanation: "A server waits for and answers requests. Initiating requests is the client's job; routing is done by routers and name lookup by DNS." },
    { q: "When your browser loads a page from a web API, which role is the browser playing?", a: ["Client", "Server", "Proxy", "Load balancer"], correct: "Client", explanation: "The browser initiates the request, which makes it the client; the API host that answers is the server. A proxy or load balancer sits between the two." },
    { q: "In a distributed data pipeline, what does the networking layer mainly provide?", a: ["Transport of data between machines", "Scheduling of jobs on a timetable", "Validation of records against a schema", "Persistent storage of the raw data"], correct: "Transport of data between machines", explanation: "Networking moves bytes between nodes so they can cooperate. Scheduling is orchestration, validation is data quality, and storage is the job of disks or object stores." },
    { q: "A ping to a server reports 80 ms. Which networking property is that number measuring?", a: ["Latency", "Bandwidth", "Throughput", "Packet loss"], correct: "Latency", explanation: "Ping measures round-trip delay, which is latency. Bandwidth and throughput are amounts of data per second, not time delays." },
    { q: "A link is rated at 1 Gbps. What does that figure describe?", a: ["Its bandwidth, the maximum data rate", "Its latency, the delay per packet", "Its jitter, the variation in delay", "Its MTU, the largest packet size"], correct: "Its bandwidth, the maximum data rate", explanation: "Bits per second describe capacity, i.e. bandwidth. Latency and jitter are measured in time, and MTU in bytes." },
    { q: "In networking, what is a protocol?", a: ["An agreed set of rules for exchanging messages", "A physical cable standard such as Cat6 copper", "A unique numeric address assigned to a device", "A program that encrypts traffic between hosts"], correct: "An agreed set of rules for exchanging messages", explanation: "A protocol (TCP, HTTP, DNS…) defines message formats and the order of exchange. A device address is an IP address, and encryption is just one thing some protocols do." },
    { q: "In a typical tabular dataset used for machine learning, what does each row usually represent?", a: ["One observation", "One feature", "One label class", "One data type"], correct: "One observation", explanation: "Rows are observations (records, samples) and columns are features. A label class is a value of the target, not a row." },
    { q: "Which of these is an example of metadata for a photo file?", a: ["The camera model and timestamp stored in its EXIF tags", "The red, green and blue values of every single pixel", "The compressed image bytes inside the JPEG file body", "A lower-resolution thumbnail copy of the same picture"], correct: "The camera model and timestamp stored in its EXIF tags", explanation: "Metadata is data about data, such as when and how the photo was taken. Pixel values, image bytes and a thumbnail are all the image content itself." },
    { q: "Which activity falls most squarely under data governance?", a: ["Defining who may access customer data and how it is kept", "Tuning query indexes so dashboards load more quickly", "Choosing a chart type that best shows monthly sales", "Selecting the learning rate for a neural network model"], correct: "Defining who may access customer data and how it is kept", explanation: "Governance sets policies for data ownership, access, quality and retention. Index tuning, chart design and model tuning are technical tasks, not governance." },
    { q: "The CRM lists a customer's birth year as 1985, but the billing system lists 1958 for the same customer. Which data quality dimension is violated?", a: ["Consistency", "Completeness", "Timeliness", "Validity"], correct: "Consistency", explanation: "Two systems disagreeing about the same fact is a consistency problem. Both years are valid-looking values and nothing is missing, so validity and completeness aren't the issue." },
    { q: "A survey picks every 10th name from an alphabetical list of members. What kind of sampling is this?", a: ["Systematic sampling", "Stratified sampling", "Cluster sampling", "Convenience sampling"], correct: "Systematic sampling", explanation: "Taking every k-th unit from an ordered list is systematic sampling. Stratified sampling samples within subgroups, and cluster sampling selects whole groups." },
    { q: "The true average height of all adults in a country is an example of what?", a: ["A population parameter", "A sample statistic", "A point estimate", "A test statistic"], correct: "A population parameter", explanation: "A parameter describes the whole population and is usually unknown. A statistic or point estimate is computed from a sample to approximate it." },
    { q: "If you quadruple the size of a random sample, what happens to the standard error of the sample mean?", a: ["It is cut in half", "It is cut to a quarter", "It stays the same", "It doubles"], correct: "It is cut in half", explanation: "Standard error is σ/√n, so multiplying n by 4 divides the error by √4 = 2, not by 4." },
    { q: "A bathroom scale always reads 2 kg too heavy. In statistical terms, this is an example of what?", a: ["Bias", "Variance", "Random noise", "Sampling error"], correct: "Bias", explanation: "Bias is systematic error that pushes every measurement the same way. Variance and random noise scatter readings in both directions." },
    { q: "In the bias-variance sense, what does it mean for a model to have high variance?", a: ["Its predictions change a lot when trained on different samples", "Its predictions are consistently off in the same direction", "Its training error is high because the model is too simple", "Its target variable has a large spread of possible values"], correct: "Its predictions change a lot when trained on different samples", explanation: "High variance means the fitted model is sensitive to the particular training data and tends to overfit. Consistent offset or a too-simple model describe high bias." },
    { q: "Which part of prediction error cannot be reduced by any model, no matter how good?", a: ["Irreducible error from noise", "Bias from a simple model", "Variance from overfitting", "Error from too few epochs"], correct: "Irreducible error from noise", explanation: "Expected error decomposes into bias², variance and irreducible noise. Better models can shrink bias and variance, but the inherent noise in the data remains." },
    { q: "A regression predicts 50 for a point whose observed value is 47. What is the residual?", a: ["−3", "3", "47", "0.94"], correct: "−3", explanation: "Residual = observed − predicted = 47 − 50 = −3. Getting +3 comes from subtracting in the wrong order." },
    { q: "In machine learning, what does the training optimizer adjust during fitting?", a: ["The model's parameters, such as weights", "The model's hyperparameters, such as depth", "The labels in the training dataset", "The features chosen for the model"], correct: "The model's parameters, such as weights", explanation: "An optimizer updates learned parameters to minimize loss. Hyperparameters like tree depth are set outside training, e.g. by a tuning search." },
    { q: "In gradient descent, what happens if the learning rate is set far too large?", a: ["Updates overshoot and the loss may diverge", "Training converges faster to the same minimum", "The model will overfit the training data", "Each step barely moves and training stalls"], correct: "Updates overshoot and the loss may diverge", explanation: "Too large a step jumps past the minimum and can make loss oscillate or blow up. Tiny steps that stall are the symptom of a learning rate that is too small." },
    { q: "Which loss function is the standard choice for training a binary classifier like logistic regression?", a: ["Log loss (cross-entropy)", "Mean squared error", "Mean absolute error", "Hinge loss (SVM)"], correct: "Log loss (cross-entropy)", explanation: "Logistic regression is fit by minimizing binary cross-entropy (log loss). MSE and MAE are mainly regression losses, and hinge loss is what linear SVMs minimize." },
    { q: "A fraud dataset is 99% legitimate transactions. A model that predicts 'legitimate' every time scores what accuracy?", a: ["99%", "50%", "1%", "0%"], correct: "99%", explanation: "It is right on all the legitimate cases, 99% of the data, yet it catches no fraud. That is why accuracy is misleading on imbalanced data." },
    { q: "A spam filter flags 50 emails; 40 of them really are spam. What is its precision?", a: ["0.80", "0.40", "0.20", "1.25"], correct: "0.80", explanation: "Precision = TP / (TP + FP) = 40 / 50 = 0.80. It measures how many flagged items were actually positive." },
    { q: "There are 100 actual spam emails and the filter catches 60 of them. What is its recall?", a: ["0.60", "0.40", "0.75", "1.67"], correct: "0.60", explanation: "Recall = TP / (TP + FN) = 60 / 100 = 0.60. It is the share of actual positives that were found; 0.40 is the miss rate." },
    { q: "The F1 score combines precision and recall using which kind of mean?", a: ["Harmonic mean", "Arithmetic mean", "Geometric mean", "Weighted median"], correct: "Harmonic mean", explanation: "F1 = 2PR / (P + R), the harmonic mean, which is pulled down sharply if either value is low. A plain arithmetic average would let a high recall hide a terrible precision." },
    { q: "In a binary confusion matrix, a 'false negative' is a case that was…", a: ["Actually positive but predicted negative", "Actually negative but predicted positive", "Actually negative and predicted negative", "Actually positive and predicted positive"], correct: "Actually positive but predicted negative", explanation: "'Negative' is the prediction and 'false' means it was wrong, so the true class was positive. The second option is a false positive." },
    { q: "Which of these is a hyperparameter rather than a learned parameter?", a: ["The maximum depth of a decision tree", "A coefficient in a linear regression", "A weight inside a neural network", "A split threshold the tree chooses"], correct: "The maximum depth of a decision tree", explanation: "Max depth is set before training. Coefficients, weights and the split thresholds a tree picks are all learned from the data." },
    { q: "Which of these is an example of data leakage?", a: ["Fitting a scaler on the full data before splitting off the test set", "Using stratified splitting so class ratios match in train and test", "Holding out the most recent month as the test set for a forecast", "Tuning hyperparameters on a validation set separate from the test"], correct: "Fitting a scaler on the full data before splitting off the test set", explanation: "The scaler learns test-set statistics, so information from the test data leaks into training and scores look too good. The other three are standard good practice." },
    { q: "Why is it recommended to put preprocessing steps like scaling inside a scikit-learn Pipeline used with cross-validation?", a: ["They are refit on each training fold, preventing leakage", "They run on a GPU instead of the CPU, cutting training time", "They are skipped at prediction time, so inference is faster", "They let the model train without needing any labeled data"], correct: "They are refit on each training fold, preventing leakage", explanation: "A Pipeline refits each step on only the training fold, so validation folds never influence preprocessing. Pipelines do not change hardware or skip steps at prediction time." },
    { q: "A team releases a new model to 5% of users while the old model serves the rest, then widens it if metrics hold. What deployment strategy is this?", a: ["Canary release", "Shadow deployment", "Blue-green deployment", "Batch scoring"], correct: "Canary release", explanation: "A canary sends a small slice of real traffic to the new version first. Shadow deployment runs the new model without serving its answers, and blue-green flips all traffic at once." },
    { q: "Which signal would a model-monitoring system typically track to catch problems after deployment?", a: ["Shift in the distribution of incoming features", "Number of epochs used in the original training", "Learning rate chosen during hyperparameter tuning", "Random seed used to split the training data"], correct: "Shift in the distribution of incoming features", explanation: "Monitoring watches live behavior such as input drift, latency and error rates. Epochs, learning rate and seed are fixed training settings, not live signals." },
    { q: "Which log level is conventionally used for a recoverable problem that deserves attention but does not stop the program?", a: ["WARNING", "DEBUG", "INFO", "CRITICAL"], correct: "WARNING", explanation: "WARNING flags something unexpected that the program survived. DEBUG and INFO are routine detail, and CRITICAL is for failures that may halt the program." },
    { q: "An ETL job's transform step crashes halfway through. Which design property lets you safely re-run the whole job without creating duplicate rows?", a: ["Idempotency", "Parallelism", "Compression", "Normalization"], correct: "Idempotency", explanation: "An idempotent job produces the same result whether it runs once or many times, so retries after a failure are safe. Parallelism and compression affect speed and size, not retry safety." },
    { q: "In the CAP theorem for distributed databases, what does 'consistency' mean?", a: ["Every read sees the most recent write", "Every request gets some non-error reply", "The system keeps working if links fail", "Each transaction is all-or-nothing"], correct: "Every read sees the most recent write", explanation: "CAP consistency means all nodes return the latest value. The second option is availability, the third is partition tolerance, and all-or-nothing is ACID atomicity." },
    { q: "What is the main benefit of database replication?", a: ["Higher availability and read capacity", "Smaller total storage on disk", "Stronger encryption of stored rows", "Faster writes on a single node"], correct: "Higher availability and read capacity", explanation: "Copies on several nodes let the system survive a node failure and spread reads. Replication uses more storage, not less, and adds work to each write." },
    { q: "How does sharding differ from replication in a database?", a: ["Each node holds a different slice of the rows", "Each node holds a full copy of every row", "Each node holds a different set of columns", "Each node holds a compressed backup of the rows"], correct: "Each node holds a different slice of the rows", explanation: "Sharding splits rows across nodes (often by a shard key) to scale writes and storage. Full copies on every node is replication; splitting columns is vertical partitioning." },
    { q: "A cache serves 900 of 1,000 requests directly without going to the database. What is its hit rate?", a: ["90%", "10%", "9%", "0.9%"], correct: "90%", explanation: "Hit rate = hits / total requests = 900 / 1000 = 90%. The 10% that go to the database are the miss rate." },
    { q: "Users in Australia see slow page loads from a server in Virginia. Which change most directly cuts that network latency?", a: ["Serving content from a nearby CDN edge", "Doubling the server's RAM and CPU cores", "Raising the bandwidth of the Virginia link", "Adding more indexes to the main database"], correct: "Serving content from a nearby CDN edge", explanation: "Latency is dominated by physical distance; a CDN edge close to the users shortens the round trip. More bandwidth or server power doesn't make light travel faster." },
    { q: "Which of these is a defining challenge of distributed systems that a single machine does not face?", a: ["Partial failure of some nodes", "Running out of available RAM", "Bugs in the application code", "Slow disk reads from storage"], correct: "Partial failure of some nodes", explanation: "In a distributed system some machines or links can fail while others keep running, which must be handled explicitly. Memory limits, bugs and slow disks occur on single machines too." },
    { q: "A cluster keeps serving requests after one of its three nodes crashes. Which property does this demonstrate?", a: ["Fault tolerance", "Strong consistency", "Vertical scaling", "Data locality"], correct: "Fault tolerance", explanation: "Fault tolerance is continuing to operate correctly despite component failures. Vertical scaling means a bigger machine, and consistency is about reads seeing the latest writes." },
    { q: "Items A, B, C are added to a queue in that order. Which item is removed first?", a: ["A", "C", "B", "Whichever is smallest"], correct: "A", explanation: "A queue is first-in, first-out (FIFO), so A leaves first. C would come out first from a stack, and 'smallest first' describes a priority queue." },
    { q: "Which everyday programming feature relies on a stack (last-in, first-out)?", a: ["Undo history in an editor", "A printer's job queue", "Round-robin CPU scheduling", "Breadth-first graph search"], correct: "Undo history in an editor", explanation: "Undo reverses the most recent action first, which is LIFO. Print queues, round-robin scheduling and breadth-first search all use FIFO queues." },
    { q: "Which property must a good cryptographic hash function have?", a: ["It is infeasible to reverse it to the input", "Its output length grows with input length", "Its input can be recovered with a key", "Similar inputs give similar outputs"], correct: "It is infeasible to reverse it to the input", explanation: "Cryptographic hashes are one-way and give fixed-size outputs where a tiny input change scrambles the result. Recovering input with a key describes encryption." },
    { q: "Which of these is a key-value store?", a: ["Redis", "PostgreSQL", "Neo4j", "Snowflake"], correct: "Redis", explanation: "Redis maps keys to values and is widely used for caching. PostgreSQL is relational, Neo4j is a graph database, and Snowflake is a cloud data warehouse." },
    { q: "MongoDB stores records as JSON-like documents. Which category of NoSQL database is it?", a: ["Document store", "Column-family store", "Graph database", "Key-value store"], correct: "Document store", explanation: "MongoDB is a document database. Cassandra is a column-family store, Neo4j a graph database, and Redis a key-value store." },
    { q: "In a schema-less (schema-on-read) database, when is the structure of a record interpreted?", a: ["When the data is read by a query", "When the table is first created", "When the data is written to disk", "When the database is restarted"], correct: "When the data is read by a query", explanation: "Schema-on-read stores records as-is and applies structure at query time. Relational databases are schema-on-write, enforcing structure when data is inserted." },
    { q: "A huge event table is partitioned by date. Why does a query filtered to one day run much faster?", a: ["The engine can skip all other days' partitions", "The partitions are kept compressed in memory", "The rows are encrypted per partition in batches", "The query is copied to every partition at once"], correct: "The engine can skip all other days' partitions", explanation: "Partition pruning lets the engine read only the partition matching the filter instead of scanning the whole table." },
    { q: "With 10,000 training examples and a batch size of 100, how many gradient updates happen in one epoch?", a: ["100", "10,000", "1,000", "1"], correct: "100", explanation: "Each batch gives one update, so 10,000 / 100 = 100 updates per epoch. One update per epoch would be full-batch gradient descent." },
    { q: "During training, validation loss keeps falling for 20 epochs, then starts rising while training loss keeps falling. What does this usually indicate?", a: ["The model has begun to overfit", "The model has begun to underfit", "The learning rate is far too small", "The validation set is too large"], correct: "The model has begun to overfit", explanation: "Training loss improving while validation loss worsens means the model is memorizing training data. Early stopping around epoch 20 is the usual remedy." },
    { q: "In gradient descent, parameters are updated in which direction relative to the gradient of the loss?", a: ["Opposite to the gradient", "Along the gradient", "Perpendicular to it", "In a random direction"], correct: "Opposite to the gradient", explanation: "The gradient points toward steepest increase in loss, so stepping against it decreases the loss. Moving along it would be gradient ascent." },
    { q: "Min-max normalization rescales a feature with min 10 and max 30. What does the value 25 become?", a: ["0.75", "0.83", "0.50", "1.25"], correct: "0.75", explanation: "(x − min) / (max − min) = (25 − 10) / (30 − 10) = 15/20 = 0.75. Dividing 25 by 30 gives the tempting but wrong 0.83." },
    { q: "A feature has mean 50 and standard deviation 10. After standardization (z-scores), what does the value 65 become?", a: ["1.5", "6.5", "0.65", "15"], correct: "1.5", explanation: "z = (x − μ) / σ = (65 − 50) / 10 = 1.5. The result has mean 0 and standard deviation 1." },
    { q: "Using the 1.5 × IQR rule with Q1 = 20 and Q3 = 40, above what value is a point flagged as an outlier?", a: ["70", "60", "55", "80"], correct: "70", explanation: "IQR = 40 − 20 = 20, so the upper fence is Q3 + 1.5 × IQR = 40 + 30 = 70. 60 is the mistake of adding just one IQR instead of 1.5." },
    { q: "Which technique addresses class imbalance by creating synthetic examples of the minority class?", a: ["SMOTE", "PCA", "Bagging", "Dropout"], correct: "SMOTE", explanation: "SMOTE interpolates between neighboring minority-class points to make new examples. PCA reduces dimensions, bagging builds ensembles, and dropout regularizes neural networks." },
    { q: "In bootstrap resampling, how are the resamples drawn from a dataset of n points?", a: ["n points drawn with replacement", "n points drawn without replacement", "n/2 points drawn with replacement", "k equal folds without any overlap"], correct: "n points drawn with replacement", explanation: "Each bootstrap sample has the original size n and is drawn with replacement, so some points repeat. Non-overlapping folds describe cross-validation." },
    { q: "A dataset is 90% class A and 10% class B. Why use a stratified train/test split?", a: ["To keep the 90/10 ratio in both sets", "To force a 50/50 ratio in both sets", "To put all of class B in the test set", "To remove class B from the training set"], correct: "To keep the 90/10 ratio in both sets", explanation: "Stratification preserves the original class proportions in each split. Forcing 50/50 would be resampling, not stratification." },
    { q: "For a regression task, which is a common baseline model to beat?", a: ["Predicting the training mean for every case", "A tuned gradient-boosted tree ensemble", "A deep neural network with dropout", "A random forest with 500 trees"], correct: "Predicting the training mean for every case", explanation: "A baseline is a deliberately simple reference; always predicting the mean shows whether a real model adds any value. The other options are full models, not baselines." },
    { q: "Which ensemble method trains models sequentially, each one focusing on the errors of the previous ones?", a: ["Boosting", "Bagging", "Stacking", "Voting"], correct: "Boosting", explanation: "Boosting (e.g. AdaBoost, XGBoost) builds learners one after another to correct earlier mistakes. Bagging trains models independently in parallel on bootstrap samples." },
    { q: "Besides bootstrap samples, what extra source of randomness does a random forest add compared with plain bagged trees?", a: ["A random subset of features at each split", "A random learning rate for each new tree", "Random noise added to the target labels", "A random tree depth chosen for each tree"], correct: "A random subset of features at each split", explanation: "Considering only a random subset of features per split decorrelates the trees, which makes averaging them more effective. Learning rates belong to boosting, not random forests." },
    { q: "Which measure is commonly used by a classification decision tree to choose the best split?", a: ["Gini impurity", "R-squared", "Mean absolute error", "Cosine similarity"], correct: "Gini impurity", explanation: "Classification trees pick splits that most reduce Gini impurity or entropy. R-squared and MAE are regression metrics, and cosine similarity measures vector angles." },
    { q: "As you increase a model's complexity, how do bias and variance typically change?", a: ["Bias falls and variance rises", "Bias rises and variance falls", "Both bias and variance fall", "Both bias and variance rise"], correct: "Bias falls and variance rises", explanation: "Flexible models fit training data more closely (lower bias) but become more sensitive to it (higher variance). The tradeoff is choosing complexity between underfitting and overfitting." },
    { q: "A grid search tries 4 learning rates × 3 depths × 5 tree counts with 5-fold cross-validation. How many model fits is that?", a: ["300", "60", "12", "17"], correct: "300", explanation: "4 × 3 × 5 = 60 combinations, each trained 5 times for the folds, giving 300 fits. 60 forgets the cross-validation multiplier." },
    { q: "In MLOps, what is the main purpose of continuous training (CT) automation?", a: ["Retraining models automatically when new data or drift arrives", "Running unit tests automatically whenever code is committed", "Packaging the model as a container on each new release", "Generating documentation automatically from model code"], correct: "Retraining models automatically when new data or drift arrives", explanation: "Continuous training re-runs the training pipeline on triggers like a schedule, new data or detected drift. Testing on commit is continuous integration, and packaging releases is continuous delivery." },
    { q: "Which practice most directly helps another analyst get exactly the same model results from your code?", a: ["Fixing random seeds and pinning library versions", "Training on a larger, faster GPU instance", "Adding more features to the training data", "Increasing the number of training epochs"], correct: "Fixing random seeds and pinning library versions", explanation: "Seeds control random splits and initialization, and pinned versions prevent library changes from altering outputs. Hardware, features and epochs change results rather than reproduce them." },
    { q: "Which of these tools is primarily used for experiment tracking in machine learning?", a: ["MLflow", "Kafka", "Tableau", "Terraform"], correct: "MLflow", explanation: "MLflow logs parameters, metrics and artifacts for each run so runs can be compared. Kafka streams events, Tableau visualizes data, and Terraform provisions infrastructure." },
    { q: "A fraud model's inputs look the same as before, but fraudsters change tactics so the same inputs now mean a different label. What is this called?", a: ["Concept drift", "Covariate shift", "Label leakage", "Overfitting"], correct: "Concept drift", explanation: "Concept drift is a change in the relationship between inputs and the target. Covariate shift is a change in the input distribution itself, which isn't the case here." },
    { q: "Which tool is designed to version large datasets alongside Git-tracked code?", a: ["DVC", "Docker", "Jupyter", "pandas"], correct: "DVC", explanation: "DVC (Data Version Control) tracks dataset versions with small pointer files in Git. Docker packages environments, Jupyter runs notebooks, and pandas manipulates data." },
    { q: "Which of these algorithms is LEAST affected by whether features are scaled?", a: ["Decision tree", "k-nearest neighbors", "Support vector machine", "k-means clustering"], correct: "Decision tree", explanation: "Trees split on thresholds of one feature at a time, so rescaling doesn't change the splits. k-NN, SVMs and k-means rely on distances, which scaling changes." },
    { q: "Which dimensionality reduction technique is mainly used to visualize high-dimensional data in 2D while preserving local neighborhoods?", a: ["t-SNE", "Lasso", "Ridge", "One-hot encoding"], correct: "t-SNE", explanation: "t-SNE maps points to 2D or 3D so that nearby points stay near each other. Lasso and Ridge are regularized regressions, and one-hot encoding increases dimensions." },
    { q: "Which algorithm detects anomalies by randomly partitioning data and flagging points that get isolated in very few splits?", a: ["Isolation Forest", "Random Forest", "k-means", "Naive Bayes"], correct: "Isolation Forest", explanation: "Anomalies are few and different, so random splits isolate them quickly with short path lengths. Random Forest is a supervised predictor, not an anomaly detector." },
    { q: "What does SQL 'ORDER BY price DESC' do when no other ordering is given?", a: ["Sorts rows from highest to lowest price", "Sorts rows from lowest to highest price", "Keeps only rows with a non-null price", "Groups rows that share the same price"], correct: "Sorts rows from highest to lowest price", explanation: "DESC means descending, largest first; ascending (ASC) is the default. Filtering is WHERE and grouping is GROUP BY." },
    { q: "In a relational database, what is a trigger?", a: ["Code that runs automatically on an INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE", "A saved query that users run on demand to get a result", "A constraint that links a column to another table's key", "A structure that speeds up lookups on a chosen column"], correct: "Code that runs automatically on an INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE", explanation: "Triggers fire in response to data-change events. A saved query is a view or stored procedure, a link to another table is a foreign key, and fast lookups come from an index." },
    { q: "In a data warehouse star schema, what sits at the center of the star?", a: ["A fact table", "A dimension table", "A staging table", "A lookup index"], correct: "A fact table", explanation: "The central fact table holds measures (e.g. sales amounts) with keys to surrounding dimension tables such as date, product and store." },
    { q: "Which workload is most typical of an OLTP (Online Transaction Processing) system?", a: ["Many small inserts and updates, like recording orders", "Large scans that aggregate years of historical sales", "Nightly batch jobs that rebuild summary tables", "Training machine learning models on archived data"], correct: "Many small inserts and updates, like recording orders", explanation: "OLTP handles high volumes of short, real-time transactions. Large historical aggregations are OLAP workloads." },
    { q: "Why do many OLAP (Online Analytical Processing) databases store data by column rather than by row?", a: ["Queries read only the few columns they aggregate", "Single-row inserts and updates become much faster", "Foreign keys and joins are no longer needed at all", "Each row can then have a completely different schema"], correct: "Queries read only the few columns they aggregate", explanation: "Analytical queries touch a few columns across many rows, so columnar storage reads less data and compresses well. Fast single-row writes are a strength of row stores, not column stores." },
    { q: "Pipeline orchestrators usually model a workflow as a DAG. What does DAG stand for?", a: ["Directed acyclic graph", "Distributed aggregate grid", "Dynamic allocation group", "Data access gateway"], correct: "Directed acyclic graph", explanation: "Tasks are nodes and dependencies are directed edges with no cycles, so there is always a valid order to run them." },
    { q: "In Apache Airflow, how are workflows (DAGs) normally defined?", a: ["As Python code files", "As SQL stored procedures", "As YAML files only", "As drag-and-drop diagrams"], correct: "As Python code files", explanation: "Airflow DAGs are written as Python scripts that declare tasks and dependencies. Some other tools use YAML or visual designers, but Airflow's core approach is code." },
    { q: "Which technology is most associated with real-time stream processing rather than batch processing?", a: ["Apache Kafka", "Apache Hive", "Cron jobs", "Nightly ETL"], correct: "Apache Kafka", explanation: "Kafka carries continuous event streams that consumers process as they arrive. Hive queries stored data in batches, and cron and nightly ETL run on fixed schedules." },
    { q: "With schema evolution in a format like Avro, which change is usually backward compatible?", a: ["Adding a new field with a default value", "Changing a field's type from string to int", "Renaming a field without adding an alias", "Adding a new field with no default value"], correct: "Adding a new field with a default value", explanation: "Backward compatible means a reader on the new schema can read old records. A new field with a default lets that reader fill in the value for old records; without a default, old records have nothing to fill it with. Incompatible type changes and unaliased renames also break old data." },
    { q: "Which of these is a binary, column-oriented serialization format popular for analytics?", a: ["Parquet", "JSON", "CSV", "XML"], correct: "Parquet", explanation: "Parquet stores data in compressed binary columns, which suits analytical queries. JSON, CSV and XML are text formats organized by record." },
    { q: "Why is deserializing data from an untrusted source with Python's pickle considered dangerous?", a: ["It can execute arbitrary code during loading", "It silently drops all float precision values", "It needs far more RAM than the input file", "It can only read files on the same machine"], correct: "It can execute arbitrary code during loading", explanation: "Unpickling can construct objects that run code, so a malicious file can take over the process. Safer formats like JSON only rebuild plain data." },
    { q: "In a model-serving setup, what is an API 'endpoint'?", a: ["A specific URL that clients send requests to", "The JSON schema describing a request body", "The authentication token attached to a request", "The server process that hosts the trained model"], correct: "A specific URL that clients send requests to", explanation: "An endpoint is the address (such as /predict) where a client sends requests. The server process sits behind it, and the schema and token are things the request carries." },
    { q: "In ML inference, what does 'latency' measure?", a: ["Time to return a single prediction", "Number of predictions served per second", "Time needed to retrain on fresh data", "Memory footprint of the loaded model"], correct: "Time to return a single prediction", explanation: "Latency is the delay between sending a request and getting its prediction back. Predictions per second is throughput, a different metric." },
    { q: "A serving system handles 2,000 requests per second, and each request takes 50 ms. Which figure is its throughput?", a: ["2,000 requests per second", "50 milliseconds per request", "40 requests per second", "100 seconds of compute per second"], correct: "2,000 requests per second", explanation: "Throughput is the work completed per unit of time. The 50 ms per request is latency, and 40/s comes from wrongly dividing the two numbers." },
    { q: "In MLOps, what does 'model serving' refer to?", a: ["Exposing a trained model to answer live prediction requests", "Storing versioned model files with their training metadata", "Scheduling recurring retraining jobs on new data batches", "Packaging a model's code and dependencies into an image"], correct: "Exposing a trained model to answer live prediction requests", explanation: "Serving makes a model available, usually behind an API, so applications can get predictions. Storing versioned models is the job of a model registry." },
    { q: "An A/B test reports p = 0.03. What does this p-value mean?", a: ["3% chance of data this extreme if there's no true effect", "3% chance that variant B is not really better than A", "97% probability that the observed lift is genuine", "3% chance a repeat of the test would not replicate it"], correct: "3% chance of data this extreme if there's no true effect", explanation: "A p-value assumes the null hypothesis is true and gives the probability of a result at least this extreme. It is not the probability that the hypothesis itself is true or false." },
    { q: "Which activity is an example of statistical inference?", a: ["Estimating a city's average income from 500 surveyed homes", "Reporting the median income of the 500 surveyed households", "Plotting a histogram of incomes among surveyed households", "Removing duplicate households from the survey response file"], correct: "Estimating a city's average income from 500 surveyed homes", explanation: "Inference uses a sample to draw conclusions about a larger population. Summarizing or plotting only the surveyed households is descriptive." },
    { q: "Which of these is a descriptive statistic rather than an inferential one?", a: ["The standard deviation of test scores in your class", "A 95% confidence interval for the national mean score", "A p-value comparing two schools' average test scores", "A regression forecast of next year's mean test score"], correct: "The standard deviation of test scores in your class", explanation: "Descriptive statistics summarize the data you actually have. Confidence intervals, p-values and forecasts all generalize beyond the observed data." },
    { q: "Why do inferential results come with margins of error, while a summary of a complete dataset usually does not?", a: ["They generalize from a sample, so sampling variability adds uncertainty", "They rely on larger datasets, which accumulate more measurement error", "They assume normally distributed data, which adds approximation error", "They involve more arithmetic steps, so rounding error must be reported"], correct: "They generalize from a sample, so sampling variability adds uncertainty", explanation: "A different sample would give a slightly different estimate, and the margin of error measures that sampling variability. Many inferential methods don't assume normality at all." },
    { q: "Which task is the best example of data wrangling?", a: ["Parsing messy date strings and reshaping a table wide to long", "Choosing the number of trees for a random forest via cross-validation", "Designing a dashboard to show weekly sales numbers to executives", "Calculating a confidence interval for the average order value"], correct: "Parsing messy date strings and reshaping a table wide to long", explanation: "Wrangling (or munging) means cleaning, parsing and restructuring raw data into a usable form. Tuning a model and building a dashboard come later in the workflow." },
    { q: "On streaming platforms like Kafka, what is a schema registry?", a: ["A central service that stores and versions message schemas", "A broker that routes messages between producers and topics", "A catalog listing every dataset and its business owner", "A log recording which jobs transformed each data field"], correct: "A central service that stores and versions message schemas", explanation: "A schema registry holds the schemas for messages (for example Avro) so producers and consumers agree on the format. A catalog of datasets and owners is a data catalog." },
    { q: "What is the main purpose of a data catalog?", a: ["Helping users find datasets via searchable metadata", "Storing the raw data files in a cheap central location", "Enforcing compatible versions of message schemas", "Tracking runtime metrics for scheduled pipelines"], correct: "Helping users find datasets via searchable metadata", explanation: "A data catalog is a searchable inventory of data assets: descriptions, owners, tags. It doesn't store the data itself, which is the role of a lake or warehouse." },
    { q: "An analyst finds a wrong revenue figure and wants to trace which upstream tables and transformations produced it. Which capability helps most?", a: ["Data lineage", "Data catalog", "Schema registry", "Data profiling"], correct: "Data lineage", explanation: "Lineage records where data came from and every transformation it went through, so errors can be traced upstream. Profiling only summarizes what a dataset currently contains." },
    { q: "Which is a defining characteristic of cloud storage compared with a local disk?", a: ["Data lives on a provider's remote servers, reached over a network", "Data reads faster than from a locally attached solid-state drive", "Data must be stored in relational tables with fixed schemas", "Data is encrypted with keys held only on the user's own laptop"], correct: "Data lives on a provider's remote servers, reached over a network", explanation: "Cloud storage is hosted remotely by a provider and accessed over the network. That network hop usually makes it slower than a local SSD, not faster." },
    { q: "Services like Amazon S3 are 'object storage.' How do they organize data?", a: ["As objects with metadata, each addressed by a key", "As fixed-size blocks attached to a single virtual machine", "As files in a nested directory tree with POSIX permissions", "As rows in tables linked by primary and foreign keys"], correct: "As objects with metadata, each addressed by a key", explanation: "Object stores keep each blob with its metadata in a flat namespace, retrieved by key. Folder-like paths in S3 are just key prefixes, not a real directory tree." },
    { q: "What is the main reason to use a distributed computing framework like Spark?", a: ["To split work across machines when one is too slow or small", "To speed up a single CPU core through instruction pipelining", "To keep all data on one machine and avoid network transfers", "To run several models in sequence on one shared GPU card"], correct: "To split work across machines when one is too slow or small", explanation: "Distributed frameworks partition data and computation across a cluster so jobs can exceed one machine's memory or speed. Network transfer is a cost they accept, not one they avoid." },
    { q: "In Apache Spark, what is Spark SQL?", a: ["The module for querying structured data with SQL", "Spark's library of distributed machine-learning algorithms", "The engine for processing live data streams in micro-batches", "Spark's API for analyzing graphs of vertices and edges"], correct: "The module for querying structured data with SQL", explanation: "Spark SQL handles structured data through SQL queries and the DataFrame API. The other options describe MLlib, Spark Streaming and GraphX." },
    { q: "In Spark, what is a partition of a DataFrame?", a: ["A chunk of rows processed in parallel by one task", "A single column stored separately for compression", "A cached copy of the DataFrame kept on the driver", "A filtered subset of rows that match a WHERE clause"], correct: "A chunk of rows processed in parallel by one task", explanation: "Spark splits a DataFrame's rows into partitions spread across executors, and each task processes one partition. That is how the work runs in parallel." },
    { q: "When joining an orders table to a customers table, what is the join key?", a: ["The column whose values are matched in both tables", "The column used to sort the result after the join", "The index built to speed up lookups on the larger table", "The column that must be unique in the joined output"], correct: "The column whose values are matched in both tables", explanation: "The join key (such as customer_id) is compared across the two tables to pair up rows. An index can make that faster, but it isn't the key itself." },
    { q: "In an ETL pipeline, what happens during the 'T' (transform) step?", a: ["Data is cleaned, reformatted and joined into shape", "Data is pulled from source systems like APIs and databases", "Data is written into the destination warehouse tables", "Data is copied raw into a lake before any processing"], correct: "Data is cleaned, reformatted and joined into shape", explanation: "Extract pulls the data, Transform cleans and reshapes it, and Load writes it to the target. Copying raw data first describes the ELT pattern." },
    { q: "Orchestrators like Airflow define pipelines as a DAG. What does the 'acyclic' part guarantee?", a: ["No task can depend, directly or indirectly, on itself", "Every task runs in parallel with every other task", "Each task runs exactly once per day on a schedule", "Failed tasks are retried automatically until success"], correct: "No task can depend, directly or indirectly, on itself", explanation: "Acyclic means the graph has no cycles, so there is always a valid order to run the tasks. Parallelism, scheduling and retries are separate settings." },
    { q: "In data systems, what does a job scheduler such as cron or Airflow's scheduler do?", a: ["Triggers tasks at set times or when their dependencies finish", "Allocates CPU time slices among threads in the operating system", "Balances incoming web requests across replicas of a service", "Deletes logs from past runs after a fixed number of days"], correct: "Triggers tasks at set times or when their dependencies finish", explanation: "A job scheduler starts pipeline tasks on a timetable or once their upstream tasks finish. CPU time-slicing is done by the operating system's process scheduler, which is a different thing." },
    { q: "Why do long-running data processing jobs use checkpointing?", a: ["So a failed job can resume from saved state, not from scratch", "So intermediate results can be shown live on a monitoring dashboard", "So partitions are rebalanced evenly across all the worker nodes", "So output is validated against the schema before being written"], correct: "So a failed job can resume from saved state, not from scratch", explanation: "Checkpointing saves intermediate state to durable storage, so after a failure the job restarts from the last checkpoint. It is a fault-tolerance feature, not a monitoring one." },
    { q: "A pipeline rejects a record because its 'age' field contains the string \"twenty\" instead of an integer. Which check caught it?", a: ["Schema validation", "Deduplication", "Data lineage", "Outlier detection"], correct: "Schema validation", explanation: "Schema validation checks that fields have the expected types and structure. Outlier detection looks for unusual numeric values, but \"twenty\" isn't a number at all." },
    { q: "A fraud model's inputs shift because customers adopt a new payment app, but what counts as fraud is unchanged. What is this called?", a: ["Data drift", "Concept drift", "Label leakage", "Overfitting"], correct: "Data drift", explanation: "Data drift (covariate shift) is a change in the input distribution. Concept drift would mean the relationship between the inputs and fraud itself had changed." },
    { q: "Which scenario is concept drift rather than just data drift?", a: ["Customers with identical features now churn at a different rate", "The average age of incoming users rises over several months", "A sensor starts reporting Fahrenheit instead of Celsius", "Mobile traffic grows from 30% to 60% of all requests"], correct: "Customers with identical features now churn at a different rate", explanation: "Concept drift means P(y | x) changes: the same inputs now lead to different outcomes. A change in the input mix alone is data drift, and a unit switch is a data-quality bug." },
    { q: "What is the main role of a monitoring dashboard in a production ML system?", a: ["Displaying key metrics over time so people can spot problems", "Paging on-call staff the moment a threshold is breached", "Storing raw application logs for later forensic searching", "Recording which model version is deployed to each endpoint"], correct: "Displaying key metrics over time so people can spot problems", explanation: "A dashboard visualizes metrics such as latency, error rate and prediction distribution so people can inspect them. Paging people automatically is the job of alerting." },
    { q: "How does an alerting system differ from a monitoring dashboard?", a: ["It notifies people when a rule's condition is met", "It stores metrics at higher resolution for longer", "It shows historical trends across many services at once", "It aggregates logs from all servers into one search index"], correct: "It notifies people when a rule's condition is met", explanation: "Alerting pushes a notification (page, email, chat message) when a rule fires, so no one has to be watching. Dashboards wait for someone to look at them." },
    { q: "A data team formally promises consumers that the daily sales table will be ready by 6 a.m. on 99% of days. What is this commitment called?", a: ["Service level agreement (SLA)", "Key performance indicator (KPI)", "Recovery time objective (RTO)", "Recovery point objective (RPO)"], correct: "Service level agreement (SLA)", explanation: "An SLA is a commitment to consumers about service performance, such as delivery time or availability. RTO and RPO are disaster-recovery targets." },
    { q: "What core problem does a feature store solve for ML teams?", a: ["Keeping features consistent between training and serving", "Versioning trained models and promoting them to production", "Tracking hyperparameters and metrics from each experiment", "Labeling raw examples through a crowdsourcing workflow"], correct: "Keeping features consistent between training and serving", explanation: "A feature store centralizes feature definitions so the same logic feeds both training and inference, which avoids training/serving skew. Versioning models is a model registry's job." },
    { q: "In a feature store, how are 'offline' features typically produced?", a: ["In scheduled batch jobs over historical data", "On request, as each prediction call arrives", "From a live event stream with millisecond lag", "By the model itself during its forward pass"], correct: "In scheduled batch jobs over historical data", explanation: "Offline features are precomputed in batch, often daily, over historical data. They are mainly used to build training sets." },
    { q: "Why does a feature store keep an 'online' store separate from its offline store?", a: ["To serve the latest values with low latency at inference", "To hold the full feature history needed for training sets", "To keep a backup copy of features for disaster recovery", "To hide sensitive features from data scientists' notebooks"], correct: "To serve the latest values with low latency at inference", explanation: "The online store (often a key-value database) holds only current feature values for fast lookups during prediction. The full history lives in the offline store." },
    { q: "What is a model registry used for in MLOps?", a: ["Versioning trained models and their deployment stage", "Storing precomputed features for training and inference", "Logging every hyperparameter tried during a tuning sweep", "Routing prediction traffic between two model versions"], correct: "Versioning trained models and their deployment stage", explanation: "A registry stores model versions with metadata and stage labels such as staging or production. Storing features is a feature store's job, and splitting traffic is handled by the serving layer." },
    { q: "In software engineering and MLOps, what does CI/CD stand for?", a: ["Continuous Integration / Continuous Delivery", "Continuous Inspection / Continuous Development", "Code Integration / Cluster Deployment", "Container Isolation / Container Deployment"], correct: "Continuous Integration / Continuous Delivery", explanation: "CI automatically builds and tests every code change. CD stands for Continuous Delivery, or Continuous Deployment when releases go out automatically. 'Development' is a common mix-up." },
    { q: "What is the main benefit of containerizing an application?", a: ["It bundles dependencies so it runs the same on any host", "It runs a full guest operating system with its own kernel", "It compiles the code to machine instructions for faster runs", "It encrypts the application so its source cannot be read"], correct: "It bundles dependencies so it runs the same on any host", explanation: "A container packages the app with its libraries, so it behaves the same on any host with a container runtime. Containers share the host's kernel; a full guest OS describes a virtual machine." },
    { q: "In Docker, what is the difference between an image and a container?", a: ["An image is a read-only template; a container is a running instance of it", "A container is the template; an image is a running instance built from it", "An image holds the code; a container holds the attached data volumes", "They are two names for the same packaged application artifact on disk"], correct: "An image is a read-only template; a container is a running instance of it", explanation: "You build an image from a Dockerfile, then start one or more containers from it, much like objects created from a class. Volumes are a separate storage mechanism." },
    { q: "What is Kubernetes mainly used for?", a: ["Orchestrating containers across a cluster of machines", "Building container images from a Dockerfile's steps", "Scheduling batch data pipelines as DAGs of tasks", "Hosting and versioning container images in a registry"], correct: "Orchestrating containers across a cluster of machines", explanation: "Kubernetes schedules, scales and heals containers across many nodes. Images are built with tools like Docker and stored in a registry." },
    { q: "A service handles 10× more users by adding more servers behind a load balancer. What kind of scaling is this?", a: ["Horizontal scaling (scaling out)", "Vertical scaling (scaling up)", "Vertical partitioning of tables", "Denormalizing the database schema"], correct: "Horizontal scaling (scaling out)", explanation: "Adding more machines is horizontal scaling. Vertical scaling means giving one machine more CPU or memory." },
    { q: "What distinguishes elasticity from plain scalability in cloud computing?", a: ["Resources grow and shrink automatically with current demand", "Capacity can be increased by buying larger physical servers", "The system stays available even if a data center goes down", "Costs stay fixed regardless of how much traffic arrives"], correct: "Resources grow and shrink automatically with current demand", explanation: "Scalability is the ability to handle more load. Elasticity adds automatic provisioning and release of resources as demand rises and falls." },
    { q: "Which practice is a common cloud cost optimization for fault-tolerant batch jobs?", a: ["Running them on discounted spot or preemptible instances", "Running them on dedicated hosts reserved for a single tenant", "Over-provisioning instances so jobs don't wait in a queue", "Replicating each job's output across three separate regions"], correct: "Running them on discounted spot or preemptible instances", explanation: "Spot or preemptible capacity can be far cheaper, and jobs that can restart tolerate being interrupted. Dedicated hosts and over-provisioning raise costs." },
    { q: "Which is an example of encryption at rest?", a: ["A database's disk files are encrypted with AES-256", "A browser connects to an API over HTTPS (TLS)", "An API request is signed with an HMAC token", "A VPN tunnel protects traffic between two offices"], correct: "A database's disk files are encrypted with AES-256", explanation: "Encryption at rest protects stored data on disk. HTTPS and VPNs protect data in transit, and an HMAC signature proves integrity without encrypting anything." },
    { q: "Which protocol is most commonly used to encrypt data in transit between a client and a web API?", a: ["TLS", "AES-256", "SHA-256", "Kerberos"], correct: "TLS", explanation: "TLS (the 'S' in HTTPS) encrypts network traffic. AES is a cipher TLS can use internally, SHA-256 is a hash function, and Kerberos is an authentication protocol." },
    { q: "A user logs in with a password plus a one-time code from their phone. Which security process is this?", a: ["Authentication", "Authorization", "Auditing", "Encryption in transit"], correct: "Authentication", explanation: "Authentication verifies who someone is, here with two factors. Authorization decides what that verified user may do." },
    { q: "An authenticated analyst can read the sales table but gets 'permission denied' when trying to drop it. Which process blocked the action?", a: ["Authorization", "Authentication", "Data masking", "Rate limiting"], correct: "Authorization", explanation: "The analyst's identity was already verified (authentication). Authorization then checked their permissions and denied the drop." },
    { q: "Which list orders standard logging levels from least to most severe?", a: ["DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR", "INFO, DEBUG, WARNING, ERROR", "DEBUG, WARNING, INFO, ERROR", "INFO, WARNING, DEBUG, ERROR"], correct: "DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR", explanation: "DEBUG is the most verbose and least severe, then INFO, WARNING and ERROR (CRITICAL is above that). Setting a level shows that level and everything more severe." },
    { q: "Why should a web framework's debug mode be turned off in production?", a: ["Its detailed error pages can expose code and secrets", "It stops the app from writing any log messages", "It disables authentication so developers can test", "It turns off HTTPS so traffic can be inspected"], correct: "Its detailed error pages can expose code and secrets", explanation: "Debug mode shows stack traces, settings and sometimes an interactive console to anyone who triggers an error. That is useful in development and dangerous in production." },
    { q: "A service promises 99.9% uptime. Roughly how much downtime does that allow in a 30-day month?", a: ["About 43 minutes", "About 4.3 minutes", "About 7 hours", "About 3.6 days"], correct: "About 43 minutes", explanation: "30 days is 43,200 minutes, and 0.1% of that is about 43 minutes. 99% would allow about 7 hours, and 99.99% about 4.3 minutes." },
    { q: "Availability is often estimated as MTBF ÷ (MTBF + MTTR). Which change most directly shortens the downtime of each incident?", a: ["Lowering MTTR, the mean time to repair", "Raising MTTR, the mean time to repair", "Lowering MTBF, the mean time between failures", "Lowering RPO, the recovery point objective"], correct: "Lowering MTTR, the mean time to repair", explanation: "MTTR is how long each outage lasts, so lowering it cuts downtime per incident. Lowering MTBF would make failures more frequent, and RPO concerns data loss, not downtime." },
    { q: "A database is backed up every 6 hours and nothing else is replicated. If it fails just before the next backup, how much data could be lost?", a: ["Almost 6 hours of changes", "Almost 3 hours of changes", "Almost 12 hours of changes", "None, since backups exist"], correct: "Almost 6 hours of changes", explanation: "Everything written since the last backup is lost, which is up to the full backup interval. This is why backup frequency sets the recovery point objective (RPO)." },
    { q: "In disaster recovery planning, what does the recovery time objective (RTO) specify?", a: ["The longest acceptable time to restore service", "The largest acceptable amount of data lost", "The interval between scheduled full backups", "The average time between system failures"], correct: "The longest acceptable time to restore service", explanation: "RTO limits how long you can be down after a disaster. The acceptable amount of lost data is the RPO, the other main DR target." },
    { q: "What does a data retention policy specify?", a: ["How long each kind of data is kept", "Who is allowed to read or modify each dataset", "How often each dataset is backed up offsite", "Which fields must be masked before sharing"], correct: "How long each kind of data is kept", explanation: "Retention policies set how long data is stored and when it must be deleted or archived, often for legal reasons. Access rules belong to access control." },
    { q: "A team adds a column to a production table using a versioned script that can be rolled back. What is this practice called?", a: ["Schema migration", "Schema validation", "Data backfill", "Change data capture"], correct: "Schema migration", explanation: "Schema migrations are versioned, ordered changes to a database's structure. A backfill populates data, while validation checks records against an existing schema." },
    { q: "An event happens at 9:00, lands in the warehouse at 9:12 and appears on a dashboard at 9:15. What is the end-to-end pipeline latency?", a: ["15 minutes", "12 minutes", "3 minutes", "27 minutes"], correct: "15 minutes", explanation: "End-to-end latency runs from the event to its availability for the final consumer: 9:00 to 9:15. 12 minutes covers only the ingestion leg." },
    { q: "Observability is often described with three 'pillars.' Which set is it?", a: ["Metrics, logs and traces", "Metrics, alerts and dashboards", "Logs, backups and audits", "Traces, schemas and lineage"], correct: "Metrics, logs and traces", explanation: "Metrics, logs and distributed traces are the telemetry that let you infer a system's internal state. Alerts and dashboards are built on top of them." },
    { q: "Which log pattern is most likely an anomaly worth investigating?", a: ["Error rate jumps from 0.1% to 8% within five minutes", "Request volume rises each weekday morning as users log in", "The nightly batch job logs 'completed' at 2 a.m. as usual", "Debug messages appear in a staging environment's logs"], correct: "Error rate jumps from 0.1% to 8% within five minutes", explanation: "A sudden, large departure from normal behavior is an anomaly. Predictable daily patterns and expected messages are normal baseline activity." },
    { q: "A dashboard last refreshed at 8:00 and it is now 11:00. Which data quality dimension does this 3-hour gap concern?", a: ["Freshness", "Completeness", "Accuracy", "Consistency"], correct: "Freshness", explanation: "Freshness (or timeliness) is how up to date the data is. The values could still be complete and accurate as of 8:00." },
    { q: "A stream job counts clicks in fixed, non-overlapping 1-minute intervals. What type of window is this?", a: ["Tumbling window", "Sliding window", "Session window", "Global window"], correct: "Tumbling window", explanation: "Tumbling windows are fixed-size and don't overlap, so each event falls in exactly one window. Sliding windows overlap, and session windows close after a gap in activity." },
    { q: "In stream processing, what does a watermark represent?", a: ["The event time up to which input is assumed complete", "The offset the consumer last committed to the broker", "The point at which job state was saved to storage", "The processing time when each event was received"], correct: "The event time up to which input is assumed complete", explanation: "A watermark says roughly 'no more events older than time T are expected', which lets windows close and makes late data identifiable. Saved state is a checkpoint." },
    { q: "Together with replayable sources and transactional sinks, checkpointing lets stream processors like Flink provide which guarantee?", a: ["Exactly-once processing", "At-most-once processing", "Zero end-to-end latency", "Strict global event ordering"], correct: "Exactly-once processing", explanation: "After a failure the job rolls back to a consistent checkpoint and replays input, so each event affects the results exactly once. Checkpointing adds latency rather than removing it." },
    { q: "In a Spark join, one customer ID accounts for 40% of rows, so one task runs for an hour while the others finish in minutes. What is this problem called?", a: ["Data skew", "Data drift", "Small files problem", "Memory spill"], correct: "Data skew", explanation: "Skew means data is unevenly spread across partitions, so a few tasks do most of the work. Salting the hot key is a common fix." },
    { q: "Why is 'country' often a poor partition key for a global events table?", a: ["A few countries hold most rows, giving very uneven partitions", "Country names are strings, and partition keys must be numeric", "Too many distinct values create millions of tiny partitions", "Country values change often, forcing rows to move partitions"], correct: "A few countries hold most rows, giving very uneven partitions", explanation: "Traffic is concentrated in a few countries, so their partitions become hot spots (skew). There are only about 200 countries, far too few to cause a tiny-partition problem." },
    { q: "In a schema registry such as Confluent's, what does a 'backward compatible' schema change mean?", a: ["Consumers on the new schema can read data written with the old one", "Consumers on the old schema can read data written with the new one", "The old and new schemas must be identical field for field", "Producers may change field types freely if the names match"], correct: "Consumers on the new schema can read data written with the old one", explanation: "Backward compatibility means the new schema can read old data, so consumers can upgrade first. Old readers handling new data is forward compatibility." },
    { q: "What does a data lakehouse (for example, built with Delta Lake or Iceberg) add on top of a data lake?", a: ["Warehouse features like ACID tables over open files", "A separate copy of all data loaded into a proprietary warehouse", "Real-time stream processing in place of batch storage", "Schema-free storage that skips table metadata entirely"], correct: "Warehouse features like ACID tables over open files", explanation: "Lakehouse table formats add transactions, schema enforcement and time travel on top of cheap object storage, so no separate warehouse copy is needed." },
    { q: "Which is a post-hoc explainability method, applied after a black-box model is already trained?", a: ["Partial dependence plots", "Lasso feature shrinkage", "Monotonic constraints", "Intrinsically simple models"], correct: "Partial dependence plots", explanation: "Partial dependence plots probe a trained model by varying one feature and averaging the predictions. The other options shape the model during training, which builds in interpretability rather than explaining afterward." },
    { q: "For one prediction, the model's average (base) output is 0.30 and the SHAP values of its features sum to +0.25. What is this prediction?", a: ["0.55", "0.25", "0.05", "0.30"], correct: "0.55", explanation: "SHAP values are additive: the base value plus all feature contributions equals the prediction, so 0.30 + 0.25 = 0.55." },
    { q: "How does LIME explain an individual prediction?", a: ["It fits a simple model to perturbed samples near that input", "It averages each feature's marginal contribution over all orderings", "It backpropagates gradients from the output to the input pixels", "It retrains the model without each feature and compares outputs"], correct: "It fits a simple model to perturbed samples near that input", explanation: "LIME (Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations) builds a local, simple surrogate model around one prediction. Averaging over feature orderings describes Shapley values." },
    { q: "Which model is generally considered the most intrinsically interpretable?", a: ["A logistic regression on five features", "A gradient-boosted ensemble of 500 trees", "A deep convolutional neural network", "A random forest of 200 deep trees"], correct: "A logistic regression on five features", explanation: "A small logistic regression can be read directly from its coefficients. Large ensembles and deep networks need post-hoc tools to explain them." },
    { q: "A classifier has an ROC AUC of 0.5. What does that indicate?", a: ["It ranks positives no better than chance", "It correctly classifies half of all examples", "It is perfectly wrong, inverting every ranking", "It has 50% precision at the default threshold"], correct: "It ranks positives no better than chance", explanation: "AUC is the probability that a random positive is scored above a random negative, so 0.5 means chance level. A perfectly inverted model would have AUC = 0." },
    { q: "A confusion matrix shows TP = 40, FP = 10, FN = 20, TN = 30. What is the precision?", a: ["0.80", "0.67", "0.70", "0.75"], correct: "0.80", explanation: "Precision = TP / (TP + FP) = 40/50 = 0.80. 0.67 is recall (40/60), 0.70 is accuracy and 0.75 is specificity." },
    { q: "Which is a feature selection method rather than a feature extraction method?", a: ["Recursive feature elimination", "Principal component analysis", "Feature hashing", "Linear discriminant analysis"], correct: "Recursive feature elimination", explanation: "Selection keeps a subset of the original features. PCA, LDA and autoencoders build new combined features instead (extraction)." },
    { q: "Why should a model's final evaluation use a held-out test set rather than the training data?", a: ["Training-set scores overstate how well it generalizes", "Test sets are larger, so their metrics have less variance", "Training data can't be passed through a model twice", "Held-out data makes the model's loss function convex"], correct: "Training-set scores overstate how well it generalizes", explanation: "A model can memorize its training data, so only unseen data gives an honest estimate of real-world performance. Test sets are usually smaller than training sets, not larger." },
    { q: "In a dataset where 95% of transactions are legitimate, a fraud model reaches 95% accuracy. Why compare it to a baseline?", a: ["Predicting 'legitimate' every time also scores 95%", "Baselines raise accuracy by adding prior information", "Accuracy can't be computed without a reference model", "A random coin-flip baseline would score only about 5%"], correct: "Predicting 'legitimate' every time also scores 95%", explanation: "A trivial majority-class baseline matches the model's 95% accuracy, so the model may have learned nothing. A coin flip would score about 50%, not 5%." },
    { q: "During hyperparameter tuning, which of these would you search over?", a: ["The learning rate of gradient descent", "The weights of a neural network layer", "The coefficients of a linear regression", "The split thresholds inside a fitted tree"], correct: "The learning rate of gradient descent", explanation: "Hyperparameters such as the learning rate are set before training and tuned from outside. Weights, coefficients and split points are parameters the training process learns." },
    { q: "In one iteration of a typical neural network training loop, what is the correct order of steps?", a: ["Forward pass, compute loss, backpropagate, update weights", "Compute loss, forward pass, update weights, backpropagate", "Forward pass, update weights, compute loss, backpropagate", "Backpropagate, forward pass, compute loss, update weights"], correct: "Forward pass, compute loss, backpropagate, update weights", explanation: "You need predictions before you can compute a loss, and gradients from backpropagation before the optimizer can update the weights." },
    { q: "What does batch normalization do inside a neural network?", a: ["Rescales layer inputs using the batch mean and variance", "Rescales the input features once before training starts", "Randomly zeroes activations to reduce overfitting", "Clips gradients whose norm exceeds a set threshold"], correct: "Rescales layer inputs using the batch mean and variance", explanation: "Batch norm normalizes each layer's inputs per mini-batch, then applies a learned scale and shift, which stabilizes and speeds up training. Randomly zeroing activations is dropout." },
    { q: "How does dropout behave at inference time in a standard implementation?", a: ["It is turned off, so all neurons are used", "It drops the same neurons as in the last epoch", "It drops neurons at twice the training rate", "It drops whole layers instead of single neurons"], correct: "It is turned off, so all neurons are used", explanation: "Dropout only acts during training. With the usual 'inverted dropout', activations are scaled during training, so inference uses the full network unchanged." },
    { q: "What happens if every layer of a deep network uses a linear activation function?", a: ["The whole network collapses to one linear map", "The network can still fit any nonlinear function", "Gradients vanish and training cannot begin", "Each layer's outputs are squashed between 0 and 1"], correct: "The whole network collapses to one linear map", explanation: "A composition of linear functions is still linear, so depth adds no expressive power. Nonlinear activations are what let networks model complex functions." },
    { q: "What is the value of the sigmoid function σ(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(−x)) at x = 0?", a: ["0.5", "0", "1", "−1"], correct: "0.5", explanation: "At x = 0, e^0 = 1, so σ(0) = 1/2. The sigmoid's output always lies strictly between 0 and 1, which is why it is used for probabilities." },
    { q: "What does the ReLU activation output for an input of −3?", a: ["0", "−3", "3", "−0.03"], correct: "0", explanation: "ReLU(x) = max(0, x), so any negative input becomes 0. The −0.03 output would come from Leaky ReLU with slope 0.01." },
    { q: "Why are deep networks with sigmoid activations prone to vanishing gradients?", a: ["Its derivative is at most 0.25, so gradients shrink per layer", "Its outputs grow without bound as more layers are stacked", "Its gradient is exactly zero for every negative input", "It makes the loss function non-differentiable at zero"], correct: "Its derivative is at most 0.25, so gradients shrink per layer", explanation: "Backpropagation multiplies derivatives layer by layer, and factors of 0.25 or less shrink the gradient quickly. A zero gradient for every negative input describes ReLU, not sigmoid." },
    { q: "Which technique most directly targets exploding gradients?", a: ["Gradient clipping", "Dropout", "Data augmentation", "Early stopping"], correct: "Gradient clipping", explanation: "Gradient clipping caps the gradient's norm or values before each update, which prevents huge, destabilizing steps. The other options mainly fight overfitting." },
    { q: "Compared with one-hot encoding, what is a key property of learned word embeddings?", a: ["Similar words get nearby dense vectors", "Each word gets its own orthogonal axis", "Vectors are sparse with a single 1", "Vector length equals vocabulary size"], correct: "Similar words get nearby dense vectors", explanation: "Embeddings map words to short dense vectors where related words end up close together. The other three options describe one-hot encoding." },
    { q: "In clustering and retrieval, what does a similarity measure return for a pair of data points?", a: ["A score that is higher the more alike the points are", "A score that is higher the farther apart the points are", "The probability that both points share a class label", "The count of features on which the two points differ"], correct: "A score that is higher the more alike the points are", explanation: "Similarity grows as points become more alike. A score that grows with separation is a distance (dissimilarity), and counting differing features is Hamming distance." },
    { q: "Two document vectors point in exactly the same direction, but one is 3× longer than the other. What is their cosine similarity?", a: ["1", "0", "3", "1/3"], correct: "1", explanation: "Cosine similarity depends only on the angle between vectors, not their lengths. Same direction means an angle of 0°, and cos 0° = 1." },
    { q: "What is the Euclidean distance between the points (1, 2) and (4, 6)?", a: ["5", "7", "25", "3.5"], correct: "5", explanation: "√((4−1)² + (6−2)²) = √(9 + 16) = √25 = 5. The value 7 is the Manhattan distance (3 + 4), and 25 is the squared distance." },
    { q: "In k-nearest neighbors (KNN) classification, how is the class of a new point decided?", a: ["By majority vote among the k closest training points", "By the nearest of k centroids learned during training", "By splitting features until k points remain per leaf", "By which side of a maximum-margin boundary it falls on"], correct: "By majority vote among the k closest training points", explanation: "KNN stores the training data and lets the k nearest points vote. Learned centroids belong to k-means, leaves to decision trees, and maximum-margin boundaries to SVMs." },
    { q: "In data governance, what is a data catalog?", a: ["A searchable inventory of datasets and their metadata", "A query engine that runs SQL directly over lake files", "A log recording every change made to a table's rows", "A BI tool for publishing dashboards of business data"], correct: "A searchable inventory of datasets and their metadata", explanation: "A catalog helps people discover what data exists, who owns it, and what it means. It does not execute queries or store change logs itself." },
    { q: "In a relational database, what does a table's schema define?", a: ["Its column names, data types, and constraints", "The physical disk blocks where its rows are kept", "The order in which its rows were first inserted", "The specific rows the table holds at this moment"], correct: "Its column names, data types, and constraints", explanation: "The schema is the table's structure. The actual rows present at a given moment are the table's contents (an instance), not its schema." },
    { q: "Which SQL statement reads rows from a table without changing any data?", a: ["SELECT", "UPDATE", "INSERT", "MERGE"], correct: "SELECT", explanation: "SELECT retrieves data. UPDATE, INSERT, and MERGE all modify table contents." },
    { q: "Which SQL statement adds a new row to an existing table?", a: ["INSERT INTO", "ALTER TABLE", "UPDATE ... SET", "CREATE TABLE"], correct: "INSERT INTO", explanation: "INSERT INTO adds rows. ALTER TABLE changes the table's structure (for example, adding a column), not its rows." },
    { q: "What happens if you run UPDATE employees SET salary = 50000; with no WHERE clause?", a: ["Every row's salary is set to 50000", "Only the first row's salary is changed", "The statement fails with a syntax error", "A new row with salary 50000 is added"], correct: "Every row's salary is set to 50000", explanation: "Without WHERE, UPDATE applies to all rows in the table. That is valid SQL, which is exactly why it is a common and costly mistake." },
    { q: "How does DELETE FROM orders; (with no WHERE clause) differ from DROP TABLE orders;?", a: ["DELETE removes all rows but keeps the table", "DELETE removes the table and its definition", "DELETE removes only rows with NULL values", "DELETE fails unless a WHERE clause is given"], correct: "DELETE removes all rows but keeps the table", explanation: "DELETE without WHERE empties the table but leaves its structure in place. DROP TABLE removes the table definition itself." },
    { q: "In SQL, what is a constraint such as CHECK (age >= 0)?", a: ["A rule the database enforces on a table's values", "A filter applied to query results at read time", "An index that speeds up lookups on a column", "A trigger that logs changes to a separate table"], correct: "A rule the database enforces on a table's values", explanation: "Constraints reject writes that would violate a rule, protecting data integrity. A WHERE filter only affects what a query returns, not what can be stored." },
    { q: "In most SQL databases, how does a UNIQUE constraint differ from a PRIMARY KEY?", a: ["It can allow NULLs, and a table may have several", "It allows duplicates as long as other columns differ", "It applies only to text columns, not numeric ones", "It is enforced only when the column is not indexed"], correct: "It can allow NULLs, and a table may have several", explanation: "A table has one primary key, which cannot be NULL. It can have many UNIQUE constraints, and those columns typically accept NULL." },
    { q: "A column is declared NOT NULL with no default value. Which insert will be rejected?", a: ["A row that supplies no value for that column", "A row that sets that column to the number 0", "A row repeating another row's value there", "A row that stores a negative number there"], correct: "A row that supplies no value for that column", explanation: "NOT NULL only forbids missing values. Zero, negatives, and duplicates are all real values; preventing duplicates is UNIQUE's job." },
    { q: "A bank transfer debits one account and credits another inside a single transaction. If the credit fails, what should happen?", a: ["Both changes are rolled back", "Only the debit is kept", "The debit is kept and flagged", "The credit is retried forever"], correct: "Both changes are rolled back", explanation: "A transaction is all-or-nothing: if any step fails, every change in it is undone, so money is never debited without being credited." },
    { q: "In the ACID properties of database transactions, what does the 'A' stand for?", a: ["Atomicity", "Availability", "Accuracy", "Auditability"], correct: "Atomicity", explanation: "ACID is Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability. Availability is a tempting guess because it is the 'A' in the CAP theorem." },
    { q: "What is the main benefit of adding an index on a column that queries often filter by?", a: ["Faster lookups on that column", "Faster inserts into that column", "Less disk space for the table", "Automatic removal of duplicates"], correct: "Faster lookups on that column", explanation: "An index lets the database find matching rows without scanning the whole table. It actually uses extra space and slows writes slightly." },
    { q: "Which of these is a data modeling artifact that describes entities and their relationships rather than physical storage?", a: ["An entity-relationship diagram", "A B-tree index on a column", "A query execution plan", "A write-ahead log file"], correct: "An entity-relationship diagram", explanation: "An ER diagram is a conceptual/logical data model of entities and how they relate. Indexes, execution plans, and logs are physical or runtime details." },
    { q: "In the relational model, how are relationships between tables represented?", a: ["By matching key values across tables", "By pointers nested inside each record", "By edges stored in a separate graph", "By rows stored physically side by side"], correct: "By matching key values across tables", explanation: "Relational tables link through shared values, such as a foreign key matching a primary key. Embedded pointers and graph edges belong to other data models." },
    { q: "Which is a commonly cited advantage of NoSQL document stores over relational databases?", a: ["Flexible schemas and easy horizontal scaling", "Richer multi-table joins and ACID by default", "One standard query language for all vendors", "Stricter type checking on every stored field"], correct: "Flexible schemas and easy horizontal scaling", explanation: "Document stores let records vary in shape and spread across many machines. Rich joins and a shared standard language (SQL) are strengths of relational systems." },
    { q: "According to the CAP theorem, when a network partition occurs, a distributed system must trade off which two properties?", a: ["Consistency and availability", "Durability and isolation", "Atomicity and consistency", "Latency and throughput"], correct: "Consistency and availability", explanation: "During a partition, a system must either refuse some requests (keeping consistency) or answer with possibly stale data (keeping availability). Durability and isolation are ACID terms." },
    { q: "A user writes to the primary database, then immediately reads from a replica and sees the old value. What causes this?", a: ["Replication lag", "Write amplification", "A deadlock", "A phantom read"], correct: "Replication lag", explanation: "Replicas apply changes after a delay, so a read right after a write may hit stale data. A phantom read is a transaction isolation anomaly, not a replica delay." },
    { q: "In an ETL process, what happens during the Load step?", a: ["Processed data is written into the target system", "Raw data is pulled out of the operational sources", "Records are cleaned, joined, and reshaped", "Quality rules are run and failures are logged"], correct: "Processed data is written into the target system", explanation: "Extract pulls data from sources, Transform cleans and reshapes it, and Load writes the result into the destination such as a warehouse." },
    { q: "Why do idempotent steps make a failed data pipeline easier to recover?", a: ["Rerunning a step won't duplicate or corrupt data", "They stop failures from happening in the first place", "A retried step runs faster than the first attempt", "Failed steps are skipped automatically on a rerun"], correct: "Rerunning a step won't duplicate or corrupt data", explanation: "An idempotent step gives the same result whether it runs once or several times, so you can safely restart after a crash. It does not prevent failures." },
    { q: "Which of these is an example of data validation in a pipeline?", a: ["Rejecting rows where age is negative", "Encrypting rows before writing to disk", "Compressing files to cut storage costs", "Partitioning a table by order date"], correct: "Rejecting rows where age is negative", explanation: "Validation checks that data is correct and well-formed. Encryption, compression, and partitioning change how data is stored, not whether it is valid." },
    { q: "When loading a CSV, a tool samples the rows and decides that the 'price' column holds floats. What is this called?", a: ["Schema inference", "Schema evolution", "Schema enforcement", "Schema migration"], correct: "Schema inference", explanation: "Inference detects structure and types automatically from the data. Evolution and migration change an existing schema; enforcement rejects data that doesn't match one." },
    { q: "A survey about internet usage is collected only through an online form. What problem does this most directly create?", a: ["Sampling bias", "Random sampling error", "Measurement error", "Multicollinearity"], correct: "Sampling bias", explanation: "People without internet access can't respond, so the sample systematically misrepresents the population. Random sampling error shrinks with more data; this bias does not." },
    { q: "In stratified sampling, how are individuals selected?", a: ["Randomly within each predefined subgroup", "By picking whole clusters and using everyone", "By taking every k-th person from a list", "By choosing whoever is easiest to reach"], correct: "Randomly within each predefined subgroup", explanation: "Stratified sampling splits the population into strata (e.g. age bands) and samples randomly from each. Picking whole groups is cluster sampling; every k-th is systematic sampling." },
    { q: "A result is reported as 'statistically significant at α = 0.05'. What does that tell you?", a: ["Its p-value was below 0.05", "The effect is big enough to matter", "There is a 95% chance the effect is real", "The null has a 5% chance of being true"], correct: "Its p-value was below 0.05", explanation: "Significance only means the data would be unusual if the null were true (p < α). It says nothing about practical importance, and it is not the probability that the null is true." },
    { q: "What does a 95% confidence level mean for a confidence interval procedure?", a: ["About 95% of such intervals would capture the true value", "There is a 95% chance this one interval holds the true value", "95% of the sample's data points fall inside the interval", "The estimate is within 5% of the true value 95% of the time"], correct: "About 95% of such intervals would capture the true value", explanation: "The 95% describes the method's long-run success rate over repeated samples. Once computed, a specific interval either contains the true value or it doesn't." },
    { q: "Two random samples drawn from the same population produce different means. What is this natural variation called?", a: ["Sampling error", "Selection bias", "Measurement error", "Nonresponse bias"], correct: "Sampling error", explanation: "Sampling error is the chance difference between a sample statistic and the population value, which shrinks as samples grow. Biases are systematic, not random." },
    { q: "Which of these is a population parameter rather than a sample statistic?", a: ["The true mean height of all adults in Canada", "The mean height of 500 surveyed adults", "The standard deviation of a pilot sample", "The median height in a random subsample"], correct: "The true mean height of all adults in Canada", explanation: "A parameter describes the entire population and is usually unknown. Anything computed from a sample is a statistic used to estimate it." },
    { q: "In statistics, what is an estimator?", a: ["A rule that turns sample data into a guess of a parameter", "The true, fixed value of a population parameter", "The gap between a statistic and the true parameter", "An interval certain to contain the true parameter"], correct: "A rule that turns sample data into a guess of a parameter", explanation: "An estimator, like the sample mean, is a formula applied to data. The parameter is the unknown target, and the gap between them is the estimation error." },
    { q: "An estimator is called consistent if…", a: ["it converges to the true value as n grows", "its expected value equals the true value", "it has the least variance of unbiased options", "it returns the same value on every sample"], correct: "it converges to the true value as n grows", explanation: "Consistency is about large samples: the estimate homes in on the truth as n increases. Equal expected value describes unbiasedness, and least variance describes efficiency." },
    { q: "Among unbiased estimators of the same parameter, which one is called the most efficient?", a: ["The one with the smallest variance", "The one with the smallest bias", "The one that is quickest to compute", "The one needing the fewest assumptions"], correct: "The one with the smallest variance", explanation: "Efficiency means precision: the least spread around the true value. Among unbiased estimators every bias is already zero, so bias can't separate them." },
    { q: "In maximum likelihood estimation, what is the likelihood function L(θ)?", a: ["The probability of the observed data, viewed as a function of θ", "The probability that θ is the true value given the observed data", "The belief about θ held before any data has been observed", "The probability of the data averaged over all values of θ"], correct: "The probability of the observed data, viewed as a function of θ", explanation: "Likelihood is P(data | θ) with the data fixed and θ varying. The probability of θ given the data is the posterior, which also requires a prior." },
    { q: "In a Bayesian analysis, what does the prior distribution represent?", a: ["Beliefs about a parameter before seeing data", "Beliefs about a parameter after seeing data", "The spread of the observed data points", "The probability of data given a parameter"], correct: "Beliefs about a parameter before seeing data", explanation: "The prior encodes what you believe before the data arrives. Beliefs after seeing data form the posterior; P(data | parameter) is the likelihood." },
    { q: "As more and more data is collected, what typically happens to a Bayesian posterior?", a: ["The likelihood dominates and the prior matters less", "It drifts back toward the shape of the prior", "It stays identical to the prior regardless of data", "It widens steadily as uncertainty accumulates"], correct: "The likelihood dominates and the prior matters less", explanation: "With lots of data, the evidence outweighs the initial assumptions, so reasonable priors converge to similar, narrower posteriors." },
    { q: "What is the sampling distribution of the mean?", a: ["The spread of sample means across many samples", "The spread of values within a single sample", "The spread of every value in the population", "The spread of residuals from a fitted model"], correct: "The spread of sample means across many samples", explanation: "It describes how the sample mean would vary if you repeated sampling. Its standard deviation is the standard error, the basis of confidence intervals and tests." },
    { q: "A fair die is rolled thousands of times. What does the law of large numbers say about the average roll?", a: ["It gets closer and closer to 3.5", "Its distribution becomes a bell curve", "Low rolls become due after high ones", "Its variance grows with more rolls"], correct: "It gets closer and closer to 3.5", explanation: "The sample mean converges to the expected value (3.5). The bell-curve statement is the central limit theorem, and 'due' rolls is the gambler's fallacy." },
    { q: "Which statement best describes the central limit theorem (for a population with finite variance)?", a: ["Sample means are roughly normal for large n", "Raw data values become normal for large n", "Sample means equal the true mean for large n", "Sample variance shrinks to zero for large n"], correct: "Sample means are roughly normal for large n", explanation: "The CLT concerns the distribution of the sample mean, which approaches normal whatever the population's shape. The data themselves keep their original distribution." },
    { q: "In a hypothesis test, what should you conclude if the p-value is larger than α?", a: ["Fail to reject the null hypothesis", "The null hypothesis is proven true", "Reject the null hypothesis", "Accept the alternative hypothesis"], correct: "Fail to reject the null hypothesis", explanation: "A large p-value means the data aren't unusual under the null, so there's insufficient evidence against it. That is not proof the null is true." },
    { q: "A trial tests whether a new drug lowers blood pressure. What is the null hypothesis?", a: ["The drug has no effect on blood pressure", "The drug lowers blood pressure", "The drug raises blood pressure", "The drug's effect cannot be measured"], correct: "The drug has no effect on blood pressure", explanation: "The null is the default 'no effect' position that the data must provide evidence against. 'The drug lowers blood pressure' is the alternative." },
    { q: "Researchers want to detect whether a new font changes reading speed in either direction from the old mean μ₀. Which alternative hypothesis fits?", a: ["μ ≠ μ₀", "μ > μ₀", "μ < μ₀", "μ = μ₀"], correct: "μ ≠ μ₀", explanation: "'Changes in either direction' calls for a two-sided alternative. μ > μ₀ or μ < μ₀ are one-sided, and μ = μ₀ is the null hypothesis." },
    { q: "A test concludes that a perfectly fair coin is biased. What kind of mistake is this?", a: ["Type I error (false positive)", "Type II error (false negative)", "A correct rejection", "Selection bias"], correct: "Type I error (false positive)", explanation: "The null ('the coin is fair') was true but got rejected, which is a Type I error. A Type II error would be missing a coin that really is biased." },
    { q: "How can you lower a test's Type II error rate without raising α?", a: ["Increase the sample size", "Lower the significance level", "Switch to a two-sided test", "Use fewer observations"], correct: "Increase the sample size", explanation: "More data increases power (1 − Type II rate). Lowering α makes rejecting harder and so raises Type II errors." },
    { q: "What does a p-value measure?", a: ["P(result at least this extreme | null true)", "P(null hypothesis is true | observed data)", "P(alternative hypothesis is true | data)", "P(rejecting the null when the null is true)"], correct: "P(result at least this extreme | null true)", explanation: "The p-value assumes the null and asks how surprising the data are. It is not the probability the null is true; P(reject | null true) is α." },
    { q: "A test uses α = 0.05 and the null hypothesis is actually true. What is the probability of a Type I error?", a: ["0.05", "0.95", "0.025", "It depends on n"], correct: "0.05", explanation: "α is defined as the probability of rejecting a true null, so it is 0.05 regardless of sample size. 0.95 is the chance of correctly not rejecting." },
    { q: "What does logistic regression output for a binary outcome?", a: ["A probability from 0 to 1 via a sigmoid", "An unbounded value from a straight-line fit", "A cluster label assigned by distance", "A class picked by a vote of neighbors"], correct: "A probability from 0 to 1 via a sigmoid", explanation: "Logistic regression passes a linear combination through the sigmoid, giving a probability you can threshold into a class. Unbounded outputs come from linear regression." },
    { q: "Ordinary least squares (OLS) regression chooses the line that minimizes what?", a: ["The sum of squared residuals", "The sum of absolute residuals", "The largest single residual", "The count of misfit points"], correct: "The sum of squared residuals", explanation: "OLS minimizes the squared vertical distances to the line. Minimizing absolute residuals is a different method (least absolute deviations)." },
    { q: "A model predicts 70 for a point whose actual value is 64. What is the residual?", a: ["−6", "6", "36", "−0.09"], correct: "−6", explanation: "Residual = observed − predicted = 64 − 70 = −6. A negative residual means the model over-predicted; 36 is the squared residual." },
    { q: "A regression model has R² = 0.64. What does this mean?", a: ["64% of the variance in y is explained by the model", "The correlation between x and y is exactly 0.64", "64% of the model's predictions are exactly right", "The model's average prediction error is 36%"], correct: "64% of the variance in y is explained by the model", explanation: "R² is the share of outcome variance the model accounts for. In simple linear regression, R² = 0.64 corresponds to a correlation of ±0.8, not 0.64." },
    { q: "What is a common symptom of strong multicollinearity in a linear regression?", a: ["Unstable coefficients with inflated standard errors", "Residual spread that grows with the fitted values", "Residuals correlated across consecutive time points", "Near-zero training error but a large test error"], correct: "Unstable coefficients with inflated standard errors", explanation: "When predictors are highly correlated, the model can't separate their effects, so coefficients swing and standard errors balloon. Growing residual spread is heteroscedasticity." },
    { q: "In reinforcement learning, what does an agent learn from?", a: ["Rewards received after taking actions", "Labeled input–output pairs given upfront", "Patterns in unlabeled data alone", "Rules hand-coded by a domain expert"], correct: "Rewards received after taking actions", explanation: "An RL agent interacts with an environment and adjusts its policy to maximize reward. Labeled pairs define supervised learning; unlabeled patterns define unsupervised learning." },
    { q: "Why should the test set be kept untouched until final evaluation?", a: ["To get an unbiased estimate of performance on new data", "To give the model extra data for learning its weights", "To speed up training by using fewer total examples", "To balance the class proportions in the training data"], correct: "To get an unbiased estimate of performance on new data", explanation: "If test data influences training or tuning, the score becomes optimistic. Keeping it separate simulates how the model will do on unseen data." },
    { q: "What typically happens if the learning rate in gradient descent is set far too high?", a: ["The loss bounces around or diverges", "The loss falls slowly but steadily", "The model memorizes the training set", "The gradients become exactly zero"], correct: "The loss bounces around or diverges", explanation: "Huge steps overshoot the minimum, so the loss oscillates or explodes. A learning rate that is too small is what causes slow, steady progress." },
    { q: "In neural network training, what is one epoch?", a: ["One full pass over the training set", "One parameter update on one training batch", "One forward pass of a single example", "One round of hyperparameter tuning"], correct: "One full pass over the training set", explanation: "An epoch means every training example has been used once. A single update on one batch is called an iteration or step." },
    { q: "Min-max normalization rescales a feature to the range [0, 1]. If the feature ranges from 10 to 60, what does the value 30 become?", a: ["0.4", "0.5", "0.6", "0.2"], correct: "0.4", explanation: "(30 − 10) ÷ (60 − 10) = 20 ÷ 50 = 0.4. Forgetting to subtract the minimum gives 30 ÷ 60 = 0.5." },
    { q: "Which best describes a data pipeline?", a: ["Automated steps that move data from source to target", "A central store that keeps all historical data", "A dashboard that charts data in near real time", "A schema listing each table's columns and types"], correct: "Automated steps that move data from source to target", explanation: "A pipeline extracts, transforms, and delivers data in an automated, repeatable flow. A central historical store is a warehouse, which a pipeline may feed." },
    { q: "Compared with a transactional (OLTP) database, a data warehouse is optimized for what?", a: ["Large analytical queries over history", "Many small, fast inserts and updates", "Cheap storage of raw files of any type", "Low-latency reads behind a web app"], correct: "Large analytical queries over history", explanation: "Warehouses (OLAP) are built for aggregating large volumes of historical data. Frequent small writes are the OLTP workload; cheap raw storage describes a data lake." },
    { q: "An INNER JOIN of customers and orders on customer_id returns what?", a: ["Only customers that have a matching order", "All customers, with NULLs where none match", "Every customer paired with every order", "All rows from both tables, matched or not"], correct: "Only customers that have a matching order", explanation: "An inner join keeps only rows with matches on both sides. Keeping all customers is a LEFT JOIN, every pairing is a CROSS JOIN, and all rows is a FULL OUTER JOIN." },
    { q: "Which property must the values in a primary key column have?", a: ["Values that are unique and not NULL", "Values that auto-increment as integers", "Values referencing another table's key", "Values that may repeat but not be NULL"], correct: "Values that are unique and not NULL", explanation: "A primary key must uniquely identify each row, so it can't repeat or be NULL. Auto-increment is common but optional; referencing another table describes a foreign key." },
    { q: "orders.customer_id is a foreign key to customers.id. What does the database prevent by default?", a: ["An order pointing to a nonexistent customer", "Two orders sharing the same customer_id", "A customer who has no orders at all", "A NULL customer_id in the orders table"], correct: "An order pointing to a nonexistent customer", explanation: "A foreign key enforces referential integrity: every non-NULL value must exist in the referenced table. Repeats are fine, and NULLs are allowed unless the column is also NOT NULL." },
    { q: "What is the typical cost of adding many indexes to a heavily written table?", a: ["Slower inserts and updates", "Slower SELECT queries", "Loss of referential integrity", "Duplicate rows become allowed"], correct: "Slower inserts and updates", explanation: "Every index must be updated on each write, so writes slow down and storage grows. Reads generally get faster, not slower." },
    { q: "In data systems, what does latency measure?", a: ["The delay before a response arrives", "The amount of data moved per second", "The total data a system can store", "The share of requests that fail"], correct: "The delay before a response arrives", explanation: "Latency is time delay, usually in milliseconds. Data moved per second is throughput, a different measure that is often confused with it." },
    { q: "What is a key reason to monitor a machine learning model after deployment?", a: ["To catch data drift and falling performance", "To retrain the model on every single request", "To replace the need for a held-out test set", "To lower the model's inference latency"], correct: "To catch data drift and falling performance", explanation: "Real-world data changes over time, so a model that tested well can quietly degrade. Monitoring detects that so you can investigate or retrain." },
    { q: "In version control systems like Git, what is a commit?", a: ["A saved snapshot of changes with a message", "A separate line of parallel development", "A copy of the repository on a server", "A request to merge changes after review"], correct: "A saved snapshot of changes with a message", explanation: "A commit records the state of tracked files at a point in history. A separate line of work is a branch, and a review request is a pull request." },
    { q: "In Git, why create a new branch for a feature?", a: ["To work in isolation without altering main", "To back up the repository to another server", "To roll the project back to an older commit", "To combine two lines of work into one"], correct: "To work in isolation without altering main", explanation: "A branch is a separate development path, so experiments don't disturb the main line until merged. Combining lines of work is what merging does." },
    { q: "In a model that predicts house prices, which of these is a feature?", a: ["Number of bedrooms", "Sale price", "Mean squared error", "Learned coefficient"], correct: "Number of bedrooms", explanation: "Features are input variables used to make predictions. The sale price is the target, MSE is the loss, and coefficients are learned parameters." },
    { q: "In a spam classifier trained on emails, what is the label?", a: ["Whether each email is spam or not", "The words that appear in each email", "The length of each email in characters", "The domain name of each sender"], correct: "Whether each email is spam or not", explanation: "The label is the target the model learns to predict. Words, length, and sender domain are features used as inputs." },
    { q: "In machine learning, what is a 'model'?", a: ["A function learned from data mapping inputs to outputs", "The raw dataset used to train the learning algorithm", "The optimization method, such as gradient descent", "The loss value measured on the held-out test set"], correct: "A function learned from data mapping inputs to outputs", explanation: "Training an algorithm on data produces a model, which then makes predictions. Gradient descent is the training procedure, not the model itself." },
    { q: "You roll two fair six-sided dice. What is the probability that the sum is exactly 7?", a: ["1/6", "1/12", "7/36", "5/36"], correct: "1/6", explanation: "Six of the 36 equally likely outcomes sum to 7 (1+6, 2+5, ... 6+1), so 6/36 = 1/6. 5/36 is the chance of a sum of exactly 6 (or, separately, exactly 8)." },
    { q: "You roll a fair die 4 times. Approximately what is the probability of getting at least one six?", a: ["≈ 0.52", "≈ 0.67", "≈ 0.48", "≈ 0.39"], correct: "≈ 0.52", explanation: "P(no six) = (5/6)⁴ ≈ 0.48, so P(at least one) ≈ 1 − 0.48 = 0.52. Adding 4 × 1/6 = 0.67 wrongly double-counts overlapping outcomes." },
    { q: "In the classic birthday problem (365 equally likely birthdays), what is the smallest group size for which the chance of at least two people sharing a birthday exceeds 50%?", a: ["23", "183", "57", "50"], correct: "23", explanation: "With 23 people there are 253 possible pairs, enough to push the probability to about 50.7%. 183 is half of 365, a common but wrong intuition; 57 people gives about 99%." },
    { q: "A raffle sells 1,000 tickets at 2 credits each, and exactly one ticket wins a 500-credit prize. What is the expected net value of buying one ticket?", a: ["−1.50 credits", "+0.50 credits", "−2.00 credits", "−0.50 credits"], correct: "−1.50 credits", explanation: "Expected winnings are 500 × 1/1000 = 0.50, minus the 2-credit cost, giving −1.50. +0.50 forgets the ticket price; −2.00 ignores the prize." },
    { q: "You draw two cards from a standard 52-card deck without replacement. What is the probability that both are aces?", a: ["1/221", "1/169", "1/26", "1/338"], correct: "1/221", explanation: "(4/52) × (3/51) = 12/2652 = 1/221. 1/169 is the answer with replacement, where the second draw is still 4/52." },
    { q: "A family has two children, and you are told at least one of them is a boy. Assuming boys and girls are equally likely and independent, what is the probability that both are boys?", a: ["1/3", "1/2", "1/4", "2/3"], correct: "1/3", explanation: "The equally likely cases BB, BG, GB, GG reduce to BB, BG, GB once GG is ruled out, so P(BB) = 1/3. 1/2 would be the answer if you were told the older child is a boy." },
    { q: "A disease affects 1% of people. A test detects 90% of true cases but also flags 9% of healthy people. If someone tests positive, roughly what is the chance they have the disease?", a: ["About 9%", "About 90%", "About 91%", "About 50%"], correct: "About 9%", explanation: "Per 1,000 people: 9 true positives versus about 89 false positives (9% of 990), so 9/98 ≈ 9%. Answering 90% confuses P(positive | disease) with P(disease | positive) and ignores the low base rate." },
    { q: "In the Monty Hall problem, you pick one of three doors, and the host (who knows where the car is) opens a different door showing a goat. What is your chance of winning if you switch?", a: ["2/3", "1/2", "1/3", "3/4"], correct: "2/3", explanation: "Your first pick is right only 1/3 of the time; the remaining 2/3 is concentrated on the other unopened door. 1/2 is the common mistake of treating the two remaining doors as equally likely." },
    { q: "A fair coin is flipped 3 times. What is the probability of getting exactly 2 heads?", a: ["3/8", "1/4", "1/8", "1/2"], correct: "3/8", explanation: "There are 3 sequences with exactly two heads (HHT, HTH, THH) out of 8 equally likely outcomes. 1/8 counts just one specific sequence." },
    { q: "A fair coin has just landed heads five times in a row. What is the probability that the next flip is heads?", a: ["Still exactly 1/2", "About 1/64", "About 1/32", "Below 1/2; tails is due"], correct: "Still exactly 1/2", explanation: "Flips of a fair coin are independent, so past results do not change the next one. Believing tails is 'due' is the gambler's fallacy; 1/64 is the probability of six heads in a row decided in advance." },
    { q: "If P(A) = 0.5, P(B) = 0.4, and P(A and B) = 0.2, what is P(A or B)?", a: ["0.7", "0.9", "0.5", "0.2"], correct: "0.7", explanation: "P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B) = 0.5 + 0.4 − 0.2 = 0.7. 0.9 forgets to subtract the overlap, which would be counted twice." },
    { q: "You keep rolling a fair die until you get a six. On average, how many rolls will that take (including the roll that shows the six)?", a: ["6", "3.5", "3", "5"], correct: "6", explanation: "The number of trials to first success is geometric with mean 1/p = 1/(1/6) = 6. 3.5 is the average face value of a single roll, a different quantity." },
    { q: "A study reports p = 0.03 for its main comparison. Which interpretation is correct?", a: ["If the null were true, data this extreme would occur about 3% of the time", "There is a 3% probability that the null hypothesis is true given these data", "There is a 97% probability that the alternative hypothesis is true", "There is a 3% probability that the result will fail to replicate"], correct: "If the null were true, data this extreme would occur about 3% of the time", explanation: "A p-value is computed assuming the null is true; it is P(data at least this extreme | null), not P(null | data). Reading it as the probability the null is true is the most common misinterpretation." },
    { q: "A researcher runs 20 independent tests at α = 0.05, and in reality every null hypothesis is true. How many 'significant' results should they expect on average?", a: ["1", "0", "0.05", "5"], correct: "1", explanation: "Each test has a 5% false-positive rate, so the expected count is 20 × 0.05 = 1. Zero would only be expected if there were no Type I error risk at all." },
    { q: "Running 20 independent tests at α = 0.05 when all nulls are true, roughly what is the probability of getting at least one false positive?", a: ["≈ 0.64", "≈ 0.05", "≈ 1.00", "≈ 0.36"], correct: "≈ 0.64", explanation: "P(no false positives) = 0.95²⁰ ≈ 0.36, so P(at least one) ≈ 0.64. Multiplying 20 × 0.05 = 1.00 gives the expected count, not a probability." },
    { q: "You run 10 hypothesis tests and want the family-wise error rate held at 0.05 using the Bonferroni correction. What per-test significance threshold should you use?", a: ["0.005", "0.05", "0.5", "0.0005"], correct: "0.005", explanation: "Bonferroni divides α by the number of tests: 0.05 / 10 = 0.005. Keeping 0.05 per test lets the chance of at least one false positive climb to about 40%." },
    { q: "The null hypothesis is 'the new drug has no effect.' The drug actually works, but the trial fails to reject the null. What kind of error is this?", a: ["Type II error", "Type I error", "A false discovery", "A sampling frame error"], correct: "Type II error", explanation: "Failing to reject a false null is a Type II error (a false negative). A Type I error, or false discovery, would be declaring an effect that isn't really there." },
    { q: "Holding everything else fixed, which change increases a test's statistical power?", a: ["Increasing the sample size", "Lowering α from 0.05 to 0.01", "Studying a smaller true effect", "Adding more measurement noise"], correct: "Increasing the sample size", explanation: "A larger sample shrinks the standard error, making a real effect easier to detect. A stricter α, a smaller effect, or noisier measurements all reduce power." },
    { q: "A trial is designed to have 80% power for the effect size it assumes. If that effect is real, what is the probability of a Type II error?", a: ["20%", "5%", "80%", "95%"], correct: "20%", explanation: "Power = 1 − β, where β is the Type II error rate, so β = 1 − 0.80 = 20%. 5% is the usual Type I error rate α, a separate quantity." },
    { q: "In an A/B test with tens of millions of users, a conversion lift far too small to matter to the business yields p < 0.001. What does this mainly illustrate?", a: ["Significance does not imply practical importance", "The effect must be large to reach p < 0.001", "The result is almost certainly a Type I error", "The test was underpowered for this effect"], correct: "Significance does not imply practical importance", explanation: "With huge samples, even trivially small differences become statistically significant. The p-value says the effect is probably not zero, not that it matters; the test was overpowered, not underpowered." },
    { q: "A well-run study finds p = 0.40 for the difference between two groups. What is the most appropriate conclusion?", a: ["The data give little evidence against the null", "The study has proven the two groups are equal", "There is a 40% chance the groups truly differ", "The null hypothesis has a 60% chance of being false"], correct: "The data give little evidence against the null", explanation: "A large p-value means the data are compatible with the null, but absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. A p-value is never the probability that a hypothesis is true or false." },
    { q: "A two-sided test gives p = 0.08, and the observed effect is in the direction predicted in advance. What would the corresponding one-sided p-value be (for a symmetric test statistic)?", a: ["0.04", "0.16", "0.08", "0.92"], correct: "0.04", explanation: "A two-sided p-value counts both tails; when the effect is in the predicted direction, the one-sided p-value is half of it, 0.04. 0.92 would be the one-sided p-value had the effect gone the opposite way." },
    { q: "With the sample size fixed, what happens if you lower the significance level α from 0.05 to 0.01?", a: ["Type I errors fall and Type II errors rise", "Type I and Type II errors both fall", "Type I errors rise and Type II errors fall", "Type I errors fall and power increases"], correct: "Type I errors fall and Type II errors rise", explanation: "A stricter threshold makes false positives rarer but also makes real effects harder to detect, so β rises and power falls. Only more data (or less noise) can reduce both errors at once." },
    { q: "A team measures 15 different outcomes, finds one with p = 0.04, and publishes only that one as their headline finding. What is this practice called?", a: ["P-hacking (data dredging)", "Bonferroni correction", "Pre-registration", "Cross-validation"], correct: "P-hacking (data dredging)", explanation: "Searching many analyses and reporting the one that crosses p < 0.05 inflates false positives. Pre-registration and multiplicity corrections like Bonferroni are the remedies, not the problem." },
    { q: "A study reports a 95% confidence interval for a population mean of 12 to 18. Under the standard frequentist interpretation, what does the 95% refer to?", a: ["95% of intervals built this way would contain the true mean", "There is a 95% probability the true mean is between 12 and 18", "95% of individual observations fall between 12 and 18", "95% of future sample means will fall between 12 and 18"], correct: "95% of intervals built this way would contain the true mean", explanation: "The 95% describes the long-run success rate of the procedure across repeated samples. Once computed, a specific interval either contains the fixed true mean or not; it says nothing about 95% of individual data points." },
    { q: "Holding the confidence level fixed, how does the width of a confidence interval for a mean change if you quadruple the sample size?", a: ["It is cut in half", "It becomes a quarter", "It shrinks by about 30%", "It stays about the same"], correct: "It is cut in half", explanation: "Width is proportional to 1/√n, and √4 = 2, so the width halves. A quarter would require width to scale with 1/n; a 30% drop is what doubling n gives." },
    { q: "A sample mean is 50 with a standard error of 2. What is the approximate 95% confidence interval for the population mean?", a: ["About 46.1 to 53.9", "About 48.0 to 52.0", "About 44.0 to 56.0", "About 49.0 to 51.0"], correct: "About 46.1 to 53.9", explanation: "A 95% interval is roughly the mean ± 1.96 × SE = 50 ± 3.92. 48 to 52 uses only ±1 SE, which gives about 68% coverage." },
    { q: "Using the same data, how does a 99% confidence interval compare with a 95% confidence interval for the same parameter?", a: ["It is wider", "It is narrower", "Same width, new center", "It is narrower for large n"], correct: "It is wider", explanation: "Higher confidence requires a larger critical value (about 2.58 instead of 1.96), so the interval must be wider. Being more certain of capturing the parameter costs precision." },
    { q: "The 95% confidence interval for the difference between two group means is −1.2 to 3.4. What does this imply about a two-sided test of 'no difference' at α = 0.05?", a: ["It would not be significant", "It would be significant", "The true difference is exactly 0", "The groups must have equal variance"], correct: "It would not be significant", explanation: "Because the interval includes 0, zero difference is a plausible value, matching p > 0.05. That does not show the true difference is exactly zero." },
    { q: "Two groups' separate 95% confidence intervals overlap slightly. What can you conclude about a test of their difference at the 5% level?", a: ["The difference may still be significant", "The difference cannot be significant", "The difference is significant only if n > 30", "The test result must be exactly p = 0.05"], correct: "The difference may still be significant", explanation: "Comparing overlap of two separate intervals is a conservative check; two 95% CIs can overlap modestly while the difference test still gives p < 0.05. Only non-overlap reliably implies significance." },
    { q: "A poll of 1,000 randomly selected voters finds 50% support for a policy. What is the approximate 95% margin of error?", a: ["±3 percentage points", "±1 percentage point", "±10 percentage points", "±0.1 percentage points"], correct: "±3 percentage points", explanation: "Margin ≈ 1.96 × √(0.5 × 0.5 / 1000) ≈ 0.031, about 3 points. A ±1-point margin would need roughly 10,000 respondents." },
    { q: "Adult heights in a population are approximately normal with mean 170 cm and standard deviation 10 cm. About what percentage of adults are between 150 and 190 cm?", a: ["About 95%", "About 68%", "About 99.7%", "About 90%"], correct: "About 95%", explanation: "150 to 190 is the mean ± 2 SD, which covers about 95% under the 68-95-99.7 rule. 68% is the coverage of ±1 SD (160 to 180)." },
    { q: "For a normally distributed variable, roughly what percentage of values lie more than one standard deviation ABOVE the mean?", a: ["About 16%", "About 32%", "About 34%", "About 5%"], correct: "About 16%", explanation: "About 68% lie within ±1 SD, leaving 32% in the two tails combined, or about 16% in the upper tail. 34% is the share between the mean and +1 SD." },
    { q: "A fair coin is flipped 100 times. What is the standard deviation of the number of heads?", a: ["5", "25", "50", "10"], correct: "5", explanation: "For a binomial, variance = np(1 − p) = 100 × 0.5 × 0.5 = 25, so the SD is √25 = 5. 25 is the variance and 50 is the mean." },
    { q: "Calls arrive at a help desk as a Poisson process averaging 3 per hour. Approximately what is the probability of receiving zero calls in a given hour?", a: ["≈ 0.05", "≈ 0.33", "≈ 0.22", "0, since 3 are expected"], correct: "≈ 0.05", explanation: "P(0) = e^(−λ) = e^(−3) ≈ 0.0498. 0.22 is the probability of exactly 3 calls (tied with exactly 2 as the most likely count), but other counts still occur." },
    { q: "A count variable follows a Poisson distribution with mean 4. What is its standard deviation?", a: ["2", "4", "16", "1"], correct: "2", explanation: "For a Poisson distribution the variance equals the mean, so variance = 4 and SD = √4 = 2. 16 comes from wrongly squaring the mean." },
    { q: "Bulb lifetimes are exponentially distributed with a mean of 1,000 hours. A bulb has already lasted 500 hours. What is its expected remaining lifetime?", a: ["1,000 hours", "500 hours", "1,500 hours", "750 hours"], correct: "1,000 hours", explanation: "The exponential distribution is memoryless: a surviving bulb is 'as good as new,' so its expected remaining life is still the full mean of 1,000 hours. 500 hours wrongly subtracts the time already used." },
    { q: "When testing a mean, when is a t-test preferred over a z-test?", a: ["When σ is unknown and estimated by s", "When σ is known and the sample is small", "When the outcome is a categorical count", "When comparing more than two group means"], correct: "When σ is unknown and estimated by s", explanation: "The t-distribution accounts for the extra uncertainty from estimating the standard deviation from the sample. Comparing several group means calls for ANOVA, and categorical counts for chi-square tests." },
    { q: "As the degrees of freedom increase, which distribution does Student's t-distribution approach?", a: ["Standard normal", "Chi-square", "Exponential", "Poisson"], correct: "Standard normal", explanation: "The t-distribution has heavier tails than the normal, but they thin out as df grows; by about 30+ df it is very close to the standard normal. Chi-square is related to t but is not its limit." },
    { q: "You roll a die 600 times and want to test whether all six faces are equally likely. Which test is the standard choice?", a: ["Chi-square goodness of fit", "Paired-samples t-test", "One-way ANOVA F-test", "Pearson correlation test"], correct: "Chi-square goodness of fit", explanation: "A goodness-of-fit test compares observed category counts with expected counts (100 per face here). t-tests and ANOVA compare means of numeric outcomes, not category frequencies." },
    { q: "If events occur according to a Poisson process, which distribution describes the waiting time between consecutive events?", a: ["Exponential", "Poisson", "Binomial", "Uniform"], correct: "Exponential", explanation: "The Poisson distribution describes the count of events in a fixed interval; the continuous time between events is exponential. They are two views of the same process." },
    { q: "A landing page converts 20% of visitors independently. Out of 50 visitors, what is the expected number of conversions?", a: ["10", "8", "5", "20"], correct: "10", explanation: "For a binomial, the mean is np = 50 × 0.20 = 10. 8 is the variance, np(1 − p) = 50 × 0.2 × 0.8." },
    { q: "Independent trials each succeed with probability 0.5. What is the probability that the FIRST success occurs on the third trial?", a: ["1/8", "3/8", "1/4", "1/2"], correct: "1/8", explanation: "You need failure, failure, success: 0.5 × 0.5 × 0.5 = 1/8 (a geometric probability). 3/8 is the binomial chance of exactly one success in three trials in any position." },
    { q: "A population has standard deviation 20. What is the standard error of the mean for a random sample of size 100?", a: ["2", "0.2", "20", "200"], correct: "2", explanation: "SE = σ / √n = 20 / √100 = 20 / 10 = 2. Dividing by n instead of √n gives the incorrect 0.2." },
    { q: "For a normal distribution, roughly what percentage of values lie more than 3 standard deviations from the mean (in either direction)?", a: ["About 0.3%", "About 3%", "About 0.03%", "About 1%"], correct: "About 0.3%", explanation: "About 99.7% lie within ±3 SD, so roughly 0.3% fall outside. That is why values beyond 3 SD are often flagged as potential outliers." },
    { q: "In WWII, analysts studied bullet holes on returning bombers and suggested armoring the areas with the FEWEST holes. Which bias were they correcting for?", a: ["Survivorship bias", "Nonresponse bias", "Confirmation bias", "Recall bias"], correct: "Survivorship bias", explanation: "Only planes that survived were observed; planes hit in the unmarked areas mostly didn't return. Reinforcing where returning planes had holes would have fallen for survivorship bias." },
    { q: "A 1930s election poll drew its sample from telephone directories and car registrations, over-representing wealthier voters. What kind of bias is this?", a: ["Selection bias", "Recall bias", "Observer bias", "Survivorship bias"], correct: "Selection bias", explanation: "The sampling frame systematically excluded poorer voters, so the sample was not representative of the electorate. This is the classic Literary Digest example of selection (frame) bias." },
    { q: "A company emails a satisfaction survey to 10,000 customers; only 8% reply, and unhappy customers are far more likely to respond. What is the main threat to the results?", a: ["Nonresponse bias", "Survivorship bias", "Regression to the mean", "Simpson's paradox"], correct: "Nonresponse bias", explanation: "When the people who respond differ systematically from those who don't, the results misrepresent the population. A low response rate makes this worse but is not itself the bias." },
    { q: "In a classic kidney-stone study, treatment A had a higher success rate than B for small stones and for large stones, yet B had the higher overall rate. What best explains this?", a: ["A was used mostly on hard, large-stone cases", "B was tested on far more patients in total", "A's subgroup rates were computed incorrectly", "B was used mostly on hard, large-stone cases"], correct: "A was used mostly on hard, large-stone cases", explanation: "This is Simpson's paradox: stone size confounds the comparison. A was given mostly to difficult cases, dragging down its pooled rate even though it wins within each subgroup." },
    { q: "Flight instructors noticed that cadets praised for an excellent landing usually did worse next time, while those scolded for a poor landing usually improved. What most likely explains this pattern?", a: ["Regression to the mean", "Praise lowering motivation", "Survivorship bias", "The Hawthorne effect"], correct: "Regression to the mean", explanation: "Performance includes random variation, so extreme results tend to be followed by more average ones regardless of feedback. Kahneman used this example to show how regression gets mistaken for a causal effect." },
    { q: "Speed cameras are installed at the sites that had the most crashes last year, and crashes there fall the next year. What is the best way to estimate the cameras' true effect?", a: ["Compare with similar high-crash sites without cameras", "Compare these sites' crash counts before and after", "Compare these sites with the lowest-crash sites in the city", "Compare camera sites' speeds with the national average"], correct: "Compare with similar high-crash sites without cameras", explanation: "Sites picked for an unusually bad year would tend to improve anyway (regression to the mean). A comparison group selected the same way separates that natural drop from any camera effect." },
    { q: "A news website asks visitors to click to vote on whether a new law is fair, and 50,000 people respond. Why is this result unreliable as a measure of public opinion?", a: ["Voluntary response bias", "Too small a sample size", "Regression to the mean", "Measurement error in clicks"], correct: "Voluntary response bias", explanation: "People choose themselves into the sample, and those with strong opinions are much more likely to participate. A huge self-selected sample is still biased; size doesn't fix it." },
    { q: "A researcher randomly selects 20 schools from a district and then surveys every student in those chosen schools. What sampling method is this?", a: ["Cluster sampling", "Stratified sampling", "Systematic sampling", "Quota sampling"], correct: "Cluster sampling", explanation: "Cluster sampling randomly selects whole groups and includes everyone in them. Stratified sampling would instead draw a random sample from within every school." },
    { q: "Among hospitalized patients, two unrelated diseases appear negatively correlated, because having either one is enough to get admitted. What is this phenomenon called?", a: ["Berkson's paradox", "Simpson's paradox", "The ecological fallacy", "Regression to the mean"], correct: "Berkson's paradox", explanation: "Selecting on a variable affected by both diseases (hospital admission) creates a spurious association between them. Simpson's paradox is about a trend reversing when groups are combined, which is different." },
    { q: "Wealthier states tend to vote for Party X, so an analyst concludes that wealthy individuals tend to vote for Party X. What reasoning error risks being made?", a: ["The ecological fallacy", "The gambler's fallacy", "The base-rate fallacy", "The prosecutor's fallacy"], correct: "The ecological fallacy", explanation: "Patterns in group-level aggregates need not hold for individuals within those groups. In some US elections, richer states leaned one way while richer individuals leaned the other." },
    { q: "In a simple linear regression, the correlation between X and Y is r = 0.6. What proportion of the variance in Y is explained by X?", a: ["36%", "60%", "6%", "77%"], correct: "36%", explanation: "In simple linear regression, R² = r² = 0.6² = 0.36. 77% is √0.6, a mistake of taking the root instead of squaring." },
    { q: "A scatterplot shows a perfect U-shaped relationship (y = x², with x symmetric around 0), yet Pearson's r is about 0. What does this show?", a: ["r measures only linear association", "r is invalid when x includes negatives", "The two variables are independent", "r must be computed on ranked data"], correct: "r measures only linear association", explanation: "Pearson's r captures straight-line relationships; a symmetric curve can give r ≈ 0 even though y is completely determined by x. Zero correlation does not imply independence." },
    { q: "A study finds that people who use walking canes are in worse health, and concludes that canes harm health. What is the most obvious flaw?", a: ["Reverse causation", "Survivorship bias", "Regression to the mean", "Nonresponse bias"], correct: "Reverse causation", explanation: "Poor health leads people to use canes, not the other way around. Correlation alone cannot tell you the direction of a causal relationship." },
    { q: "Why does random assignment in a controlled experiment allow causal conclusions that observational studies usually cannot support?", a: ["It balances confounders, measured or not, on average", "It guarantees the sample matches the population", "It removes all random error from the outcome", "It ensures the treatment effect is statistically significant"], correct: "It balances confounders, measured or not, on average", explanation: "Randomization makes groups comparable in expectation on every pre-existing factor, so outcome differences can be attributed to treatment. It doesn't make the sample representative of a population; that is random sampling." },
    { q: "In causal analysis, what tends to happen if you control for a collider, a variable caused by both X and Y?", a: ["It creates a spurious X–Y association", "It removes confounding between X and Y", "It has no effect on the X–Y estimate", "It reduces variance without adding bias"], correct: "It creates a spurious X–Y association", explanation: "Conditioning on a common effect opens a non-causal path between its causes, inducing an association even if X and Y are unrelated. Adjusting for a confounder (a common cause) is what removes bias." },
    { q: "You compute the correlation between height and weight, then convert heights from centimeters to inches. What happens to r?", a: ["It stays the same", "It is divided by 2.54", "It is multiplied by 2.54", "It flips to negative"], correct: "It stays the same", explanation: "Pearson's r is unitless and unchanged by positive linear rescaling of either variable. Regression slopes change with units, but correlation does not." },
    { q: "A bag holds one fair coin and one two-headed coin. You pick one at random, flip it once, and get heads. What is the probability you picked the two-headed coin?", a: ["2/3", "1/2", "3/4", "1"], correct: "2/3", explanation: "P(heads) = 0.5 × 1 + 0.5 × 0.5 = 0.75, so P(two-headed | heads) = 0.5 / 0.75 = 2/3. 1/2 ignores that heads is more likely under the two-headed coin." },
    { q: "In a city, 85% of taxis are Green and 15% are Blue. A witness who is right 80% of the time says a cab in an accident was Blue. Roughly what is the probability it really was Blue?", a: ["About 41%", "About 80%", "About 15%", "About 12%"], correct: "About 41%", explanation: "True 'Blue' reports: 0.15 × 0.8 = 0.12; false ones: 0.85 × 0.2 = 0.17; so 0.12 / 0.29 ≈ 41%. Answering 80% ignores the low base rate of Blue cabs." },
    { q: "In Bayesian inference with a reasonable (non-zero) prior, what generally happens to the prior's influence on the posterior as you collect much more data?", a: ["It shrinks as the likelihood dominates", "It grows as the evidence accumulates", "It stays exactly constant at any n", "It flips to favor the opposite belief"], correct: "It shrinks as the likelihood dominates", explanation: "With lots of data, the likelihood becomes sharply peaked and overwhelms the prior, so different reasonable priors lead to similar posteriors. A prior of exactly zero is the exception, since no data can revive it." },
    { q: "Your prior odds that a hypothesis is true are 1:4. You then see evidence that is 8 times more likely if the hypothesis is true than if false. What is your posterior probability?", a: ["2/3", "8/9", "1/2", "1/3"], correct: "2/3", explanation: "Posterior odds = prior odds × likelihood ratio = (1/4) × 8 = 2, i.e. 2:1, which is a probability of 2/3. 8/9 is what you'd get starting from even 1:1 odds." },
    { q: "20% of emails are spam. The word 'free' appears in 40% of spam emails and 5% of non-spam emails. Given an email contains 'free', what is the probability it is spam?", a: ["About 67%", "About 89%", "About 40%", "About 20%"], correct: "About 67%", explanation: "P(free and spam) = 0.2 × 0.4 = 0.08; P(free and not spam) = 0.8 × 0.05 = 0.04; so 0.08 / 0.12 ≈ 67%. 89% (0.40 / 0.45) ignores the 20% base rate of spam." },
    { q: "Household incomes in a country are strongly right-skewed. How do the mean and median typically compare?", a: ["Mean is above the median", "Median is above the mean", "They are about equal", "Mean is half the median"], correct: "Mean is above the median", explanation: "A long right tail of very high incomes pulls the mean upward, while the median stays at the middle value. In a left-skewed distribution the mean would usually be below the median." },
    { q: "A dataset has Q1 = 20 and Q3 = 50. Using the common 1.5 × IQR rule, above what value would a point be flagged as an outlier?", a: ["95", "80", "140", "65"], correct: "95", explanation: "IQR = 50 − 20 = 30, and the upper fence is Q3 + 1.5 × IQR = 50 + 45 = 95. 80 adds only one IQR; 140 uses the 3 × IQR 'extreme outlier' fence." },
    { q: "Exam scores have mean 70 and standard deviation 10. What is the z-score of a student who scored 85?", a: ["1.5", "15", "1.2", "0.15"], correct: "1.5", explanation: "z = (85 − 70) / 10 = 1.5, meaning 1.5 standard deviations above the mean. 15 forgets to divide by the SD; 1.2 is 85/70." },
    { q: "A dataset has standard deviation 4. Every value is multiplied by 3, then 5 is added. What is the new standard deviation?", a: ["12", "17", "36", "4"], correct: "12", explanation: "Multiplying by 3 scales the SD by 3 (to 12); adding a constant shifts values without changing spread. 17 wrongly adds the 5; 36 scales by 9, which is how the variance changes." },
    { q: "A small company's salaries are mostly 40k–60k, but the founder earns 5 million. Which summary is least affected by the founder's salary?", a: ["Median", "Mean", "Range", "Standard deviation"], correct: "Median", explanation: "The median depends only on the middle value(s), so one extreme value barely moves it. The mean, range, and SD all change dramatically with a single huge outlier." },
    { q: "What is the population standard deviation of the values 2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9 (mean 5)?", a: ["2", "4", "2.14", "5"], correct: "2", explanation: "Squared deviations sum to 9+1+1+1+0+0+4+16 = 32; 32/8 = 4 is the variance, so SD = 2. 4 is the variance, and 2.14 is the sample SD using n − 1 = 7." },
    { q: "An analyst checks an A/B test every day and stops it the first time p < 0.05 appears. What is the main problem with this practice?", a: ["It inflates the false positive rate", "It lowers the test's statistical power", "It biases how users get randomized", "It turns the test into a one-sided test"], correct: "It inflates the false positive rate", explanation: "Each peek is another chance for random noise to cross the threshold, so the real false positive rate ends up far above 5%. Sequential testing methods are designed to allow peeking safely." },
    { q: "Holding significance level and power fixed, roughly how many users does an A/B test need to detect an effect half as large as originally planned?", a: ["Twice as many", "Four times as many", "About 1.4 times as many", "Eight times as many"], correct: "Four times as many", explanation: "Required sample size scales with 1/(effect size)², so halving the effect multiplies n by 2² = 4, not 2." },
    { q: "A redesigned homepage shows a large lift in clicks during week 1 of a test, but the lift shrinks steadily by week 4. What is the most likely explanation?", a: ["Sample ratio mismatch", "Simpson's paradox", "A novelty effect", "Survivorship bias"], correct: "A novelty effect", explanation: "Users often engage more with something simply because it is new, and the effect fades as it becomes familiar. Running tests long enough and checking effects over time helps detect this." },
    { q: "An experiment aims to increase ad clicks, but the team also monitors page load time and unsubscribe rate to make sure they do not get worse. What are those monitored metrics called?", a: ["Primary metrics", "Guardrail metrics", "North-star metrics", "Surrogate metrics"], correct: "Guardrail metrics", explanation: "Guardrail metrics protect against harmful side effects; the test should not ship if they degrade. Primary metrics are the ones the change is meant to move." },
    { q: "A chat feature is tested by randomizing individual users, but treated users frequently message users in the control group. Which assumption is most at risk?", a: ["Normality of the outcome metric", "No interference between units (SUTVA)", "Equal variance in both groups", "Independence of the covariates"], correct: "No interference between units (SUTVA)", explanation: "When treatment spills over to control users, outcomes depend on others' assignments, violating SUTVA. Cluster-level randomization (for example by community or region) is a common fix." },
    { q: "A test was configured for a 50/50 split, but the final counts are 50,000 users in control and 47,000 in treatment. What should the analyst do first?", a: ["Reweight treatment users to restore balance", "Proceed, since the split is within 5%", "Investigate assignment or logging bugs", "Extend the test until the groups even out"], correct: "Investigate assignment or logging bugs", explanation: "This is a sample ratio mismatch: a chi-square test gives a p-value far below 0.001, so the gap is not chance. SRM usually signals a bug that can bias results, so reweighting would hide the problem rather than fix it." },
    { q: "Users are randomized, but click-through rate is analyzed per page view, treating every page view as an independent observation. What typically happens?", a: ["Variance is overestimated, so p-values are too large", "The lift estimate is biased upward", "Variance is underestimated, so p-values are too small", "Power drops because there are fewer units"], correct: "Variance is underestimated, so p-values are too small", explanation: "Page views from the same user are correlated, so treating them as independent understates variance and produces too many false positives. The delta method or a user-level bootstrap gives correct standard errors." },
    { q: "An A/B test compares 20 independent metrics at α = 0.05, and the change truly affects none of them. How many metrics would you expect to show a 'significant' result?", a: ["About 0", "About 1", "About 2", "About 5"], correct: "About 1", explanation: "Expected false positives = 20 × 0.05 = 1. This is why corrections such as Bonferroni or Benjamini-Hochberg are used when many metrics are tested." },
    { q: "What does the CUPED technique use to make A/B test results more precise?", a: ["Sequential boundaries that allow early stopping", "Bandit allocation that shifts traffic to winners", "Pre-experiment data as a covariate to cut variance", "Bootstrap resampling to correct for skewed metrics"], correct: "Pre-experiment data as a covariate to cut variance", explanation: "CUPED (Controlled-experiment Using Pre-Experiment Data) adjusts the metric using each user's pre-period behaviour, removing predictable variance and shrinking confidence intervals without adding bias." },
    { q: "Before launching real experiments, a team runs an 'A/A test' in which both groups get the identical experience. What is the main purpose?", a: ["Measuring the novelty effect of a change", "Checking the setup produces no false effects", "Comparing two treatments without a control", "Estimating the minimum detectable effect"], correct: "Checking the setup produces no false effects", explanation: "With identical experiences, any 'significant' difference beyond the expected 5% rate points to bugs in randomization, logging, or the statistics pipeline." },
    { q: "A test is designed with 80% power for a specific effect size. If the true effect is exactly that size and the test is run 10 independent times, about how many runs would you expect to be statistically significant?", a: ["About 2", "About 5", "About 8", "About 10"], correct: "About 8", explanation: "Power is the probability of detecting a true effect of the planned size, so 80% power means about 8 of 10 runs reach significance; about 2 would be false negatives." },
    { q: "A model is fitted as ln(y) = 2 + 0.05·x. Roughly what does a one-unit increase in x correspond to?", a: ["A 0.05-unit increase in y", "About a 5% increase in y", "A 5-unit increase in y", "About a 0.05% increase in y"], correct: "About a 5% increase in y", explanation: "In a log-level model, a small coefficient β means a one-unit change in x multiplies y by e^β ≈ 1 + β, so 0.05 is about a 5% change (exactly e^0.05 ≈ 1.051)." },
    { q: "A fitted model is ln(sales) = 3 + 0.8·ln(ad spend). If ad spend rises by 10%, roughly how much do predicted sales change?", a: ["About 0.8% higher", "About 8% higher", "About 80% higher", "About 10% higher"], correct: "About 8% higher", explanation: "In a log-log model the coefficient is an elasticity: a 1% change in x gives about a 0.8% change in y, so 10% gives about 8% (1.1^0.8 ≈ 1.079)." },
    { q: "You add another predictor, even pure random noise, to an ordinary least squares model. What happens to R² on the training data?", a: ["It never decreases", "It always decreases", "It can move either way", "It stays exactly the same"], correct: "It never decreases", explanation: "OLS can always set the new coefficient to zero, so the fit cannot get worse; R² stays the same or rises. Adjusted R² is the version that can move either way, because it penalizes extra predictors." },
    { q: "Two predictors in a linear regression are correlated at r = 0.95. What is the most typical consequence?", a: ["Biased predictions from the overall model", "Inflated standard errors on their coefficients", "A lower R² for the full model", "Non-normal distribution of the residuals"], correct: "Inflated standard errors on their coefficients", explanation: "Multicollinearity makes it hard to separate the two effects, so their coefficients become unstable with large standard errors. Overall predictions and R² are usually fine." },
    { q: "A predictor has a variance inflation factor (VIF) of 10. What R² does it get when regressed on the other predictors?", a: ["0.10", "0.50", "0.90", "0.99"], correct: "0.90", explanation: "VIF = 1 / (1 − R²), so 10 = 1 / (1 − R²) gives R² = 0.90. A VIF of 100 would correspond to 0.99." },
    { q: "A residuals-versus-fitted plot shows residuals spreading out in a widening funnel as fitted values grow. What does this indicate?", a: ["Multicollinearity", "Heteroscedasticity", "Autocorrelation", "Endogeneity"], correct: "Heteroscedasticity", explanation: "A funnel means the error variance changes with the fitted value. A log transform of y or robust (heteroscedasticity-consistent) standard errors are common remedies." },
    { q: "A model is y = 2 + 3x + 4z + 1.5·x·z, where z is a 0/1 indicator. What is the slope of y with respect to x when z = 1?", a: ["3", "4.5", "7.5", "8.5"], correct: "4.5", explanation: "The slope of x is 3 + 1.5·z, which equals 3 + 1.5 = 4.5 when z = 1. The 4z term shifts the intercept, not the slope, so adding it (8.5) is a common mistake." },
    { q: "A residuals-versus-fitted plot shows a clear U-shaped curve. What is the most likely problem with the model?", a: ["A missing nonlinear term", "Error variance that changes", "Too many predictors included", "High-leverage outliers"], correct: "A missing nonlinear term", explanation: "A systematic curve means the model misses a nonlinear pattern; adding a squared term or transforming a variable usually helps. Changing variance shows up as a funnel, not a curve." },
    { q: "A house-price regression includes square footage and number of bedrooms, and the bedroom coefficient is −5,000. What does this coefficient most directly describe?", a: ["The price change per bedroom across all houses", "Evidence that bedrooms lower home values overall", "The price change per bedroom at fixed square footage", "The average price gap for one-bedroom homes"], correct: "The price change per bedroom at fixed square footage", explanation: "Coefficients are interpreted holding other predictors constant: at the same size, more bedrooms means smaller rooms, which can lower price. It does not mean bedrooms reduce value in general." },
    { q: "On 1 million rows, a regression has R² = 0.02 but its slope has p < 0.001. Which interpretation is best?", a: ["The p-value must be miscalculated given an R² that low", "The effect is likely real but explains little variation", "The slope is large but estimated with poor precision", "The model is badly overfitting its training data"], correct: "The effect is likely real but explains little variation", explanation: "With huge samples even tiny effects become statistically significant. A low R² simply means most variation in y comes from other factors; the two numbers answer different questions." },
    { q: "A categorical variable with 4 levels is added to a linear regression that has an intercept. How many dummy (indicator) columns should be used?", a: ["2", "3", "4", "5"], correct: "3", explanation: "One level becomes the baseline absorbed by the intercept. Using all 4 dummies makes them sum to the intercept column, creating perfect multicollinearity (the dummy variable trap)." },
    { q: "A classifier produces 90 true positives, 10 false positives, and 30 false negatives. What is its recall?", a: ["0.69", "0.75", "0.90", "0.25"], correct: "0.75", explanation: "Recall = TP / (TP + FN) = 90 / 120 = 0.75. The tempting 0.90 is precision, TP / (TP + FP)." },
    { q: "A spam filter has 40 true positives, 10 false positives, and 20 false negatives. What is its precision?", a: ["0.67", "0.57", "0.80", "0.20"], correct: "0.80", explanation: "Precision = TP / (TP + FP) = 40 / 50 = 0.80. The 0.67 option is recall, 40 / 60." },
    { q: "In a dataset where 1% of transactions are fraud, a model predicts 'not fraud' for every case. What are its accuracy and recall for fraud?", a: ["99% accuracy, 0% recall", "99% accuracy, 1% recall", "1% accuracy, 0% recall", "99% accuracy, 99% recall"], correct: "99% accuracy, 0% recall", explanation: "It is right on the 99% legitimate cases, so accuracy is 99%, but it catches none of the fraud, so recall is 0%. This is the accuracy paradox on imbalanced data." },
    { q: "A probabilistic classifier's decision threshold is raised from 0.5 to 0.8. What typically happens?", a: ["Precision falls and recall rises", "Precision rises and recall falls", "Both precision and recall rise", "Both precision and recall fall"], correct: "Precision rises and recall falls", explanation: "A stricter threshold flags fewer cases: the ones flagged are more likely correct (higher precision), but more true positives are missed (lower recall)." },
    { q: "A model assigns a probability of 0.8 to 1,000 cases, and 600 of them turn out to be positive. What does this suggest about those predictions?", a: ["They are underconfident", "They are well calibrated", "They are overconfident", "They have a low AUC"], correct: "They are overconfident", explanation: "Calibrated 0.8 predictions should be right about 80% of the time; only 60% were, so the model's probabilities are too high. Calibration says nothing direct about ranking quality (AUC)." },
    { q: "A team standardizes all features using the mean and standard deviation of the full dataset, then splits it into train and test sets. What is the issue?", a: ["Features lose their interpretability", "Test-set statistics leak into training", "The model is pushed toward underfitting", "Class balance shifts between the splits"], correct: "Test-set statistics leak into training", explanation: "Preprocessing must be fit on training data only. Using full-dataset statistics lets information from the test set influence training, making test scores optimistic." },
    { q: "A churn model's strongest feature is 'number of support calls in the 30 days after the prediction date'. What is wrong with it?", a: ["It is too correlated to be causal", "It needs scaling before use", "It uses data from the future", "It is collinear with tenure"], correct: "It uses data from the future", explanation: "This is temporal leakage: the feature would not be available when the prediction is actually made, so the model looks great offline and fails in production." },
    { q: "Why is shuffled k-fold cross-validation a poor choice for evaluating a daily sales forecasting model?", a: ["It requires the series to be stationary", "It lets the model train on future data", "It ignores seasonality in the target", "Its folds are too small for daily data"], correct: "It lets the model train on future data", explanation: "Shuffling mixes time periods, so the model learns from days after those it is tested on, giving overly optimistic error estimates." },
    { q: "Which validation scheme is most appropriate for a time-series forecasting model?", a: ["Stratified k-fold validation", "Walk-forward validation", "Leave-one-out validation", "Shuffled bootstrap"], correct: "Walk-forward validation", explanation: "Walk-forward (rolling-origin) validation trains on past data and tests on the next period, repeatedly moving forward, which mimics how the model is used in production." },
    { q: "A confusion matrix shows TP = 80, FN = 20, FP = 30, TN = 870. What is the false positive rate?", a: ["About 3.0%", "About 3.3%", "About 20%", "About 27%"], correct: "About 3.3%", explanation: "FPR = FP / (FP + TN) = 30 / 900 ≈ 3.3%. 27% is FP / (TP + FP), the false discovery rate, and 20% is the false negative rate." },
    { q: "Table A has ids 1, 2, 3. Table B has ids 1, 1, 4. How many rows does A LEFT JOIN B ON A.id = B.id return?", a: ["3", "4", "5", "2"], correct: "4", explanation: "id 1 matches two B rows (2 rows), and ids 2 and 3 have no match but are kept with NULLs (1 row each): 2 + 1 + 1 = 4." },
    { q: "A customers table has 3 rows and an orders table has 5 rows. Every order belongs to exactly one existing customer, and one customer has no orders. How many rows does customers LEFT JOIN orders return?", a: ["5", "6", "3", "8"], correct: "6", explanation: "The 5 orders each produce one joined row, and the customer with no orders is kept once with NULL order columns: 5 + 1 = 6." },
    { q: "Table A has ids 1, 2, 3. Table B has ids 1, 1, 4. How many rows does a FULL OUTER JOIN on id return?", a: ["4", "5", "6", "3"], correct: "5", explanation: "id 1 matches twice (2 rows), A's unmatched ids 2 and 3 add 2 rows, and B's unmatched id 4 adds 1 row: 2 + 2 + 1 = 5." },
    { q: "In standard SQL, what does SELECT * FROM t WHERE col = NULL return?", a: ["Only the rows where col is NULL", "No rows, since the test is UNKNOWN", "Every row in the table t", "Only rows where col is not NULL"], correct: "No rows, since the test is UNKNOWN", explanation: "Any comparison with NULL yields UNKNOWN, and WHERE keeps only TRUE rows. Use WHERE col IS NULL instead." },
    { q: "A table has 10 rows, and its email column is NULL in 3 of them. What do COUNT(*) and COUNT(email) return?", a: ["10 and 7", "10 and 10", "7 and 7", "7 and 10"], correct: "10 and 7", explanation: "COUNT(*) counts rows regardless of values, while COUNT(column) skips NULLs." },
    { q: "A column holds the values 10, 20, NULL, 30. What does AVG on that column return in SQL?", a: ["15", "NULL", "20", "25"], correct: "20", explanation: "Aggregate functions ignore NULLs, so AVG = (10 + 20 + 30) / 3 = 20. Treating NULL as zero would give 60 / 4 = 15." },
    { q: "In SQL, which clause keeps only the departments whose AVG(salary) exceeds 100,000 after grouping?", a: ["WHERE", "HAVING", "QUALIFY", "FILTER"], correct: "HAVING", explanation: "HAVING filters groups after aggregation. WHERE filters individual rows before grouping and cannot reference aggregates, and QUALIFY filters window-function results." },
    { q: "Scores sorted in descending order are 90, 85, 85, 80. What do RANK() and DENSE_RANK() give the score of 80?", a: ["3 and 3", "4 and 3", "4 and 4", "3 and 4"], correct: "4 and 3", explanation: "RANK leaves a gap after ties (1, 2, 2, 4), while DENSE_RANK does not (1, 2, 2, 3)." },
    { q: "Which SQL window function assigns 1, 2, 3, 4 to four rows even when two of them tie on the ORDER BY value?", a: ["RANK()", "DENSE_RANK()", "ROW_NUMBER()", "NTILE(4)"], correct: "ROW_NUMBER()", explanation: "ROW_NUMBER gives every row a unique sequential number, breaking ties arbitrarily. RANK and DENSE_RANK give tied rows the same number." },
    { q: "In pandas, what happens by default to rows with NaN in the 'city' column when running df.groupby('city')['sales'].mean()?", a: ["They form their own NaN group", "They are left out of the result", "They raise a KeyError", "They are grouped under 'None'"], correct: "They are left out of the result", explanation: "groupby uses dropna=True by default, silently excluding NaN keys. Pass dropna=False to keep them as a group." },
    { q: "The left DataFrame has keys A, B, C, D (one row each). The right DataFrame has keys A, A, A, E. How many rows does pd.merge(left, right, on='key', how='left') return?", a: ["4", "6", "7", "3"], correct: "6", explanation: "Key A matches 3 right rows (3 rows), and B, C, D are kept once each with NaN: 3 + 3 = 6. Duplicate keys silently multiply rows, a common merge gotcha." },
    { q: "Average sales in a treated city go from 50 to 70 after a policy; in a comparable control city they go from 40 to 50. What is the difference-in-differences estimate?", a: ["10", "20", "30", "15"], correct: "10", explanation: "DiD = (70 − 50) − (50 − 40) = 20 − 10 = 10. The treated city's raw change of 20 includes the 10-unit trend that also happened without the policy." },
    { q: "Which assumption is essential for a difference-in-differences estimate to be valid?", a: ["Exclusion restriction", "Parallel trends", "Common support", "Random assignment"], correct: "Parallel trends", explanation: "DiD assumes that, without treatment, the treated and control groups would have followed the same trend over time. Random assignment is not required, which is why DiD is used with observational data." },
    { q: "In instrumental variables analysis, what is the requirement that the instrument affects the outcome only through the treatment called?", a: ["Parallel trends", "Positivity", "Exclusion restriction", "Ignorability"], correct: "Exclusion restriction", explanation: "The exclusion restriction says the instrument has no direct path to the outcome. The instrument must also be relevant, meaning strongly correlated with the treatment." },
    { q: "By a widely used rule of thumb, an instrument is considered weak if its first-stage F-statistic is below what value?", a: ["1.96", "3.84", "10", "30"], correct: "10", explanation: "Staiger and Stock's rule of thumb flags first-stage F below 10 as weak. Weak instruments give biased, unreliable IV estimates. 1.96 and 3.84 are critical values for z and chi-square tests." },
    { q: "Why are randomized controlled trials considered the gold standard for estimating causal effects?", a: ["Large samples remove measurement error", "Randomization balances unmeasured confounders", "Blinding removes the need for a control group", "Matching pairs units on observed covariates"], correct: "Randomization balances unmeasured confounders", explanation: "Random assignment makes groups comparable on average in both measured and unmeasured characteristics, so outcome differences can be attributed to the treatment." },
    { q: "What does a propensity score estimate for each unit?", a: ["P(outcome | treatment)", "P(treatment | covariates)", "P(covariates | treatment)", "P(outcome | covariates)"], correct: "P(treatment | covariates)", explanation: "The propensity score is the probability of receiving treatment given observed covariates. Units with similar scores can then be matched or weighted to compare like with like." },
    { q: "Which problem can propensity score matching NOT fix?", a: ["Imbalance in observed covariates", "Confounders that were never measured", "Differing treated and control group sizes", "Observed traits that predict treatment"], correct: "Confounders that were never measured", explanation: "Propensity scores are built only from measured covariates, so they cannot balance hidden confounders. That is the key advantage randomization has over matching." },
    { q: "In inverse probability weighting, a treated unit has a propensity score of 0.2. What weight does it receive?", a: ["0.2", "0.8", "1.25", "5"], correct: "5", explanation: "Treated units get weight 1 / p = 1 / 0.2 = 5, so rarely-treated types count more. 1.25 = 1 / (1 − 0.2) is the weight a control unit with that score would get." },
    { q: "Among hospitalized patients, two diseases that are unrelated in the general population appear negatively correlated. What is the most likely explanation?", a: ["Simpson's paradox (aggregation)", "Regression to the mean effect", "Collider bias (Berkson's paradox)", "Confounding by indication"], correct: "Collider bias (Berkson's paradox)", explanation: "Hospitalization is caused by either disease, so selecting only hospitalized patients conditions on a common effect (a collider), which creates a spurious negative association." },
    { q: "A scholarship is awarded to students who score 80 or higher. Comparing outcomes for students just above and just below 80 to estimate its effect is an example of which method?", a: ["Regression discontinuity", "Difference-in-differences", "Instrumental variables", "Synthetic control"], correct: "Regression discontinuity", explanation: "Regression discontinuity exploits a cutoff: students just either side of 80 are nearly identical, so the jump in outcomes at the threshold estimates the causal effect." },
    { q: "Which chart is usually best for showing how a single metric changed month by month over two years?", a: ["Pie chart", "Histogram", "Line chart", "Treemap"], correct: "Line chart", explanation: "Line charts show trends over ordered time well. A histogram shows the distribution of values but loses their time order." },
    { q: "A bar chart compares values of 96 and 98 on a y-axis that starts at 95. How many times taller does the 98 bar appear?", a: ["About 1.02 times", "1.5 times", "2 times", "3 times"], correct: "3 times", explanation: "The visible bar heights are 96 − 95 = 1 and 98 − 95 = 3, so a 2% difference looks like a 3× difference. Bar charts should start at zero." },
    { q: "Why should bar chart axes usually start at zero, while line chart axes often need not?", a: ["Bars encode value by their length", "Bars can only display proportions", "Line charts need a log-scaled axis", "Line slopes ignore the axis range"], correct: "Bars encode value by their length", explanation: "Readers compare bar lengths, so a truncated axis distorts ratios. Line charts emphasize change and position, so zooming in is often acceptable if clearly labeled." },
    { q: "Which chart best compares the distribution of salaries across five departments?", a: ["A single pie chart", "Side-by-side box plots", "A stacked area chart", "A line chart by department"], correct: "Side-by-side box plots", explanation: "Box plots show median, spread, and outliers for each group side by side. A pie chart shows shares of a total, not distributions." },
    { q: "A scatter plot of 500,000 points has become a solid blob from overplotting. Which alternative best reveals where points concentrate?", a: ["A pie chart of both variables", "A hexbin or 2D density plot", "A stacked bar of both variables", "A dual-axis line chart"], correct: "A hexbin or 2D density plot", explanation: "Hexbin and density plots aggregate points into bins and shade by count, showing density that overlapping markers hide. Transparency (alpha) is another common fix." },
    { q: "Why are dual-axis charts (two y-axes with different scales) often criticized?", a: ["They cannot show more than two series", "They require both series in the same units", "Axis scaling can create a fake relationship", "They hide the individual data points"], correct: "Axis scaling can create a fake relationship", explanation: "Each axis range is chosen freely, so the two lines can be made to cross, overlap, or track each other at will, suggesting correlations that the data do not support." },
    { q: "According to Cleveland and McGill's graphical perception research, which visual encoding do people judge most accurately?", a: ["Angles of the pie slices", "Area of the circles", "Position on a common scale", "Saturation of colors"], correct: "Position on a common scale", explanation: "People compare positions along a shared axis (as in dot plots and bar charts) more accurately than angles, areas, or color intensity, which is one reason bar charts often beat pie charts." },
    { q: "A choropleth map shades US states by the raw number of flu cases. What is the main problem?", a: ["It cannot show state borders", "It mostly mirrors population size", "It needs a diverging color palette", "It hides states with zero cases"], correct: "It mostly mirrors population size", explanation: "Populous states will have the most cases almost regardless of risk. Rates (for example cases per 100,000 people) make the map meaningful." },
    { q: "What is the main lesson of Anscombe's quartet, published by Francis Anscombe in 1973?", a: ["Scatter plots overstate weak correlations", "Linear regression fails on small samples", "Matching summary stats can hide different patterns", "Correlation is unaffected by single outliers"], correct: "Matching summary stats can hide different patterns", explanation: "Its four datasets share nearly identical means, variances, correlations, and regression lines, yet look completely different when plotted. Always visualize your data." },
    { q: "Why is the rainbow ('jet') colormap discouraged for continuous data?", a: ["It has too few distinct hues to read", "Its lightness is not perceptually uniform", "It cannot be printed reliably in color", "It only suits diverging data ranges"], correct: "Its lightness is not perceptually uniform", explanation: "Rainbow maps create false boundaries and hide real gradients because brightness jumps unevenly between hues. Perceptually uniform maps like viridis avoid this." },
    { q: "Florence Nightingale is famous in statistics for popularizing which type of chart to show causes of soldier deaths in the Crimean War?", a: ["Box-and-whisker plot", "Polar area (rose) diagram", "Scatter plot matrix", "Stacked bar chart"], correct: "Polar area (rose) diagram", explanation: "Her polar area diagrams (also called coxcombs) showed that preventable disease killed far more soldiers than battle wounds, helping drive sanitary reform." },
    { q: "Who is credited with inventing the line graph and the bar chart, publishing them in 1786?", a: ["Charles Minard", "Florence Nightingale", "John Snow", "William Playfair"], correct: "William Playfair", explanation: "Scottish engineer William Playfair introduced line and bar charts in his 1786 Commercial and Political Atlas, and the pie chart in 1801." },
    { q: "Who mapped deaths in London's 1854 cholera outbreak and traced them to the Broad Street water pump?", a: ["William Farr", "John Snow", "Joseph Lister", "Edwin Chadwick"], correct: "John Snow", explanation: "Physician John Snow's map showed deaths clustered around the pump, supporting the idea that cholera spreads through contaminated water rather than 'bad air'." },
    { q: "William Sealy Gosset published the t-distribution in 1908 under the pen name 'Student' while working for which employer?", a: ["Carlsberg brewery", "Guinness brewery", "Rothamsted Station", "Bell Laboratories"], correct: "Guinness brewery", explanation: "Gosset worked on small-sample quality control at Guinness in Dublin, and the company's policy led him to publish under a pseudonym." },
    { q: "Which statistician introduced the famous Iris flower dataset in a 1936 paper on discriminant analysis?", a: ["Karl Pearson", "Francis Galton", "R. A. Fisher", "Jerzy Neyman"], correct: "R. A. Fisher", explanation: "Fisher used measurements of three Iris species (collected by Edgar Anderson) to demonstrate linear discriminant analysis, and the dataset is still a classic teaching example." },
    { q: "Who coined the term 'regression' after observing that children of very tall parents tend to be less extreme in height?", a: ["Francis Galton", "Karl Pearson", "Carl Friedrich Gauss", "Adrien-Marie Legendre"], correct: "Francis Galton", explanation: "Galton described 'regression towards mediocrity' in heights in the 1880s. His protégé Karl Pearson later developed much of the mathematics of correlation." },
    { q: "Who first published the method of least squares, in 1805?", a: ["Carl Friedrich Gauss", "Adrien-Marie Legendre", "Pierre-Simon Laplace", "Francis Galton"], correct: "Adrien-Marie Legendre", explanation: "Legendre published least squares in 1805. Gauss published his version in 1809 and claimed he had used it earlier, leading to a famous priority dispute." },
    { q: "Which statistician popularized the box plot and wrote the 1977 book Exploratory Data Analysis?", a: ["Edward Tufte", "William Cleveland", "John Tukey", "George Box"], correct: "John Tukey", explanation: "Tukey championed exploring data visually before formal testing. He is also credited with coining the word 'bit'. George Box, despite the name, did not invent the box plot." },
    { q: "The 'lady tasting tea' experiment, used to illustrate randomization and the null hypothesis, appears in which statistician's 1935 book The Design of Experiments?", a: ["Jerzy Neyman", "Egon Pearson", "W. S. Gosset", "R. A. Fisher"], correct: "R. A. Fisher", explanation: "Fisher described testing a lady's claim that she could tell whether milk or tea was poured first, using randomized cups to compute the chance of guessing correctly." },
    { q: "Thomas Bayes's essay containing his famous theorem was published after his death, in 1763. Who edited and submitted it?", a: ["Pierre-Simon Laplace", "Abraham de Moivre", "Richard Price", "Thomas Simpson"], correct: "Richard Price", explanation: "Bayes's friend Richard Price edited the essay and presented it to the Royal Society. Laplace independently developed and generalized the idea later." },
    { q: "The Netflix Prize (2006–2009) offered $1 million for improving the RMSE of Netflix's recommendation algorithm by at least what percentage?", a: ["1%", "5%", "10%", "20%"], correct: "10%", explanation: "The goal was a 10% improvement over Netflix's Cinematch system; the team BellKor's Pragmatic Chaos reached it and won in 2009." }
];
